// Research library. Sources checked 7 Nov 2026. Not legal advice.
export default [{"id":"F-AI","name":"AI Act","scope":"EU","summary":"Check the system's purpose and the operator's role first, including existing prohibited uses. Disclosure does not make a prohibited use lawful. Additional qualified intimate-image and child-abuse-material prohibitions start on 2 December 2026. July 2026 changed literacy duties. Most transparency duties already apply, with a limited marking transition. Annex III high-risk duties start on 2 December 2027 and the product-linked phase on 2 August 2028. Sensitive-data bias testing has strict separate safeguards.","claims":[{"id":"F-AI-01","q":"Can a non-EU small website be covered?","p":"EU market placement or putting into service can bring a provider into scope wherever it is based. EU-based deployers are covered. Third-country providers and deployers are also covered where their AI output is used in the EU. Small size alone is not an exemption.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Art. 2(1)(a)-(c), Art. 3(1)"}],"x":["No AI system or relevant model activity","No applicable EU territorial link"],"unk":["Whether incidental overseas output use meets the territorial test in the particular facts."]},{"id":"F-AI-02","q":"Is the operator a provider or a deployer?","p":"A provider develops or commissions an AI system or general-purpose AI model and supplies it under its own name, including free supply. A deployer uses an AI system under its authority for professional purposes. Integrating a third-party model into an own-brand service can make the operator a system provider. It does not automatically make it the provider of the underlying model.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Art. 3(3), (4), (68)"}],"x":["Pure personal non-professional use is excluded from the deployer definition"],"unk":["API wrappers and embedded vendor widgets require a system-boundary assessment."]},{"id":"F-AI-03","q":"What AI literacy rule applied from February 2025?","p":"Original Article 4 required providers and deployers to take measures, to their best extent, to ensure sufficient AI literacy of relevant staff and persons acting for them. It applied from 2 February 2025. This is historical wording for the period ending on 26 July 2026.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act original Official Journal text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 4 and 113(a)"},{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, Digital Omnibus on AI","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng","l":"Art. 1(5) and Art. 4"}],"x":["Do not present the original wording as the current duty"],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-AI-04","q":"What is the current AI literacy duty?","p":"From 27 July 2026, amended Article 4 requires measures to support development of AI literacy among relevant staff and persons acting for the operator. Measures must reflect their knowledge, experience, training, use context and affected people. The operator need not guarantee a specific literacy level for each individual. The duty was simplified, not abolished.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Art. 4(1)-(3)"},{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, Digital Omnibus on AI","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng","l":"Art. 1(5), Art. 4"}],"x":["No blanket microenterprise exemption in Article 4"],"unk":["Appropriate evidence and training measures depend on use and risk."]},{"id":"F-AI-05","q":"When must a chatbot identify itself?","p":"Since 2 August 2026, Article 50(1) requires providers to design systems that interact directly with people so users know they are interacting with AI, unless that is obvious in context to a reasonably well-informed, observant and circumspect person. Article 50(5) requires clear, distinguishable and accessible information by the first interaction. A site operator must first establish whether it is the provider.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Art. 50(1), (5) and Art. 113 second paragraph"},{"t":"Commission AI Act implementation timeline","u":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act","l":"2 August 2026 milestone"}],"x":["Obvious AI interaction exception","Narrow statutory law-enforcement exception"],"unk":["Whether an interface makes AI interaction sufficiently obvious."]},{"id":"F-AI-06","q":"Who must mark generated content in machine-readable form?","p":"Article 50(2) requires providers of systems generating synthetic audio, images, video or text to make outputs machine-readable as artificial and detectable, within the provision's technical-feasibility qualifications. It normally applies from 2 August 2026. Standard assistive editing and processing that does not substantially change inputs or their meaning are excluded to the stated extent.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Art. 50(2) and Art. 113 second paragraph"}],"x":["Standard-editing and no-substantial-alteration exceptions","Narrow statutory law-enforcement exception"],"unk":["Technical compliance standards for the particular media and system."]},{"id":"F-AI-07","q":"Is all Article 50 transparency postponed until December 2026?","p":"No. Article 111(4) gives providers of synthetic-content AI systems placed on the market before 2 August 2026 until 2 December 2026 to comply with Article 50(2). This transition does not postpone chatbot identification under Article 50(1) or deployer disclosure under Article 50(4).","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Art. 111(4), Art. 50(1)-(4), Art. 113"},{"t":"Commission AI Act implementation timeline","u":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act","l":"2 December 2026 milestone"}],"x":["Systems first placed on the market on or after 2 August 2026","Other Article 50 duties"],"unk":["Evidence of first market placement for a changed system version."]},{"id":"F-AI-08","q":"When must a site disclose AI-generated publications?","p":"Since 2 August 2026, deployers must disclose AI-generated or manipulated deepfake images, audio and video. Evidently artistic, fictional or satirical works get a tailored disclosure method, not a complete exemption. AI text published to inform the public on matters of public interest must also be disclosed, unless human review or editorial control occurred and a person or entity holds editorial responsibility. This text exception does not remove the separate deepfake duty.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Art. 3(60), Art. 50(4)-(5), Art. 113"}],"x":["Not every AI-assisted product description is public-interest text","Narrow statutory law-enforcement exception"],"unk":["Whether a specific publication is on a matter of public interest."]},{"id":"F-AI-09","q":"Are emotion recognition and biometric categorisation separate triggers?","p":"Article 50(3) requires deployers of emotion-recognition or biometric-categorisation systems to inform exposed people. It applies from 2 August 2026 and does not replace data-protection rules. Some uses are prohibited under Article 5, so disclosure cannot make a prohibited use lawful.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Art. 50(3), (5)-(6), Art. 5 and Art. 113"}],"x":["Narrow statutory law-enforcement exception"],"unk":["Prohibition and special-category-data assessment for the concrete use."]},{"id":"F-AI-10","q":"What are the other main phased dates?","p":"Chapters I and II generally applied from 2 February 2025. Chapter III Section 4, Chapters V, VII and XII, and Article 78 applied from 2 August 2025, except Article 101. The general application date is 2 August 2026. Under amended Article 113(d), Articles 102-110 apply from 27 July 2026. New Article 5(1)(ba)-(bb) and 5(1a)-(1b) prohibitions apply from 2 December 2026.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Art. 113(a)-(d)"},{"t":"Commission AI Act implementation timeline","u":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act","l":"Milestones for 2025 and 2026"}],"x":["High-risk and legacy transitions must be checked separately"],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-AI-11","q":"What are the amended high-risk dates?","p":"Chapter III Sections 1-3, except Article 6(5), apply from 2 December 2027 to Annex III high-risk systems and from 2 August 2028 to Article 6(1) and Annex I high-risk systems. Article 25 provider-role changes for rebranding, substantial modification or changing intended purpose belong to this phased high-risk regime. A routine support chatbot is not high-risk merely because it uses AI.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Arts. 6, 25, 113(c), Annexes I and III"},{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, Digital Omnibus on AI","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng","l":"Art. 1, amendment replacing Art. 113(c)"}],"x":["AI use alone does not meet the high-risk classification","Article 6(3) has a limited Annex III exclusion subject to conditions"],"unk":["Classification and significant-risk analysis require use-specific review."]},{"id":"F-AI-12","q":"Do older AI systems or models have separate transitions?","p":"Providers of general-purpose AI models placed on the market before 2 August 2025 have until 2 August 2027 under Article 111(3). Article 111(2) makes the amended high-risk rules conditional on significant design changes for certain systems already on the market before their relevant Chapter III date. Public-authority high-risk uses and Annex X large-scale IT systems have separate 2030 rules. These transitions do not disapply Article 5 prohibitions.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Art. 111(1)-(3)"}],"x":["Do not apply the GPAI model transition to every downstream chatbot"],"unk":["Whether updates count as significant design changes."]},{"id":"F-AI-13","q":"Which 2025-2026 amendment is enacted?","p":"The AI Omnibus became Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, adopted on 8 July 2026 and published on 24 July 2026. Article 4 brought it into force on 27 July 2026. The current AI Act consolidation identifies this amending act. Its literacy and timing changes must be treated as law, not as an outstanding proposal.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, Digital Omnibus on AI","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng","l":"Title, OJ date, Arts. 1 and 4"},{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Consolidation header and amendment table"}],"x":["Older articles describing only a proposal or political agreement"],"unk":["No claim that this bounded check found every delegated or implementing act."]},{"id":"F-AI-14","q":"Which related proposals must remain separate?","p":"The general Digital Omnibus COM(2025) 837, procedure 2025/0360/COD, remains ongoing in the retrieved record. The medical-device proposal COM(2025) 1023, procedure 2025/0404/COD, also remains ongoing and includes an AI Act Annex I amendment. Neither record establishes an enacted change to the AI Act. They must not be conflated with Regulation (EU) 2026/1744.","k":"proposal","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Procedure 2025/0360/COD, general Digital Omnibus","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/procedure/EN/2025_360","l":"Procedure status: Ongoing, proposal title"},{"t":"Procedure 2025/0404/COD, medical-device simplification proposal","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/procedure/EN/2025_404","l":"Procedure status: Ongoing, proposal title"}],"x":["A proposal does not change current duties"],"unk":["Monitor later adoption, publication and provision-specific commencement."]},{"id":"F-AI-15","q":"Which existing prohibited practices need screening?","p":"Screen Article 5 before offering or using AI. It prohibits materially behaviour-distorting subliminal, manipulative or deceptive techniques meeting the significant-harm test, and exploitation of age, disability or social/economic vulnerabilities meeting that test. It also prohibits specified social scoring, criminal-risk prediction based solely on profiling or personality, untargeted facial-image scraping, workplace or education emotion inference subject to medical/safety exceptions, and specified sensitive biometric categorisation. Real-time public-space biometric identification for law enforcement has narrow statutory exceptions and safeguards. Each trigger and exception needs its own facts. Disclosure cannot legalise a prohibited practice.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Article 5(1)(a)-(h), (2)-(7), Article 113(a)"}],"x":["Do not treat every persuasion tool, score or biometric use as prohibited without the statutory conditions."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-AI-16","q":"Which additional prohibitions start on 2 December 2026?","p":"From 2 December 2026, Article 5(1)(ba)-(bb) adds specified realistic intimate or sexually explicit material about an identifiable person without their freely given, specific, informed, unambiguous and explicit consent, and the child-abuse material or performance defined by Directive 2011/93/EU Article 2(c),(e), subject to its national 'without right' defence. Market placement or putting into service is prohibited only where generation/manipulation is the intended purpose, or a reasonably foreseeable reproducible outcome without significant technical modification and adequate preventive/corrective safeguards are absent. Use is prohibited where the deployer uses the system for that purpose. Under paragraph 1b, manipulation that neither increases exposure of depicted intimate parts nor changes the nature of depicted sexual activities is excluded from the first limb.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, Digital Omnibus on AI","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng","l":"Article 1(7), inserting Article 5(1)(ba)-(bb), (1a)-(1b). Article 1 amendment to Article 113(a)"}],"x":[],"unk":["Applicable national defence and case-specific classification require specialist review."]},{"id":"F-AI-17","q":"Do personal, research or open-source exclusions remove every duty?","p":"Article 2 excludes specifically developed and deployed scientific-research-only systems/models, and pre-market research/testing/development, but not real-world testing under that latter exclusion. The purely personal non-professional exclusion concerns natural-person deployer obligations. Free/open-source AI systems are not excluded if high-risk or within Articles 5 or 50. These exclusions do not disapply other applicable EU law.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Article 2(6), (8), (10), (12)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-AI-18","q":"Which website uses need Annex III high-risk escalation?","p":"Recruitment or applicant filtering, education admission decisions and natural-person creditworthiness assessments are listed Annex III examples. The financial-fraud exception is specific to the creditworthiness entry. Article 6(3) can exclude an Annex III system only where it poses no significant harm risk, including by not materially influencing decisions, and satisfies a listed narrow-task, completed-human-work, pattern-review or preparatory-task condition. Profiling of natural persons prevents that exclusion. The amended Annex III Chapter III phase starts on 2 December 2027. Existing prohibitions and other laws can already apply.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Article 6(2)-(4), Annex III points 3(a), 4(a), 5(b), Article 113(c)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-AI-19","q":"Does bias testing permit unrestricted sensitive-data processing?","p":"AI Act Article 4a permits exceptional special-category processing only as strictly necessary for the specified bias detection and correction. High-risk providers use paragraph 1. Other-system/model providers and deployers and high-risk deployers use paragraph 2's health, safety, fundamental-rights or unlawful-discrimination conditions. Other or synthetic/anonymised data must be insufficient. Apply technical reuse limits, security and pseudonymisation, strict documented access and confidentiality, no access by other parties, deletion when bias is corrected or retention ends, and records explaining necessity and why alternatives fail. Applicable data-protection rules remain. Paragraph 2 creates no duty to perform bias testing.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Article 4a(1)(a)-(f), (2), Article 2(7)"},{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, Digital Omnibus on AI","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng","l":"Article 1(6), inserting Article 4a"}],"x":["Not a general training-data exemption."],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{"s":"A static merchant site uses no AI and supplies no AI model.","r":"No AI Act role identified on these facts."},{"s":"A one-person SaaS supplies its own AI chatbot using another company's model.","r":"Small size and third-party model use do not eliminate possible system-provider duties."},{"s":"A system first sold in September 2026 generates images.","r":"It cannot use the pre-August Article 50(2) transition."},{"s":"An editor checks AI-written public-interest text and accepts editorial responsibility.","r":"The text exception may apply. It does not exempt deepfake images."}],"gaps":["No national enforcement or penalty-law census completed.","No system-specific AI classification or role analysis completed.","Article 4a screening is now extracted. Case-specific data-protection assessment and the complete omnibus amendment set still need specialist review.","No final technical conformity assessment of marking methods or Article 50 codes of practice completed.","EEA incorporation and adaptations for IS, LI and NO were not checked. EU rules must not be labelled automatically effective throughout the EEA."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER","name":"Consumer contracts","scope":"EU","summary":"Classify the customer and contract, including digital content, digital services and human services delivered online. Covered distance contracts usually have a 14-day withdrawal period. A valid early-start request can leave a proportionate service charge after withdrawal. Paid and no-money exceptions differ. Check payment acknowledgement, durable confirmation, user-content retrieval, online withdrawal and the September 2026 information changes under national law.","claims":[{"id":"F-CONSUMER-01","q":"Who and which transaction does the CRD cover?","p":"Classify the customer, trader and contract before applying the CRD. A distance contract requires an organised remote-sales scheme and exclusively remote communication through conclusion.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Arts. 2(1)-(2), 2(5)-(8), 3(1)-(3)"}],"x":["Healthcare, gambling and other Art. 3 exclusions","Distance financial services use the special Art. 3(1b) regime."],"unk":["Mixed-purpose purchases and bundled offers may need legal classification."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-02","q":"Are digital content and digital services the same category?","p":"Digital content is supplied digital data. Digital services enable creation, processing, storage, access or interaction with data. Human services do not become digital services merely through online delivery.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/770","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/770/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 2(1)-(3), 3(4)-(6)"},{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/2161","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/2161/oj/eng","l":"Art. 4(1)(c)-(e)"}],"x":["Goods with integral digital elements supplied under their sales contract follow the goods regime.","Sector-specific exclusions in DCD Art. 3(5)."],"unk":["Mixed platforms and bundles need component-level review."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-03","q":"Can an offer with no monetary charge still be covered?","p":"CRD and DCD coverage can extend to digital supply where the consumer provides personal data. The exception concerns data processed only for supply or legal compliance, with no other purpose.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/2161","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/2161/oj/eng","l":"Art. 4(2)(b), inserting CRD Art. 3(1a)"},{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/770","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/770/oj/eng","l":"Art. 3(1), second subparagraph"}],"x":["No other processing purpose and only necessary supply or legal compliance"],"unk":["This does not authorise processing under the GDPR."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-04","q":"When does the ordinary withdrawal period run?","p":"The ordinary CRD withdrawal period is 14 days. It starts at contract conclusion for services and non-tangible digital content, and normally at receipt for goods. Missing withdrawal information can extend it.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Arts. 9(1)-(2), 10(1)-(2)"}],"x":["Art. 16 exceptions","National 30-day options concern specified off-premises situations, not every web purchase."],"unk":["Country rules and time calculation must be checked for an actual deadline."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-05","q":"When can withdrawal end for a paid digital service?","p":"For a paid service, Art. 16(a) requires full performance, prior express consent to start and acknowledgement of withdrawal loss on completion. Starting an ongoing subscription is insufficient.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Arts. 8(8), 16(a)"},{"t":"Commission Notice, guidance on Directive 2011/83/EU, 2021/C 525/01","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:52021XC1229(04)","l":"Sections 5.6 and 5.7, opening paragraph"}],"x":["Art. 16(m) is not the digital-service exception."],"unk":["Completion of a continuous or mixed service needs factual review."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-06","q":"When can withdrawal end for paid digital content?","p":"For non-tangible paid digital content, Art. 16(m) requires performance to begin, prior express consent, acknowledgement of withdrawal loss and the required contract confirmation.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Arts. 16(m)(i)-(iii), 7(2), 8(7), 14(4)(b)"},{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/2161","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/2161/oj/eng","l":"Art. 4(12)(a)(ii), replacing CRD Art. 16(m)"}],"x":["Ordinary digital services","A general acceptance of terms alone does not prove these distinct conditions."],"unk":["Evidence of each step is needed before asserting loss of withdrawal."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-07","q":"What must a subscription disclose?","p":"Disclose billing-period costs, monthly costs for fixed-rate subscriptions, duration, renewal termination conditions and minimum commitment. These disclosures do not themselves create an unrestricted right to cancel.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Art. 6(1)(e), (o), (p), Art. 8(2)"}],"x":[],"unk":["National renewal and termination rules require separate review."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-08","q":"Does each automatic renewal restart withdrawal?","p":"In Sofatutor, withdrawal was available once for the described free-trial subscription, provided the trader clearly informed the consumer at conclusion about the later payment obligation.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CJEU C-565/22, Sofatutor, 5 October 2023, ECLI:EU:C:2023:735","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62022CJ0565","l":"Paragraphs 45-51 and operative part"}],"x":["Do not generalise this to materially changed or newly concluded contracts."],"unk":["Insufficient price disclosure or different renewal facts may change the result."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-09","q":"What online withdrawal function applies from June 2026?","p":"National measures must apply from 19 June 2026. Covered online distance contracts need an accessible withdrawal function during the withdrawal period, a confirmation step and prompt durable acknowledgement containing the submission time.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2023/2673","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2023/2673/oj/eng","l":"Art. 1(2)-(3), inserting CRD Art. 11a(1)-(5), Art. 2(1)"},{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Art. 11a"}],"x":["The function does not create withdrawal where an exception removes it.","It is not a general subscription-cancellation right after withdrawal expires."],"unk":["National wording and enforcement must be checked."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-10","q":"Which other CRD information changes apply in September 2026?","p":"The September 2026 changes add conditional software-update and repair information, goods guarantee notices and durability labels. Available environmentally friendly delivery options also enter the disclosure list.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2024/825","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2024/825/oj/eng","l":"Art. 2(3)-(4), Art. 4(1)"},{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Art. 6(1)(g), (l)-(lc), (u)-(v), Art. 8(2)"},{"t":"Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2025/1960","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg_impl/2025/1960/oj","l":"Arts. 1-3 and Annexes I-II"}],"x":["Goods guarantee notices must not be presented as the digital-service guarantee regime."],"unk":["Product-specific conditions and the exact current design, including corrigenda, need implementation review."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-11","q":"Does losing withdrawal remove digital-contract remedies?","p":"DCD conformity and failure-to-supply remedies are separate from CRD withdrawal. Losing withdrawal does not itself remove remedies for defective digital supply.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/770","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/770/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 1, 13-14 and 22"},{"t":"Commission Notice, guidance on Directive 2011/83/EU, 2021/C 525/01","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:52021XC1229(04)","l":"Section 5.4, comparison with Digital Content Directive"}],"x":[],"unk":["This task does not fully map remedies, update duties or limitation periods."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-12","q":"Is the old EU ODR-platform duty still a current checklist item?","p":"Regulation 524/2013 was repealed from 20 July 2025 and the EU ODR platform was discontinued. Other applicable ADR information duties require their own check.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2024/3228","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32024R3228","l":"Arts. 1-2"},{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Art. 6(1)(t)"}],"x":["Do not retain the repealed platform-link duty as current EU law."],"unk":["The wider ADR regime and later amendments were not fully researched."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-13","q":"How can consumer rules reach a trader established outside the EU?","p":"Under Rome I, qualifying directed activity can preserve mandatory protections of the consumer's habitual-residence law despite a foreign-law clause.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EC) No 593/2008, Rome I","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2008/593/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 2, 6(1)-(4), recital 24"}],"x":["Art. 6(4) exceptions","Website accessibility alone does not establish directed activity."],"unk":["No global jurisdiction or enforcement conclusion follows from this claim alone."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-14","q":"Which consumer checkout and confirmation duties apply?","p":"For a paid electronic distance contract, present the Article 8(2) information clearly and prominently directly before ordering. Obtain explicit acknowledgement of the payment obligation. A payment button must carry only an easily legible 'order with obligation to pay' label or equivalent unambiguous wording. Failure to meet that acknowledgement/button rule leaves the consumer unbound. Show delivery restrictions and accepted payment methods by the start of ordering. Give durable contract confirmation within a reasonable time, no later than delivery or before service performance, including required information and applicable digital-content consent confirmation.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Article 8(2)-(3), (7)"}],"x":["A particular Member State's implementation must be checked."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-15","q":"Can withdrawal still leave a proportionate service charge?","p":"A consumer who validly requested early service performance can owe a proportionate amount for performance before notifying withdrawal. Use the agreed total price, or market value if that price is excessive. No service charge is due if the trader failed to give the Article 6(1)(h) or (j) information, or the consumer did not expressly request an early start. For paid distance services, Article 8(8) also requires acknowledgement of loss after full performance. Starting a service does not itself extinguish withdrawal.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Articles 6(1)(h),(j), 8(8), 14(3)-(4)(a), 16(a)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-16","q":"Do paid-contract conditions cover contracts without a money price?","p":"Under CRD Article 16(a), the service exception applies after full performance, while the express-consent and acknowledgement condition is tied to an obligation to pay. Under Article 16(m), non-tangible digital-content withdrawal ends when performance begins, with the further consent, acknowledgement and confirmation conditions tied to an obligation to pay. First establish CRD coverage under Article 3(1a). This EU wording is not a verified outcome under every national implementation, including Croatia.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Articles 3(1a), 16(a), 16(m)"}],"x":[],"unk":["Croatian non-tangible no-money branch remains unresolved."]},{"id":"F-CONSUMER-17","q":"What happens to user-created non-personal content after withdrawal?","p":"The trader must stop using user-provided or created non-personal content, subject to Article 13(5)'s exceptions for context-only content, activity-only content, inseparably aggregated content and jointly generated content others can continue using. On request, return the content unless the first three exceptions apply. Retrieval must be free, unhindered, within a reasonable time and in a commonly used machine-readable format. Disabling access must preserve the retrieval right.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Article 13(5)-(8)"}],"x":["Personal data remain governed by GDPR."],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["This is a bounded 2025-2026 change check, not an exhaustive consumer-law reform census.","Member State subscription renewal, cancellation, language and enforcement rules remain unreviewed outside the Croatian tasks.","Classification of mixed digital offers and data-only contracts needs qualified review before automated conclusions.","The exact harmonised guarantee artwork and any corrigenda must be checked before implementation.","DCD remedies, ongoing modification rules, goods conformity, sector-specific financial services and the wider ADR amendments need separate research.","Cross-border application requires facts and a country-specific conflict-of-law analysis."]},{"id":"F-CRA","name":"Cyber Resilience Act","scope":"EU","summary":"The CRA covers many commercially supplied software and hardware products, including required remote processing. Standalone SaaS and open-source roles need careful classification. Manufacturer reporting has applied since 11 September 2026 and includes affected-user information as well as authority reports. Act without undue delay within the outer deadlines. Most other duties, including support information and update availability, start on 11 December 2027.","claims":[{"id":"F-CRA-01","q":"What is a product with digital elements?","p":"The CRA covers software or hardware products and their remote data-processing solutions, including separately marketed components. The intended or reasonably foreseeable use must include a direct or indirect logical or physical connection to a device or network. Downloadable software can be covered. EU commercial market supply is the trigger, not the existence of a website alone.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Arts. 2(1), 3(1)-(2), 3(8)-(10), 3(21)-(22)"}],"x":["No qualifying product or market supply","Article 2 sectoral exclusions"],"unk":["Product boundary and EU market-targeting facts."]},{"id":"F-CRA-02","q":"Is standalone SaaS excluded?","p":"The Commission FAQ explains that standalone SaaS is not itself a product with digital elements. A website that does not support such a product is also outside that product definition. A service is covered as remote processing if it is developed by or under the manufacturer's responsibility and its absence would prevent a product function. A mobile app with a required backend therefore needs a separate assessment.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Commission CRA implementation FAQ v1.4, 4 September 2026","u":"https://ec.europa.eu/newsroom/dae/redirection/document/123307","l":"Section 1.2, paragraphs on websites, standalone SaaS and remote data processing"},{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Art. 3(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Cyber Resilience Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/2847","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/2847/oj/eng","l":"Recitals 11-12"}],"x":["Standalone SaaS with no qualifying product relationship"],"unk":["Hybrid apps, downloadable clients and browser-delivered products require architecture-specific analysis."]},{"id":"F-CRA-03","q":"Which sectoral and other exclusions matter?","p":"Article 2 excludes products covered by the medical-device and in-vitro diagnostic regulations and the specified vehicle-safety regulation. It also excludes specified certified civil-aviation products, marine equipment, identical-specification replacement parts and products exclusively for national security or defence or designed for classified information. These are conditional exclusions, not a general exemption for regulated industries.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Art. 2(2)-(7)"}],"x":["Excluded products can remain regulated under other law"],"unk":["Any delegated exclusion under Article 2(5) applicable to the product."]},{"id":"F-CRA-04","q":"Is free or open-source software automatically exempt?","p":"The CRA's ordinary market-supply test requires commercial activity. Recitals 15-18 explain that free price or an open-source licence does not by itself settle that test. Monetised support and other commercial arrangements can matter. Non-commercial open-source development and mere code contributions to products outside the contributor's responsibility are treated differently. Role and monetisation must be checked before saying an open-source project is exempt.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Art. 3(13)-(14), (22), (48)"},{"t":"Cyber Resilience Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/2847","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/2847/oj/eng","l":"Recitals 15-19"}],"x":["Free of charge is not sufficient for exclusion","A downstream commercial product is not exempt because it contains open source"],"unk":["Fact-specific commercial-activity and steward analysis."]},{"id":"F-CRA-05","q":"What is an open-source software steward?","p":"Article 3(14) defines a steward as a legal person, other than a manufacturer, that systematically supports the development of specified open-source products intended for commercial activities and ensures their viability. Article 24 gives stewards a tailored cybersecurity policy, cooperation and reporting regime. It is distinct from the manufacturer regime and does not automatically cover every volunteer contributor.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Art. 3(14), Art. 24(1)-(3)"},{"t":"Cyber Resilience Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/2847","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/2847/oj/eng","l":"Recitals 18-20"}],"x":["Individual contribution alone does not establish steward status"],"unk":["Foundation or corporate sponsorship arrangements."]},{"id":"F-CRA-06","q":"When do the main and early CRA provisions apply?","p":"The CRA entered into force on 10 December 2024. Article 71(2) sets general application at 11 December 2027. Article 14 manufacturer reporting applies from 11 September 2026. Chapter IV, Articles 35-51 on conformity-assessment bodies, applies from 11 June 2026. The early Chapter IV date does not make every manufacturer duty effective in June 2026.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Art. 71(1)-(2), OJ publication 20 November 2024"},{"t":"Commission CRA reporting obligations","u":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/cra-reporting","l":"Opening paragraph"}],"x":["Do not collapse entry into force and application into one date"],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-CRA-07","q":"What must manufacturers report from September 2026?","p":"Article 14 requires manufacturers to report actively exploited vulnerabilities contained in their products and severe incidents affecting product security. Reports go through the single platform to the coordinating CSIRT and ENISA. Early warning and notification must be submitted without undue delay, with outer limits of 24 hours and 72 hours respectively from awareness. Vulnerability final reports are due within 14 days after a corrective or mitigating measure becomes available. Severe-incident final reports are due within one month after notification.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Art. 3(42), Art. 14(1)-(7), Art. 71(2)"},{"t":"Commission CRA reporting obligations","u":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/cra-reporting","l":"What are the main rules?"}],"x":["Not every discovered vulnerability is actively exploited","No general small-business exemption from reporting"],"unk":["Whether a particular incident is severe and the appropriate reporting endpoint."]},{"id":"F-CRA-08","q":"Do reporting duties also reach older products?","p":"Article 69(3) makes Article 14 apply to covered products already placed on the market before 11 December 2027. The separate Article 69(2) transition generally reserves other CRA requirements for older products that undergo substantial modification from that date. Pre-2027 market placement is therefore not a blanket escape from current reporting.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Art. 69(2)-(3), Art. 71(2)"}],"x":["Do not use a product-type development date as proof that every later copy was already placed on the market"],"unk":["Product versions, individual market placement and support-period interaction."]},{"id":"F-CRA-09","q":"When do open-source steward reporting duties start?","p":"The Commission's September 2026 FAQ states that Article 24(3) steward reporting applies from 11 December 2027. Manufacturer reporting under Article 14 started on 11 September 2026. Steward duties refer to actively exploited vulnerabilities where the steward is involved in development and specified severe incidents affecting development infrastructure. A person that is actually a manufacturer cannot use the steward date just because its code is open source.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Commission CRA implementation FAQ v1.4, 4 September 2026","u":"https://ec.europa.eu/newsroom/dae/redirection/document/123307","l":"Section 5.5, added in version 1.4 on 4 September 2026"},{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Arts. 3(13)-(14), 24(3), 71(2)"},{"t":"Commission CRA reporting obligations","u":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/cra-reporting","l":"Opening paragraphs"}],"x":["Manufacturer duty is not deferred to the steward date"],"unk":["Specific role and reportable development incident."]},{"id":"F-CRA-10","q":"What main duties should an in-scope software seller prepare for?","p":"From 11 December 2027, manufacturers generally need cybersecurity risk assessment, secure product design, vulnerability handling, technical documentation, conformity assessment and the required conformity declaration and CE marking. Article 13(8) normally requires a support period of at least five years unless expected use is shorter. Article 13(5) requires due diligence for integrated third-party components, including non-commercial open source. Legacy transitions and product class affect the exact route.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Art. 13(1)-(8), (12), Arts. 28-32, Annex I, Arts. 69 and 71"}],"x":["Third-party assessment is not mandatory for every product","Article 69 legacy transition"],"unk":["Important or critical class, applicable standards and conformity procedure."]},{"id":"F-CRA-11","q":"Does small business status remove CRA duties?","p":"Small size does not exclude an otherwise covered manufacturer. Article 64(10)(a) excludes specified administrative fines for micro or small manufacturers' failure to meet the 24-hour early-warning deadlines. It does not remove those reporting duties or create general immunity. Article 64(10)(b) separately excludes these administrative fines for stewards. Article 64 belongs to the main 2027 application phase.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Arts. 2, 14(2)(a), 14(4)(a), 64(10), 71(2)"}],"x":["No blanket SME exclusion or waiver of 72-hour notification"],"unk":["National enforcement and penalty provisions."]},{"id":"F-CRA-12","q":"Were the CRA dates postponed in 2025 or 2026?","p":"The retrieved EUR-Lex current consolidation includes the 2024 and 2025 corrigenda and retains the dates in Article 71. Current Commission reporting guidance confirms September 2026 manufacturer reporting and December 2027 main application. This check found no enacted postponement of those dates. That bounded finding is not a complete census of every delegated or implementing measure.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Correction table and Art. 71"},{"t":"Commission CRA reporting obligations","u":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/cra-reporting","l":"Opening paragraphs"}],"x":["Announcements about other digital laws are not CRA postponements"],"unk":["A complete subordinate-act and product-specific exemption register has not been audited."]},{"id":"F-CRA-13","q":"Must manufacturers inform users as well as authorities?","p":"After learning of an actively exploited vulnerability or severe product-security incident, the manufacturer must inform impacted users and, where appropriate, all users. Include the vulnerability or incident and, where necessary, mitigation and corrective measures users can take. Use readily processable structured machine-readable information where appropriate. Article 14(8) belongs to the manufacturer-reporting phase applicable since 11 September 2026.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Articles 14(8), 71(2), 69(3)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-CRA-14","q":"What makes a product-security incident severe?","p":"Article 14(5) treats an incident as severe if it harms or is capable of harming the product's ability to protect sensitive or important data/functions' availability, authenticity, integrity or confidentiality. It also covers incidents that introduce or could introduce/execute malicious code in the product or the user's network/information systems. The statutory capability limbs mean realised compromise is not always required.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Article 14(5)(a)-(b)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-CRA-15","q":"Are the 24-hour and 72-hour periods waiting periods?","p":"Article 14(2) and (4) require early warnings and the following notifications without undue delay and in any event within their 24-hour and 72-hour awareness-based limits. The limits do not authorise waiting when an earlier notification is required. Subsequent final-report deadlines use different triggers.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Article 14(2)(a)-(c), (4)(a)-(c)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-CRA-16","q":"What support and update information applies in the 2027 phase?","p":"For the main phase from 11 December 2027, subject to legacy transitions, disclose the support-end month and year clearly at purchase and notify users when support ends where technically feasible. Keep each issued security update available for at least ten years from issuance or the remainder of support, whichever is longer. Keep the Annex II information and instructions available to users and market-surveillance authorities for at least ten years from market placement or the support period, whichever is longer. Online instructions must stay accessible, user-friendly and online for that period (Art. 13(18)). Internal retention alone does not meet this duty. The single contact point must permit a choice of communication and not rely solely on automated tools.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRA consolidated text with corrigenda","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R2847-20241120","l":"Article 13(9), (17)-(19), Articles 69 and 71"}],"x":[],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{"s":"An ordinary information website supplies no software product or product backend.","r":"The website itself does not trigger CRA product obligations."},{"s":"A paid downloadable desktop app connects to a network.","r":"Digital-only delivery does not exclude the product."},{"s":"A mobile app relies on the developer's cloud API for a core function.","r":"The API may be covered remote data processing despite a SaaS label."},{"s":"A business monetises an open-source product through paid support.","r":"The licence and free download do not settle commercial manufacturer status."},{"s":"A covered manufacturer released software in 2025 and learns of active exploitation in October 2026.","r":"The legacy-product transition does not by itself remove Article 14 reporting."}],"gaps":["No product-specific architecture, commercial-activity or manufacturer/steward classification.","No complete review of July 2026 CRA guidance, product standards or every delegated act.","No operational test of the Single Reporting Platform or national CSIRT endpoints.","National enforcement arrangements and EEA incorporation remain unchecked.","The CRA does not replace GDPR breach reporting or any independently applicable NIS2 duties."]},{"id":"F-DEVICE","name":"Cookies and device access","scope":"EU","summary":"Device rules cover more than cookies. Assess storage, wholly local use and external access separately. Information used only within a device can fall outside the access limb, while a write initiated by another party still needs assessment. Cookieless and IP-only analytics can be covered. If terminal origin of an IP address cannot be ruled out, apply Article 5(3)'s steps. Check national rules and any purpose-specific exemption.","claims":[{"id":"F-DEVICE-01","q":"What does Article 5(3) require?","p":"Storing information on, or accessing stored information from, a user's device generally requires informed consent. Article 5(3) provides two narrow exemptions.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2002/58","l":"Article 5(3), both sentences"}],"x":["The Directive requires national implementation. This is not a worldwide territorial ruling."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-02","q":"What are the two exemptions?","p":"An exemption can cover access solely needed to transmit a communication, or access strictly necessary to provide a service the user expressly requested.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2002/58","l":"Article 5(3), second sentence"}],"x":["Business usefulness alone does not establish either exemption."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-03","q":"Does anonymous information fall outside the rule?","p":"Planet49 confirms that the consent rule does not depend on whether the stored or accessed information is personal data.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Planet49, C-673/17","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62017CJ0673","l":"Paragraphs 68-71 and operative part point 2"},{"t":"ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2002/58","l":"Article 5(3), reference to information"}],"x":["GDPR applicability to subsequent processing remains a separate question."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-04","q":"What consent and information did Planet49 require?","p":"A pre-ticked box is not valid consent. The user must receive information about cookie duration and third-party access.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Planet49, C-673/17","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62017CJ0673","l":"Paragraphs 52-65 and 74-81, operative part points 1 and 3"}],"x":["Silence or a user's failure to untick a box does not establish consent."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-05","q":"Do the technical guidelines decide the exemption?","p":"EDPB Guidelines 2/2023 identify technical scope. They expressly leave consent exemptions to a separate assessment of the facts, national law and authority guidance.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 4, 6 and 40"}],"x":["Being within technical scope does not by itself prove consent is required for every operation."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-06","q":"Are cookies the only covered storage?","p":"EDPB treats storage as technology-neutral. Storage duration and medium do not set a minimum threshold. Storage and access can occur independently and involve different parties.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraph 6 and paragraphs 35-39"}],"x":["A session-only or memory-only implementation is not automatically outside scope."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-07","q":"How should localStorage be assessed?","p":"A website's use of localStorage to write or read information on a visitor's device should be assessed under the same storage/access test. Distinguish a write initiated by another party from wholly local use that gives no other actor access to information or derived results. Paragraph 44 qualifies access, not the separate storage operation.","k":"inference","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2002/58","l":"Article 5(3)"},{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 6, 35-39, 44 and 52-53"}],"x":["This is a technical application of the general rule, not a named localStorage exemption.","A storage API name does not establish necessity."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-08","q":"Can pixels fall within scope without cookies?","p":"EDPB identifies tracking pixels and tracked links as storage and access mechanisms. Temporary caching and instructions to return tracking identifiers can bring them within Article 5(3).","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Section 3.1, paragraphs 46-51"}],"x":["Do not classify every ordinary untracked image request as a tracking pixel."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-09","q":"What about fingerprinting?","p":"WP29 applies Article 5(3) to fingerprinting that stores or accesses device information. Its examples reject consent exemptions for fingerprinting used for first-party analytics or targeted advertising.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Opinion 9/2014 on device fingerprinting, WP224","u":"https://ec.europa.eu/justice/article-29/documentation/opinion-recommendation/files/2014/wp224_en.pdf","l":"Section 6, final paragraphs, and sections 7.1-7.2"},{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraph 2 and sections 2.5-2.6"}],"x":["No cookie file does not establish no device access."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-10","q":"Are cookieless analytics automatically exempt?","p":"Cookieless is not a legal conclusion. Identify all device access first, including client-side computation and IP-based tracking, then test any exemption.","k":"inference","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 4, 40 and 52-55"},{"t":"Opinion 04/2012 on Cookie Consent Exemption, WP194","u":"https://ec.europa.eu/justice/article-29/documentation/opinion-recommendation/files/2012/wp194_en.pdf","l":"Section 4.3, pages 10-11"}],"x":["A vendor's privacy label is not evidence about every operation.","A wholly server-side calculation on lawfully obtained data may involve no new terminal access, but collection and later processing still need assessment."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-11","q":"What is the IP-only qualification?","p":"EDPB says IP-based tracking can fall within scope when the address originates from terminal equipment. The analysis can differ for addresses generated by carrier-grade NAT. Under paragraph 55, an entity that cannot ensure that the address does not originate from terminal equipment must take the steps required by Article 5(3). Whether consent is required still depends on the exemptions.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 54-56 and footnotes 28-29"}],"x":["Do not assert that every IP address always comes from the user's device.","Necessary network transmission and later tracking are separate purposes."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-12","q":"Which functions may qualify as necessary?","p":"WP29 gives conditional examples including shopping-cart input, session authentication, user-centred security, requested media playback, load balancing and user-selected interface settings.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Opinion 04/2012 on Cookie Consent Exemption, WP194","u":"https://ec.europa.eu/justice/article-29/documentation/opinion-recommendation/files/2012/wp194_en.pdf","l":"Sections 2.1-2.4 and 3.1-3.7"}],"x":["Persistent login, unrelated profiling and mixed tracking purposes need separate assessment."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-13","q":"Are first-party analytics and advertising necessary by default?","p":"WP29 says first-party analytics are not strictly necessary merely because operators value them. Third-party advertising and its operational cookies do not qualify on that basis either.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Opinion 04/2012 on Cookie Consent Exemption, WP194","u":"https://ec.europa.eu/justice/article-29/documentation/opinion-recommendation/files/2012/wp194_en.pdf","l":"Sections 4.2-4.3, pages 9-11"}],"x":["The opinion's suggestion of a future analytics exemption is not an enacted exemption.","National audience-measurement rules cannot be copied into another country without checking its law."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-14","q":"What evidence should the public helper request?","p":"Ask what each tool reads, writes, sends and uses. Record the requested function, retention, recipients and reasons for any exemption. Test the site before and after consent choices.","k":"inference","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 6, 30-40 and 46-55"},{"t":"Planet49, C-673/17","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62017CJ0673","l":"Paragraphs 74-81"},{"t":"Opinion 04/2012 on Cookie Consent Exemption, WP194","u":"https://ec.europa.eu/justice/article-29/documentation/opinion-recommendation/files/2012/wp194_en.pdf","l":"Section 5, Summary and guidelines"}],"x":["No browser or technical inspection of a real site was performed in this research."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-M01","q":"When does wholly local use differ from access by another actor?","p":"Guidelines 2/2023 paragraph 44 distinguishes use wholly inside the device from access to information or derived results by another actor. Paragraphs 52-53 apply the access rule when locally computed results are made available externally. This does not exempt storage initiated by another party under paragraphs 35-36. Assess the write, local use and disclosure separately.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 35-36, 44 and 52-53"}],"x":["Local computation is not a blanket consent exemption"],"unk":["No implementation was tested"]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-M02","q":"What if the origin of an IP address is uncertain?","p":"Under Guidelines 2/2023 paragraph 55, an entity that cannot ensure that an IP address does not originate from terminal equipment must take the steps required by Article 5(3). Unknown origin is not evidence of exclusion. Paragraph 56 preserves the separate assessment of whether a consent exemption applies.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 54-56, especially the last sentence of paragraph 55"}],"x":["The default is not a finding that every IP use requires consent"],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DEVICE-M03","q":"Does a device-access exemption settle the GDPR question?","p":"A consent exemption for device storage or access does not by itself establish lawful personal-data processing. Where GDPR applies, assess its lawful-basis and other requirements for that processing. Conversely, Article 5(3) can protect device information even when it is not personal data.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 2, 4(1), 5, 6 and 95"},{"t":"Opinion 04/2012 on Cookie Consent Exemption, WP194","u":"https://ec.europa.eu/justice/article-29/documentation/opinion-recommendation/files/2012/wp194_en.pdf","l":"Sections 1 and 5"},{"t":"Planet49, C-673/17","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62017CJ0673","l":"Paragraphs 68-71"}],"x":["This is not a universal duplicate Article 6 test for all ePrivacy processing. The Inteligo Media Article 13(2) communication rule is separate"],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["National implementation, territorial reach and national analytics exemptions were not reviewed outside the separate Croatia outputs.","No actual analytics product, localStorage implementation, pixel or fingerprinting system was technically tested.","No vendor marketing claims were treated as evidence of exemption or anonymity.","The 2012 and 2014 WP29 opinions predate GDPR. They are used for Article 5(3) interpretation, with current EDPB and court sources, not as current GDPR consent definitions.","No independent legal review was requested."]},{"id":"F-DSA","name":"Digital Services Act","scope":"EU","summary":"The DSA applies to in-scope intermediary services, not every website. Hosting user material can create notice, explanation and order-handling duties. In-scope small intermediary and hosting businesses retain the relevant baseline duties. Section-specific exemptions remove some additional platform duties. Check redress and automation notices, the limits of hosting liability protection, and independent product-safety obligations.","claims":[{"id":"F-DSA-01","q":"When does the DSA reach an overseas operator?","p":"The DSA applies to intermediary services offered to recipients established or located in the EU, regardless of provider location. Offering in the EU requires a substantial connection. EU establishment, significant EU recipients or targeting one or more Member States can establish it. Mere technical accessibility from the EU is not enough by itself.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Art. 2(1), Art. 3(d)-(e), recitals 7-8"}],"x":["Mere worldwide accessibility without a substantial connection"],"unk":["Whether the actual targeting and user evidence establishes a substantial connection."]},{"id":"F-DSA-02","q":"What distinguishes intermediary, hosting and platform services?","p":"Intermediary services comprise mere conduit, caching and hosting. Hosting stores information supplied by and at a recipient's request. An online platform is a hosting service that also disseminates that information to the public, subject to the narrow ancillary-feature exception. Classification applies to the actual service activity.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Art. 3(g)(i)-(iii), (i) and (k)"}],"x":["Hosting does not require public dissemination","A website is not automatically a platform"],"unk":["Mixed-service features may need separate classification."]},{"id":"F-DSA-03","q":"Does an ordinary merchant website fall within the DSA?","p":"A site that publishes only the operator's own material and sells only its own goods or services is not an intermediary merely because it is online. Its hosting company may separately be an intermediary. Customer-storage, public reviews or third-party selling features require their own analysis. Other consumer and product laws can still apply.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 2(1)-(2), 3(g) and (i)"}],"x":["No third-party information service on the stated facts"],"unk":["A review, upload or marketplace feature may change the result."]},{"id":"F-DSA-04","q":"Are a small comments section and a discussion forum the same?","p":"User comments can be hosting even where their public dissemination is a minor and purely ancillary feature. Article 3(i) excludes such a feature from online-platform status only under its technical-link and anti-circumvention conditions. Recital 13 gives an ancillary newspaper comments section as an example. A forum or social network cannot assume that exclusion.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Art. 3(g)(iii), (i), recital 13"}],"x":["Ancillary-platform exclusion does not itself exclude hosting duties"],"unk":["No automatic exemption for every blog comment section."]},{"id":"F-DSA-05","q":"How is micro or small enterprise status tested?","p":"The incorporated SME definition treats a small enterprise as fewer than 50 staff with annual turnover or balance-sheet total no more than EUR 10 million. A microenterprise has fewer than 10 staff and turnover or balance-sheet total no more than EUR 2 million. Partner and linked enterprises can require aggregation. Use the Recommendation's accounting and staff rules, not visitor counts or one founder's headcount alone.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 15(2), 19 and 29"},{"t":"Commission Recommendation 2003/361/EC","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reco/2003/361/oj/eng","l":"Annex Arts. 2-6"}],"x":["Turnover alone is not the whole test"],"unk":["Actual group calculation and two-period rule."]},{"id":"F-DSA-06","q":"What does Article 19 exempt?","p":"Qualifying micro and small online-platform providers are exempt from Chapter III Section 3, except Article 24(3). This removes duties in that section such as the statutory internal complaints system and its platform-specific advertising and minors rules. Article 24(3) information requests still apply. Article 19 has a 12-month continuation after loss of SME status, but designated very large platforms cannot use the exemption.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Art. 19(1)-(2), Art. 24(3), Chapter III Section 3"}],"x":["No exemption from Sections 1 and 2","No exemption from other laws protecting children"],"unk":["Accurate SME status and any designation."]},{"id":"F-DSA-07","q":"What does Article 29 exempt?","p":"Qualifying micro and small platforms that let consumers conclude distance contracts with traders are exempt from Chapter III Section 4, including Articles 30-32 trader traceability, compliance-by-design and illegal-product information duties. A 12-month continuation can apply after loss of SME status. Designated very large platforms cannot claim it. This DSA exemption does not waive separate GPSR marketplace duties.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Art. 29(1)-(2), Arts. 30-32, Art. 2(4)(f)"},{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Art. 22"}],"x":["Do not transfer the DSA exemption to the GPSR"],"unk":["Which GPSR provisions that expressly cross-refer to DSA duties apply to a particular exempt marketplace."]},{"id":"F-DSA-08","q":"Which baseline duties remain for a small hosting operator?","p":"Articles 11-12 require authority and recipient contact points, with recipient communication not solely dependent on automated tools. Article 13 requires an EU legal representative for covered providers without an EU establishment. Article 14 requires clear moderation terms, notice of significant changes and proportionate enforcement. A service aimed primarily at minors, or mainly used by them, needs terms they can understand.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 11-14, especially Art. 12(1), 13(1), 14(1)-(4)"}],"x":["Do not impose VLOP-only summaries and language duties on ordinary small hosts"],"unk":["National contact, representative and competent-coordinator arrangements."]},{"id":"F-DSA-09","q":"What must the illegal-content notice mechanism do?","p":"Article 16 requires an accessible electronic notice mechanism for specific allegedly illegal content. It must support reasons, precise location, contact details subject to the child-abuse exception, and a good-faith statement. Where contact details are provided, the host must acknowledge receipt and communicate its decision without undue delay. Notices must be handled diligently, objectively and in a timely manner. The DSA does not impose a universal 24-hour takedown deadline.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Art. 16(1)-(6)"}],"x":["Separate laws or a specific authority order can impose different deadlines"],"unk":["What is timely for the risk and context."]},{"id":"F-DSA-10","q":"When must a small host explain a moderation decision or report crime?","p":"Article 17 requires a clear statement of reasons for specified restrictions based on illegality or incompatibility with terms, where the host knows relevant electronic contact details. Its exception covers deceptive high-volume commercial content. Article 18 requires prompt reporting of suspected criminal offences threatening people's life or safety to the competent authorities. These hosting provisions have no general microenterprise exemption.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Art. 17(1)-(5), Art. 18(1)-(2)"}],"x":["Article 17 does not apply to Article 9 orders","Article 18 is not a duty to report every suspected unlawful comment"],"unk":["Whether an event meets the Article 18 threshold."]},{"id":"F-DSA-11","q":"Must a small host monitor everything or publish annual moderation reports?","p":"Article 8 prohibits a general obligation to monitor or actively seek illegal activity. Article 6 conditions the hosting liability exemption on lack of specified knowledge or awareness and expeditious action once obtained. This is not immunity from specific orders. Article 15(2) exempts qualifying micro and small intermediary providers from the annual Article 15(1) transparency report unless designated as a very large platform.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 6, 8-10, 15(2)"}],"x":["No duty to remove everything alleged to be unlawful without assessment","No exemption from Article 16 or 17"],"unk":["Whether the host has acquired legally sufficient knowledge."]},{"id":"F-DSA-12","q":"Since when do these small-site duties apply?","p":"The DSA generally applies from 17 February 2024. Article 93(2) lists earlier provisions effective from 16 November 2022, including Article 24(3). Article 92 provides an earlier route for designated very large platforms and search engines. A small operator should not use a later VLOP enforcement headline as its start date.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 92-93"}],"x":["Earlier dates apply to the express Article 93 list"],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DSA-13","q":"What must a notice-response message explain?","p":"A host's Article 16 decision notification must include available redress information and be sent without undue delay. If automated means processed the notice or made the decision, disclose that use in the notification. Article 16(4)'s acknowledgement applies where electronic contact details were supplied.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Article 16(4)-(6)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DSA-14","q":"When and how must a restriction be explained?","p":"Where Article 17 applies, give the affected recipient a clear, specific statement no later than the date the restriction takes effect. Explain the measure, duration and territorial scope where relevant, facts, automation, legal or contractual grounds and available redress. Give enough understandable detail to exercise redress. Known electronic contact details are required, deceptive high-volume commercial content is excepted, and Article 9 orders use their own rules.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Article 17(1)-(5)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DSA-15","q":"When does the hosting liability exemption not apply?","p":"Article 6(1)'s hosting exemption does not apply when the recipient acts under the provider's authority or control. It also does not protect an online marketplace's consumer-law liability for a transaction presented so the average consumer would believe the platform, or a recipient under its authority/control, supplies the information, product or service. Liability exemption and due-diligence duties require separate analysis.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Article 6(2)-(4)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-DSA-16","q":"How must authority orders be handled?","p":"For an Article 9 illegal-content order, inform the issuing or specified authority without undue delay whether and when effect was given. Article 10 information orders also require prompt acknowledgement of receipt and effect. Check the required legal basis, authority, scope, identifiers, language and redress information. Inform affected recipients by the time of action or the time specified in the order, with the required reasons and redress details. Article 10 covers information already collected for the service and within the provider's control. National procedural law remains relevant.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Articles 9(1)-(2),(5)-(6), 10(1)-(2),(5)-(6)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{"s":"A sole trader sells only their own services on a static page.","r":"No DSA intermediary role follows just from owning the site."},{"s":"A two-person public discussion forum accepts user posts.","r":"Hosting duties still apply even if Article 19 removes additional platform duties."},{"s":"A small merchant runs a minor reviews feature.","r":"Platform status needs analysis. A hosting conclusion can still trigger core duties."},{"s":"A small marketplace sells consumer products from third-party traders.","r":"Article 29 relief does not establish GPSR exemption."}],"gaps":["No operational audit of a particular comments, uploads or third-party widget architecture.","No country-by-country enforcement, authority contact or illegal-content law review.","Group size and loss-of-status calculations are fact-dependent.","English operative text checked. Non-English corrigenda and a complete subordinate-instrument census were not audited.","EEA incorporation and national adaptations remain unchecked."]},{"id":"F-EAA","name":"Accessibility (EAA)","scope":"EU","summary":"The EAA covers listed consumer services, including e-commerce, with a service microenterprise exemption and limited transitions. Covered providers need accessible services, public compliance information and ongoing correction and notification procedures. Presumption can come through an OJ-cited harmonised standard or an adopted Commission technical specification. The applicable November 2026 benchmark remains unresolved. A WCAG test alone is insufficient.","claims":[{"id":"F-EAA-01","q":"Which services does the EAA cover?","p":"Covered consumer services include e-commerce, banking, e-books, electronic communications, access to audiovisual media and specified passenger-transport elements. The list does not cover every private website.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Art. 2(2)(a)-(f), Art. 2(3)"}],"x":["Specified transport and machine-to-machine limitations"],"unk":["National territorial implementation remains relevant."]},{"id":"F-EAA-02","q":"What counts as an e-commerce service?","p":"E-commerce involves remote electronic services through websites or mobile services, requested by a consumer to conclude a consumer contract. Recital 43 includes online sales of any product or service.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Art. 3(30), recitals 42-43"}],"x":["B2B-only supply is outside this consumer e-commerce definition."],"unk":["Booking, enquiry-only and mixed-purpose interfaces may need interpretation."]},{"id":"F-EAA-03","q":"Which small service providers are exempt?","p":"Microenterprises providing services are exempt under Art. 4(5). The definition requires fewer than 10 persons and annual turnover or annual balance-sheet total not exceeding EUR 2 million.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 3(23), 4(5)"}],"x":["Ten persons fails the fewer-than-ten condition.","This is not the same exemption for covered products."],"unk":["Counting methods and related-enterprise treatment need national and enterprise-specific review."]},{"id":"F-EAA-04","q":"When do the rules apply and what can transition?","p":"National measures apply from 28 June 2025. Limited product-use and unchanged pre-existing service-contract transitions run towards 28 June 2030. Existing websites receive no blanket exemption.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 31(2), 32(1)-(2)"}],"x":["The optional terminal rule is capped at 20 years after first use.","Contract continuation ends at expiry or the five-year cap."],"unk":["National transition and legacy-product interpretation must be checked."]},{"id":"F-EAA-05","q":"Which website-content exclusions exist?","p":"Specified older media and office files, qualifying maps, uncontrolled third-party content and qualifying archives are excluded. These are content-specific tests.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Art. 2(4)(a)-(e)"}],"x":["A third-party checkout is not automatically uncontrolled.","An actively updated site is not a qualifying archive as a whole."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-EAA-06","q":"What must an in-scope e-commerce service make accessible?","p":"Annex I covers websites, service information, support and accessible identification, security and payment. Providers must also explain compliance under Art. 13 and Annex V.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Art. 4(3), Art. 13(1)-(3), Annex I III(b)-(d) and IV(g), Annex V"}],"x":[],"unk":["A real technical and usability assessment is needed."]},{"id":"F-EAA-07","q":"Can a provider simply claim excessive cost?","p":"Art. 14 permits limited fundamental-alteration or disproportionate-burden reliance. It requires assessment, with documentation, review and notification duties subject to the stated exceptions.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Art. 14(1)-(8), Annex VI"}],"x":["Cost alone is not a blanket exemption.","External accessibility funding prevents reliance on disproportionate burden for that purpose."],"unk":["No operator-specific burden assessment was performed."]},{"id":"F-EAA-08","q":"Does passing WCAG prove EAA compliance?","p":"Article 15(1)'s harmonised-standard presumption requires an OJ-published reference and covers only the requirements addressed by that standard. Article 15(3)-(4) separately allows presumption through Commission technical specifications adopted under its conditions. A WCAG result alone does not establish full EAA compliance.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Article 15(1), (3)-(4), Annex I Sections III-IV"}],"x":["WAD harmonisation does not automatically establish EAA harmonisation."],"unk":["The applicable OJ citation as of 7 November 2026 has not been established."]},{"id":"F-EAA-09","q":"What changed in EN 301 549 during 2026?","p":"AccessibleEU reported EN 301 549 v4.1.1 in September 2026, using WCAG 2.2. It said formal OJ citation was still pending at publication.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AccessibleEU, The European accessibility standard EN 301 549 has been updated","u":"https://accessible-eu-centre.ec.europa.eu/content-corner/news/european-accessibility-standard-en-301-549-has-been-updated-2026-09-07_en?prefLang=ro","l":"News dated 7 September 2026, paragraphs 1-5"}],"x":["This dated guidance is not proof of the November legal reference."],"unk":["Later OJ citation, transitional provisions and the standard's full mapping were not verified."]},{"id":"F-EAA-10","q":"What ongoing duties apply to a covered service provider?","p":"Provide public written and oral information explaining accessibility compliance under Annex V, accessible to disabled people, and keep it while the service operates. Maintain procedures for conformity as the service, requirements and standards change. Correct non-conformity and immediately inform competent authorities in the Member States where the service is provided, including the problem and corrective measures. Cooperate on reasoned authority requests.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Article 13(2)-(5), Annex V"}],"x":[],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["The Commission WAD standards page returned HTTP 429. Its search excerpt is only a discovery lead.","National rules outside Croatia, enterprise counting and third-country enforcement have not been fully reviewed.","The report does not certify accessibility or perform a technical audit.","Other equality, public-sector, procurement and national accessibility laws may apply despite an EAA exclusion.","M-EAA-CURRENT-OJ: A targeted official search did not establish the EAA OJ standard or technical-specification position on 2026-11-07. The September AccessibleEU report remains a dated report, not November proof. Do not issue a presumption-of-conformity result until the applicable instrument and covered requirements are checked."]},{"id":"F-ECOMMERCE","name":"E-commerce and trader info","scope":"EU","summary":"Identify the operator, customer and applicable national law. Online business activity can require trader information even without a fee to view the site. The order flow may need acknowledgement and input-error correction. Consumer sales add payment-button, delivery/payment restriction and durable-confirmation duties. Check the specific B2B and individual-email exceptions.","claims":[{"id":"F-ECOMMERCE-01","q":"Which online activities trigger the trader-information layer?","p":"The E-Commerce Directive covers information society services, including economic online activities funded by advertising. Its information duties can apply to B2B services.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2000/31/EC, consolidation of 17 February 2024","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/2024-02-17/eng","l":"Arts. 1(1)-(5), 2(a)-(d), 5(1)"},{"t":"Directive 2000/31/EC, original OJ text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/oj/eng","l":"Recital 18"}],"x":["Purely private non-economic activity","Art. 1(5) excluded fields"],"unk":["Borderline economic activity needs assessment."]},{"id":"F-ECOMMERCE-02","q":"What provider details must remain accessible?","p":"Article 5 requires accessible identity, establishment address and effective contact details including email. Registration, authorisation, professional and VAT details are conditional. Displayed prices must clarify tax and delivery.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2000/31/EC, consolidation of 17 February 2024","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/2024-02-17/eng","l":"Art. 5(1)(a)-(g), 5(2)"},{"t":"Directive 2000/31/EC, original OJ text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/oj/eng","l":"Art. 5"}],"x":[],"unk":["National implementation may add fields."]},{"id":"F-ECOMMERCE-03","q":"What must advertising and promotions disclose?","p":"Commercial communications must identify their commercial nature and the person represented. Permitted promotions and competitions need clear identification and accessible participation conditions.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2000/31/EC, consolidation of 17 February 2024","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/2024-02-17/eng","l":"Art. 6(a)-(d)"}],"x":["This provision does not itself permit unsolicited marketing."],"unk":["National marketing and gambling restrictions need separate review."]},{"id":"F-ECOMMERCE-04","q":"What information belongs before an electronic order?","p":"Article 10 covers contract steps, filing and access, error correction, languages and subscribed codes. Supplied terms must be storable and reproducible.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2000/31/EC, consolidation of 17 February 2024","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/2024-02-17/eng","l":"Article 10(1)-(4)"}],"x":["Non-consumers may agree otherwise for paragraphs 1-2.","Email-only or equivalent individual exchanges exclude paragraphs 1-2, not paragraph 3."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-ECOMMERCE-05","q":"What does the CRD add for consumer distance sales?","p":"Before a covered consumer is bound, Article 6 requires identity, geographical address, phone, email and applicable representative and complaints addresses. Further fields depend on the offer.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Art. 6(1)(a)-(t), especially (b)-(d)"},{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/2161","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/2161/oj/eng","l":"Art. 4(4)(a)(i) replacing CRD Art. 6(1)(c)"}],"x":["CRD Art. 3 exclusions and financial-service regime"],"unk":["Sector and national information rules remain relevant."]},{"id":"F-ECOMMERCE-06","q":"Does every website visible in Europe fall under the same law?","p":"A globally reachable website does not establish one EU-wide disclosure regime. E-Commerce Directive establishment rules and consumer-contract choice-of-law rules require separate analysis.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2000/31/EC, consolidation of 17 February 2024","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/2024-02-17/eng","l":"Arts. 1(4), 3(1)-(4) and Annex"},{"t":"Directive 2000/31/EC, original OJ text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/oj/eng","l":"Recital 58"},{"t":"Regulation (EC) No 593/2008, Rome I","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2008/593/oj/eng","l":"Art. 6(1)-(4), recital 24"}],"x":["Mere accessibility is insufficient to resolve targeting.","The Directive does not create a complete private-international-law code."],"unk":["Third-country enforcement and each destination's implementation require a country-specific check."]},{"id":"F-ECOMMERCE-07","q":"Can a chatbot replace all trader contact details?","p":"Commission guidance treats alternative tools such as chatbots as additional communication methods. They must not obstruct the contact methods required by the CRD.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Commission Notice, guidance on Directive 2011/83/EU, 2021/C 525/01","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:52021XC1229(04)","l":"Section 3.2.2.2, Means of communication"},{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Art. 6(1)(c)"}],"x":["Guidance is not an independent legislative obligation."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-ECOMMERCE-08","q":"What must the electronic order flow do?","p":"For orders placed through technological means, acknowledge receipt electronically without undue delay. Orders and acknowledgements are received when their addressees can access them. Provide effective, accessible input-error identification and correction before ordering. Non-consumer parties may agree otherwise. For contracts concluded exclusively through individual email or equivalent communications, Article 11(3) excludes the acknowledgement duty and error-correction duty, but not the receipt-access rule.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2000/31/EC, consolidation of 17 February 2024","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/2024-02-17/eng","l":"Article 11(1)-(3)"}],"x":["National implementation still needs checking."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-ECOMMERCE-09","q":"Which consumer checkout and confirmation duties apply?","p":"For a paid electronic distance contract, present the Article 8(2) information clearly and prominently directly before ordering. Obtain explicit acknowledgement of the payment obligation. A payment button must carry only an easily legible 'order with obligation to pay' label or equivalent unambiguous wording. Failure to meet that acknowledgement/button rule leaves the consumer unbound. Show delivery restrictions and accepted payment methods by the start of ordering. Give durable contract confirmation within a reasonable time, no later than delivery or before service performance, including required information and applicable digital-content consent confirmation.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Article 8(2)-(3), (7)"}],"x":["A particular Member State's implementation must be checked."],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["No complete national implementation matrix has been researched. EU directives alone must not generate a definitive country-specific checklist.","Territorial applicability for an operator outside the EU needs the operator's facts, destination law and enforcement analysis.","The conditional Article 6 checklist needs product-specific expansion, including the September 2026 changes covered in F-CONSUMER.","No EEA incorporation or UK, Swiss or other non-EU national-law conclusion is made."]},{"id":"F-EMAIL","name":"Marketing email","scope":"EU","summary":"Check permission to send before choosing a data-processing basis. The customer exception needs lawfully obtained details, a qualifying sale context, the sender's own similar offerings and an easy free objection at collection and in every message. Inteligo limits the extra Article 6 assessment for compliant covered sending. Applicable privacy notices, separate objection information and commercial-message safeguards still need checking. National rules govern company recipients.","claims":[{"id":"F-EMAIL-01","q":"Which rule permits sending?","p":"For email direct marketing to natural-person subscribers, the EU starting rule is prior consent. Article 13(2) provides a conditional customer exception. National law determines protection for legal-person subscribers under Article 13(5).","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2002/58/EC, consolidated 19 December 2009","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02002L0058-20091219","l":"Article 13(1),(2),(5)"}],"x":["No EU-wide permission for cold B2B email"],"unk":["Cross-border choice of applicable national law"]},{"id":"F-EMAIL-02","q":"What are the customer-exception conditions?","p":"The same person that lawfully obtained the address from its customer in a sale context may market its own similar products or services. Collection and every message must offer a clear, easy, free objection. An initial refusal defeats this route. Inteligo does not cure unlawful collection or authorise unrelated enrichment.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2002/58/EC, consolidated 19 December 2009","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02002L0058-20091219","l":"Article 13(2)"},{"t":"CJEU, Inteligo Media, C-654/23, 13 November 2025, ECLI:EU:C:2025:871","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62023CJ0654","l":"Paragraphs 38, 47-48 and 63"}],"x":["Bought lists","Another group company's products are not automatically the sender's own"],"unk":["Whether a particular offering is similar"]},{"id":"F-EMAIL-03","q":"Must every permitted email also pass a separate Article 6 test?","p":"GDPR Article 6 generally requires a lawful basis for personal-data processing. Inteligo holds that Article 6(1) does not apply to processing covered by a compliant Article 13(2) communication. Keep other processing operations separate. Legitimate interests cannot replace a required send consent.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Articles 6(1) and 95"},{"t":"CJEU, Inteligo Media, C-654/23, 13 November 2025, ECLI:EU:C:2025:871","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62023CJ0654","l":"Paragraphs 64-69 and operative point 2"},{"t":"Directive 2002/58/EC, consolidated 19 December 2009","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02002L0058-20091219","l":"Article 13(1)-(2)"}],"x":["No blanket GDPR exemption","No extension to list purchase, enrichment or unrelated profiling"],"unk":["Other processing and national implementation require their own assessment"]},{"id":"F-EMAIL-04","q":"Can a free account be a customer relationship?","p":"Inteligo treated the described free publishing account and newsletter as a sale context and direct marketing of similar services. The account combined limited free articles with access to paid material. This does not make every free download or registration a qualifying customer relationship.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CJEU, Inteligo Media, C-654/23, 13 November 2025, ECLI:EU:C:2025:871","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62023CJ0654","l":"Paragraphs 43-48, 53-63 and operative point 1"}],"x":["No automatic extension to lead magnets"],"unk":["Application to other free services"]},{"id":"F-EMAIL-05","q":"What evidence and withdrawal controls does consent require?","p":"Where consent is used, it must meet GDPR standards and be demonstrable. Withdrawal must be as easy as consent. Consent must be freely given, specific, informed and unambiguous.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Articles 4(11), 7(1)-(4)"},{"t":"Directive 2002/58/EC, consolidated 19 December 2009","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02002L0058-20091219","l":"Article 2(f), read with GDPR Article 94(2)"}],"x":["A pre-ticked box is not an affirmative choice"],"unk":["Appropriate evidence and retention period"]},{"id":"F-EMAIL-06","q":"Must recipients have a way to stop marketing?","p":"Marketing email must identify the sender and provide a valid cessation address. Where GDPR Article 21 applies, an objection ends direct-marketing processing, including related profiling. Article 13(2) separately requires an easy, free objection in every message.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2002/58/EC, consolidated 19 December 2009","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02002L0058-20091219","l":"Article 13(2),(4)"},{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Article 21(2)-(4)"}],"x":["No balancing override for an Article 21(2) marketing objection"],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-EMAIL-07","q":"How should double opt-in be described?","p":"As proposed evidence practice, double opt-in asks a subscriber to confirm through the submitted mailbox before marketing starts. It can support consent evidence. The cited provisions do not prescribe this workflow. Do not label it a universal legal requirement or proof of all consent conditions.","k":"proposal","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Articles 5(2) and 7(1)"}],"x":["Confirmation does not repair a misleading or bundled consent request"],"unk":["No permitted-authority guidance expressly recommending double opt-in was retrieved"]},{"id":"F-EMAIL-08","q":"How should a suppression list be handled?","p":"A minimal suppression record can prevent a refused address from being reimported. This is an implementation inference, not an express duty to keep a named list. Set a lawful retention purpose, access controls and review period. Never reuse the record for marketing.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Articles 5(1)(b),(c),(e),(f), 6(1), 21(3)"},{"t":"Directive 2002/58/EC, consolidated 19 December 2009","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02002L0058-20091219","l":"Article 13(2),(4)"}],"x":["No blanket right to retain a full customer profile","No fixed universal retention period"],"unk":["Article 6 basis for suppression and local retention rules need assessment"]},{"id":"F-EMAIL-09","q":"Which information and objection notices are needed?","p":"Where GDPR applies, provide Article 13 information when collecting data directly. For indirectly obtained data, Article 14 normally requires information within one month, or by an earlier first communication or disclosure. Apply the statutory exceptions, including information already held. Draw attention to the Article 21 marketing-objection right clearly and separately by the first communication. Assess Article 95 for the processing concerned.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Articles 13(1)-(4), 14(1)-(5), 21(2)-(4), 95"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-EMAIL-10","q":"Does every free registration qualify?","p":"Inteligo's sale-context conclusion concerned a publishing service supplied on contractual terms and remunerated indirectly through its commercial model. The exception still requires lawfully acquired details. A bare giveaway registration is not automatically equivalent.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CJEU, Inteligo Media, C-654/23, 13 November 2025, ECLI:EU:C:2025:871","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62023CJ0654","l":"Paragraphs 38-39, 48, 53-57"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-EMAIL-11","q":"What commercial-message safeguards apply?","p":"Article 13(4) also prohibits email that breaches E-Commerce Directive Article 6 or encourages visits to websites that breach it. Identify the commercial communication and represented person. Identify permitted promotional offers and competitions and make their conditions accessible and clear.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2002/58/EC, consolidated 19 December 2009","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02002L0058-20091219","l":"Article 13(4)"},{"t":"Directive 2000/31/EC, consolidation of 17 February 2024","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/2024-02-17/eng","l":"Article 6(a)-(d)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["National transposition and territorial rules outside the Croatian work packages are not researched here.","Permitted-source searches did not retrieve express double-opt-in guidance. The practice is labelled proposal, not official guidance or law.","No permitted official source establishing a general suppression-list retention duration or universal basis was found.","Inteligo's exact boundary for other processing requires legal review. Do not state either that every soft opt-in needs a second Article 6 basis or that GDPR disappears entirely.","Research extraction and source comparison are complete for these candidates. Editorial approval and qualified legal review have not occurred.","EMAIL-M4: AZOP's recommendation landing page opened, but attempts to read its Izravni marketing passage timed out. Reconcile its exact legitimate-interest wording with Inteligo paragraphs 68-69 before claiming the authority positions align. The CJEU holding does not authorise unrelated processing. Official URL: https://azop.hr/preporuka-za-provedbu-testa-legitimnog-interesa-prema-clanku-6-stavku-1-tocki-f-opce-uredbe-o-zastiti-podataka/."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS","name":"GDPR: legal basis","scope":"EU","summary":"List each purpose for using personal data and establish the applicable legal basis before starting. A contact form does not always need consent. For the specific email communications that meet ePrivacy Article 13(2), Inteligo Media removes a second Article 6 assessment. Other uses need their own analysis. Check national marketing and device-access rules and the conditions for any exception.","claims":[{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-01","q":"Is there one lawful basis for the whole website?","p":"Ordinarily, Article 6 requires a valid basis for each processing purpose. Its six bases are consent, contractual necessity, legal obligation, vital interests, public task or official authority, and legitimate interests. Establish the applicable basis before processing and link it to the actual purpose. Article 95 qualifies this framework. Under Inteligo Media, C-654/23, the communication processing that satisfies ePrivacy Article 13(2) does not require a second Article 6(1) assessment. Other data uses require their own analysis.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 5(1)(a)-(c), 5(2), 6(1), 13(1)(c), 14(1)(c), Article 95"},{"t":"Guidelines 05/2020 on consent under Regulation 2016/679","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_202005_consent_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 121-123, printed p. 25"},{"t":"Inteligo Media, C-654/23, ECLI:EU:C:2025:871","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62023CJ0654","l":"Paragraphs 64-69 and operative ruling 2"}],"x":["Accepting a privacy notice is not itself one of the six bases"],"unk":["Actual purposes have not been supplied"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-02","q":"Can a contact enquiry or order use the contract basis?","p":"Article 6(1)(b) can cover data objectively needed to perform a contract with the person or take steps they request before a contract. An enquiry about buying a service can qualify even if no contract follows. Extra tracking or unsolicited marketing does not become necessary merely because the terms mention it.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 6(1)(b)"},{"t":"Guidelines 2/2019 on Article 6(1)(b) in online services","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines-art_6-1-b-adopted_after_public_consultation_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 22-30 and 45-47, printed pp. 8-9 and 13"}],"x":["A general comment is not automatically a pre-contract request","A corporate customer's employee is not automatically the contracting data subject"],"unk":["The applicable contract law and parties must be identified"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-03","q":"Does every contact form need a consent checkbox?","p":"No universal rule requires consent for every contact form. A requested sales enquiry may use Article 6(1)(b). A general enquiry may instead justify Article 6(1)(f), if the operator shows a legitimate purpose, necessity and a favourable balance. Answering an enquiry and adding the sender to a marketing list are separate purposes. These are conditional applications of the law, not automatic approvals.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 6(1)(b), 6(1)(f), 7 and 13(1)(c)"},{"t":"Guidelines 2/2019 on Article 6(1)(b) in online services","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines-art_6-1-b-adopted_after_public_consultation_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 45-47, printed p. 13"}],"x":["A form submission alone does not authorise unrelated marketing","A required tick accepting the notice does not repair an invalid basis"],"unk":["The balancing outcome for a general enquiry remains unassessed"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-04","q":"What must legitimate interests establish?","p":"Article 6(1)(f) requires a legitimate interest, necessary processing and a balance in which the person's interests or fundamental rights and freedoms do not override the legitimate interests pursued. Consider equally effective, less intrusive means and the person's reasonable expectations. A lawful commercial interest can qualify, but it does not automatically pass necessity or balancing. Record the assessment so the operator can demonstrate compliance. Article 5(2) does not prescribe a particular assessment form.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 5(2), 6(1)(f), 13(1)(d)"},{"t":"Koninklijke Nederlandse Lawn Tennisbond, C-621/22, ECLI:EU:C:2024:858","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62022CJ0621","l":"Paragraphs 37-45 and 49-50"}],"x":["An operator's wish to profit does not settle the balance","Public authorities cannot use Article 6(1)(f) in performing their public tasks"],"unk":["No completed balancing assessment exists for a particular operator"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-05","q":"What makes consent valid and usable?","p":"Consent must be freely given, specific, informed and unambiguous, through a statement or clear affirmative action. The operator must be able to prove it. Present a written consent request separately from other matters. Explain withdrawal before consent and make withdrawal as easy as giving consent. Making a service depend on unnecessary consent weighs against freedom of choice.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 4(11), 7(1)-(4), Recital 32"}],"x":["Silence or a preselected option is not a clear affirmative choice","A privacy notice supplies information, not permission by itself"],"unk":["Children and special-category data may require additional conditions"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-06","q":"Can the operator switch to legitimate interests after withdrawal?","p":"EDPB guidance rejects using another basis retrospectively to rescue invalid consent. Stop the purpose that relied on consent when consent is withdrawn. A different purpose, such as legally required accounting retention, needs its own valid basis and transparent explanation. Consent withdrawal does not make earlier lawful processing unlawful.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 05/2020 on consent under Regulation 2016/679","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_202005_consent_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 117-123 and footnote 56, printed pp. 24-25"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 7(3), 17(1)(b), 17(3)(b)"}],"x":["A previously disclosed, independently justified accounting duty does not disappear on marketing withdrawal"],"unk":["The retention law and time limit need jurisdiction-specific research"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-07","q":"Which rules apply to newsletters and email marketing?","p":"Article 13(1) of the ePrivacy Directive generally requires prior consent for covered electronic marketing. Article 13(2) permits the same sender to market its own similar products or services using customer email details lawfully obtained in the context of a sale. Customers must have a clear, easy and free objection at collection and in every message, and must not have initially refused. Inteligo Media, C-654/23, holds that this communication processing needs no additional Article 6(1) assessment because of GDPR Article 95. Its defined free-account publication arrangement can satisfy the sale condition. That does not establish an exception for every free account or unrelated profiling. Check national implementation, recipient type and territorial reach.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 6(1), 7, 21(2)-(3) and 95"},{"t":"ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2002/58","l":"Article 13(1)-(5), consolidation 19 December 2009"},{"t":"Inteligo Media, C-654/23, ECLI:EU:C:2025:871","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62023CJ0654","l":"Paragraphs 48-69 and operative rulings 1-2"}],"x":["A published email address is not the existing-customer exception","A necessary service message without promotion is not automatically marketing","The holding does not remove the requirement that customer contact details were lawfully obtained or authorise unrelated profiling"],"unk":["National email-marketing rules are outside this family","The ePrivacy consolidation shows Directives 2006/24/EC and 2009/136/EC. This claim uses Article 13 as amended by 2009/136/EC, not the invalidated data-retention regime"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-08","q":"How should analytics be assessed?","p":"Inspect what the analytics tool actually does. If it processes personal data, it needs an Article 6 basis. The EDPB says service-improvement metrics are generally not necessary to perform an online service contract. Separately assess terminal storage or access under national rules implementing ePrivacy Article 5(3). A GDPR legitimate-interest assessment does not replace a required device-access consent.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 4(1), 5(1)(c), 6(1)"},{"t":"Guidelines 2/2019 on Article 6(1)(b) in online services","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines-art_6-1-b-adopted_after_public_consultation_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 48-49, printed p. 14"},{"t":"ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2002/58","l":"Article 5(3), consolidation 19 December 2009"}],"x":["Truly anonymous output does not establish that earlier collection was anonymous","Cookie-free does not automatically mean outside GDPR or ePrivacy","An essential shopping-cart function and audience measurement need separate analysis"],"unk":["No named analytics configuration has been examined","National analytics exemptions and territorial rules need separate checks"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-09","q":"When are the six bases insufficient on their own?","p":"A legal obligation or public-task basis needs the supporting EU or Member State law required by Article 6(3). Special-category data also need an Article 9 exception. Criminal-conviction data face Article 10 conditions. Processing based on Article 6(1)(a) in relation to information society services offered directly to a child requires the Article 8 age and parental-authorisation assessment. Article 6 alone does not settle these cases.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 4(25), 6(1)(c) and (e), 6(3), 8(1)-(3), 9(1)-(2), 10"}],"x":["Ordinary commercial convenience is not a statutory duty","A vital-interest basis is not a general business justification"],"unk":["National law, age rules and sensitive-data conditions need specialist assessment"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-M01","q":"Can a free account qualify for the email-marketing sale condition?","p":"Inteligo Media held that the specified free publication account can meet ePrivacy Article 13(2)'s sale condition. It offered limited free articles, a daily legal-news email with article links and optional paid access to additional content. The indirect remuneration through paid content mattered. For communication processing satisfying Article 13(2), Article 95 excludes an additional Article 6(1) assessment. All collection and objection conditions remain.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Inteligo Media, C-654/23, ECLI:EU:C:2025:871","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62023CJ0654","l":"Paragraphs 48-69 and operative rulings 1-2"},{"t":"ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2002/58","l":"Article 13(2)"}],"x":["Not every free account qualifies","Unrelated profiling is outside this holding"],"unk":["National implementation and actual facts remain unassessed"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-BASIS-M02","q":"What changes when data are reused for a new purpose?","p":"Where further processing is not based on the person's consent or a qualifying Union or Member State law under Article 6(4), assess compatibility with the original purpose. Consider the links between purposes, collection context, data nature, possible consequences and safeguards. A new notice or newly named basis alone does not establish compatibility. Articles 13(3) and 14(4) separately require advance information.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 5(1)(b), 6(4)(a)-(e), 13(3), 14(4)"}],"x":["Compatibility and transparency are separate requirements"],"unk":["No actual reuse proposal was supplied"]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["No operator-specific purpose map, consent flow, balance assessment or supplier settings were supplied.","EDPB Guidelines 1/2024 landing page showed version 1.0 and public consultation closed. It was not treated as a final adopted replacement. The balancing claim relies on Article 6 and the CJEU judgment.","InfoCuria returned no readable judgment body. The official judgment was read on EUR-Lex instead.","Each country's ePrivacy implementation, existing-customer exception, business-recipient rules and territorial reach remain separate research tasks.","EDPB sources were read for the cited provisions. This is not an exhaustive review of all later decisions about each technology."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE","name":"GDPR: privacy notices","scope":"EU","summary":"Identify who uses personal data and why. Explain the sources, recipients, storage periods and applicable rights. Check intended transfers to a third country or international organisation and disclose the relevant decision or safeguards. Disclosure alone does not make a transfer lawful. Give clear information at the required time. These claims are a checklist, not a completed notice.","claims":[{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-01","q":"Who and what must the operator identify first?","p":"Identify the controller and its contact details. Identify any required representative and give the DPO's contact details where applicable. Record each processing purpose and its lawful basis. If Article 6(1)(f) applies, identify the legitimate interests pursued by the controller or a third party.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 13(1)(a)-(d), 14(1)(a)-(c), 14(2)(b)"}],"x":["An Article 27 representative and a DPO are different roles","Do not invent a DPO if none is appointed or required"],"unk":["Operator identity, role and appointment facts are missing"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-02","q":"Which collection and data-source facts matter?","p":"Determine whether data come directly from the person or from another source. Article 14 requires the categories of personal data and their source, including whether the source is public. For direct collection, Article 13 requires the operator to explain whether providing data is legally or contractually required, whether the person must provide it, and the consequences of refusing.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 13(2)(e), 14(1)(d), 14(2)(f)"}],"x":["Publicly available personal data are not automatically exempt from Article 14"],"unk":["Mixed direct and indirect collection requires separate timing and content analysis"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-03","q":"What sharing and transfer facts must the operator know?","p":"Identify recipients or meaningful categories of recipients. Determine whether personal data will go to a third country or international organisation. Where applicable, state whether an adequacy decision exists. For the transfer routes named in Articles 13(1)(f) and 14(1)(f), identify the safeguards and explain how to obtain them. The notice cannot claim a transfer safeguard that has not been established.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 13(1)(e)-(f), 14(1)(e)-(f)"}],"x":["EU hosting does not alone establish that no overseas recipient can access data"],"unk":["Vendor contracts, subprocessor lists and transfer assessments were not supplied"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-04","q":"What retention facts must be settled?","p":"State how long personal data will be stored. If a fixed period is not possible, explain the criteria used to determine it. The operator must know which period or criteria apply to each purpose and data set. A phrase such as 'as long as necessary' needs enough detail to let a person understand what determines the duration.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 13(2)(a), 14(2)(a), 5(1)(e)"},{"t":"Guidelines on transparency under Regulation 2016/679, WP260 rev.01","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2023-09/wp260rev01_en.pdf","l":"Annex, storage-period row, printed pp. 38-39"},{"t":"Endorsement 1/2018","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sites/default/files/files/news/endorsement_of_wp29_documents_en_0.pdf","l":"Item 2"}],"x":["One universal number may not fit enquiries, invoices and marketing","A notice does not create a retention justification"],"unk":["No retention schedule or national tax/accounting law was supplied"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-05","q":"What rights information must the notice provide?","p":"Explain the rights to request access, correction, erasure and restriction, and the rights to object and data portability where applicable. Explain withdrawal when consent is the basis, without invalidating earlier lawful processing. Explain the right to complain to a supervisory authority. The operator needs usable routes for these requests and must avoid promising that every right applies without conditions.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 12(1)-(2), 13(2)(b)-(d), 14(2)(c)-(e)"}],"x":["Erasure is not absolute","Portability does not apply to every basis or all data"],"unk":["Cross-border authority competence must be assessed rather than copied from a template"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-06","q":"What automated-decision facts must be disclosed?","p":"Identify whether the operator makes solely automated decisions with legal or similarly significant effects, including relevant profiling. For the decisions referred to in Article 22(1) and (4), give meaningful information about the logic, significance and expected consequences. Establish these facts before drafting a statement that no automated decisions occur.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 13(2)(f), 14(2)(g), 22(1)-(4)"}],"x":["A basic recommendation without legal or similarly significant effects does not automatically fall under Article 22(1)","Other profiling can still require purpose and basis disclosure"],"unk":["No decision system was inspected","A detailed automated-decision explanation may require a separate case-law assessment"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-07","q":"When must the person receive the notice?","p":"For direct collection, provide Article 13 information when obtaining the data. For indirect collection, provide Article 14 information within a reasonable time and no later than one month. If communication or disclosure occurs sooner, provide it by the first communication or first disclosure as applicable. Before using data for a new purpose, give the required information about that purpose.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 13(1)-(3), 14(3)-(4)"}],"x":["Publishing a page later does not cure a missed collection-time duty","A new notice alone does not authorise an incompatible new purpose"],"unk":["Delivery method and proof of provision remain untested"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-08","q":"When can notice duties be limited?","p":"Article 13(4) and Article 14(5)(a) avoid repeating information the person already has. Article 14 also has specific exceptions for impossible or disproportionate provision, certain legally required obtaining or disclosure, and regulated professional secrecy. Each exception has conditions. The disproportionate-effort route requires protective measures, including making the information public. It is not a general exemption for small operators or public-source data.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 13(4), 14(5)(a)-(d), 23(1)-(2)"}],"x":["Lack of budget alone does not establish disproportionate effort","Article 14(5)(b) is not an Article 13 direct-collection exception"],"unk":["National restrictions and any claimed exception need specific review"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-09","q":"How should the operator turn these facts into a website notice?","p":"Use concise, clear and easily accessible language suitable for the audience, especially children. EDPB-endorsed guidance recommends a layered notice with key facts at the collection point and direct routes to the detail. Keep the layers consistent. First assemble a factual inventory of forms, scripts, suppliers, purposes and retention. This inventory step is an implementation inference, not a statutory template.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 12(1), 5(2), 13 and 14"},{"t":"Guidelines on transparency under Regulation 2016/679, WP260 rev.01","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2023-09/wp260rev01_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 35-36, printed p. 19"},{"t":"Endorsement 1/2018","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sites/default/files/files/news/endorsement_of_wp29_documents_en_0.pdf","l":"Item 2"}],"x":["A generic template cannot establish what the site actually does","Consent to the notice is not a substitute for transparency"],"unk":["No live website, supplier terms or data inventory were examined"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-M01","q":"How does the access explanation ruling relate to automated-decision notices?","p":"Under Article 15(1)(h), Dun & Bradstreet Austria requires an intelligible explanation of the procedure and principles actually applied to the person's data. An algorithm or exhaustive technical steps alone are insufficient. If the controller claims trade secrets or protected third-party data, it must give the allegedly protected information to the competent supervisory authority or court for a case-specific balance. Those interests do not permit a blanket refusal. The judgment directly interprets access under Article 15. Its relevance to the parallel Article 13(2)(f) and 14(2)(g) notice wording is a cross-reference, not a claim that it directly decided those notice duties.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Dun & Bradstreet Austria, C-203/22","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62022CJ0203","l":"Paragraphs 57-63 and 74-76, operative rulings 1-2"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 15(1)(h), 22(1)-(4)"}],"x":["This is an access holding, not a direct Article 13 or 14 ruling"],"unk":["The actual system and explanation remain unexamined"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-M02","q":"What must people be told about joint controllers?","p":"Joint controllers must make the essence of their Article 26 arrangement available to the person. The arrangement must reflect their roles and relationships and allocate responsibilities, except insofar as relevant law determines them. People may exercise GDPR rights against each joint controller regardless of that arrangement.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 26(1)-(3)"}],"x":["A vendor list alone does not explain shared responsibility"],"unk":["Actual joint-control relationships have not been assessed"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-NOTICE-M03","q":"How must objection rights be presented?","p":"At the latest on the first communication, explicitly bring Article 21(1) and (2) objection rights to the person's attention. Present them clearly and separately from other information. A generic rights paragraph may miss this presentation duty.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 21(4), read with Article 21(1)-(2)"}],"x":["Only the objection rights identified in Article 21(1)-(2) are covered by this rule"],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["The required operator facts are unknown. This output is a research checklist, not a completed privacy notice.","National restrictions, professional secrecy rules and individual Article 14 exceptions were not assessed.","International-transfer legality requires its own review. Disclosure is not proof that a transfer is lawful.","C-203/22 was checked for Article 15 explanations. Application of the notice duties to an actual automated-decision system remains unassessed.","Recheck the law and operator facts before public projection."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR","name":"GDPR: processors and DPAs","scope":"EU","summary":"Identify each vendor's actual role. Article 28 requires a binding written contract or other qualifying legal act covering instructions, security, assistance, audits and return or deletion. Check sub-processors and ongoing guarantees. The processor must immediately flag instructions it considers contrary to GDPR or other EU or Member State data-protection rules. A transfer mechanism does not remove these duties.","claims":[{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-01","q":"Is the vendor a processor?","p":"A controller decides the purposes and means of processing. A processor handles personal data on the controller's behalf. Assess each activity separately.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 4(7), 4(8) and 28(10)"}],"x":["A vendor's product category or DPA label alone does not settle its role."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-02","q":"Does a standard service make the vendor a controller?","p":"EDPB treats roles as functional. A processor can choose practical technical methods. Contract labels cannot displace the parties' actual decisions.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 07/2020 on the concepts of controller and processor","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2023-10/EDPB_guidelines_202007_controllerprocessor_final_en.pdf","l":"Part I, paragraphs 12-13 and 39-41"}],"x":["An instruction-bound hosting service is not automatically a joint controller."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-03","q":"When is an Article 26 arrangement needed?","p":"Joint decision-making requires an Article 26 allocation of responsibilities. Make its essence available to people. They can exercise rights against each joint controller.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 26(1)-(3)"}],"x":["Parallel independent purposes do not automatically establish joint controllership.","An Article 28 DPA does not replace a required Article 26 arrangement."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-04","q":"What must identify the contracted processing?","p":"Processing must be governed by a contract or other legal act under Union or Member State law that binds the processor to the controller. It must be in writing, including electronic form, and specify the processing's subject, duration, nature, purpose, data types, people affected and controller rights and duties.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 28(3), opening sentence, and 28(9)"}],"x":["A general privacy notice alone is not the required binding agreement.","References to a DPA elsewhere in this checklist describe the usual contract route. Article 28 also permits the qualifying binding legal act."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-05","q":"What must instructions and security cover?","p":"The DPA must require documented instructions, confidentiality, Article 32 security and lawful sub-processing. Instructions include international transfers.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 28(3)(a)-(d)"}],"x":["The statutory exception in Article 28(3)(a) is subject to advance notice unless law prohibits notice."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-06","q":"What assistance must the DPA provide?","p":"The DPA must provide help with individual rights and Articles 32-36 duties, qualified by the nature of processing and the applicable statutory limits.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 28(3)(e)-(f)"}],"x":["Processor support does not transfer all controller duties to the vendor."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-07","q":"What happens when the service ends?","p":"The controller chooses return or deletion. Existing copies must be deleted unless Union or Member State law requires storage.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 28(3)(g)"}],"x":["A vendor cannot substitute its own indefinite retention preference for the controller's choice."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-08","q":"What audit and warning rights are required?","p":"The Article 28 contract or other binding legal act must require compliance information and cooperation with audits and inspections. The processor must immediately inform the controller if it considers an instruction to infringe the GDPR or other Union or Member State data-protection provisions.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 28(3)(h) and final subparagraph"}],"x":["A certification is evidence, not a replacement for the statutory duties."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-09","q":"What must be checked for sub-processors?","p":"Sub-processors need prior specific or general written authorisation. General authorisation requires change notices and an opportunity to object. Equivalent obligations must flow down.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 28(2) and 28(4)"}],"x":["The initial processor remains fully liable to the controller for its sub-processor's performance."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-10","q":"How specific should vendor promises be?","p":"EDPB expects concrete implementation details, proportionate security and continuing checks of processor guarantees. A DPA should explain assistance, audits and confirmed deletion.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 07/2020 on the concepts of controller and processor","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2023-10/EDPB_guidelines_202007_controllerprocessor_final_en.pdf","l":"Part II, paragraphs 95-99, 110-114, 125-127, 130-145"}],"x":["Copying GDPR wording alone does not establish implementation."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-11","q":"Is a 72-hour vendor breach deadline sufficient?","p":"The processor must notify the controller without undue delay after awareness. The controller's separate authority-notification rule requires notice without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 33(1)-(2), read with Article 28(3)(f)"}],"x":["The controller's 72-hour rule includes the statutory risk exception and reasons for delay.","Do not give the vendor an automatic 72-hour waiting period."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-12","q":"Must every downstream provider be known?","p":"EDPB expects identities of all processors in the chain to remain available. Verification depth depends on risk. Obtaining every sub-processing contract is not automatic.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Opinion 22/2024 on reliance on processors and sub-processors","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-10/edpb_opinion_202422_relianceonprocessors-sub-processors_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 22-29, 34-41 and 69-71, executive summary"}],"x":["Low risk does not remove the verification obligation."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-13","q":"Does DPF certification replace the DPA?","p":"EDPB confirms that an EEA controller still needs Article 28 terms when using a DPF-certified US processor.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"EU-US Data Privacy Framework FAQ for European businesses","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2026-04/edpb_dpf_faq-for-businesses_v2_en.pdf","l":"Question 3.3, pages 5-6"}],"x":["A transfer mechanism and processor terms address different requirements."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-14","q":"Can transfer SCCs also supply processor terms?","p":"The Commission explains that completed Modules 2 and 3 of the 2021 transfer SCCs incorporate Article 28 requirements. A separate duplicate DPA is unnecessary for covered processing.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"New Standard Contractual Clauses, questions and answers","u":"https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/international-dimension-data-protection/new-standard-contractual-clauses-questions-and-answers-overview_en","l":"Question 29, Article 28 requirements in Modules 2 and 3"},{"t":"Commission Implementing Decision 2021/914","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2021/914/oj/eng","l":"Article 1(2), recital 9 and Annex, Modules 2 and 3"}],"x":["Article 28-only clauses do not themselves authorise a third-country transfer."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-15","q":"What should a small operator collect from each vendor?","p":"Use a service-by-service review. Match the DPA, security annex, sub-processor list, transfer terms and exit process to the site's actual data flows.","k":"inference","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 28(1)-(4) and 28(9)"},{"t":"Guidelines 07/2020 on the concepts of controller and processor","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2023-10/EDPB_guidelines_202007_controllerprocessor_final_en.pdf","l":"Part II, paragraphs 112-114 and 143-145"}],"x":["This checklist is not a finding that any named service complies."],"unk":["No vendor, contract, service plan or actual configuration was provided."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-16","q":"Does a broad government-order exception automatically invalidate a DPA?","p":"EDPB says broad legal-order wording does not infringe Article 28(3)(a) by itself. It does not excuse GDPR compliance, notification duties or transfer requirements.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA"],"src":[{"t":"Opinion 22/2024 on reliance on processors and sub-processors","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-10/edpb_opinion_202422_relianceonprocessors-sub-processors_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 122-124 and 128"}],"x":["Do not classify every broad legal-compulsion clause as automatically lawful or unlawful."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-M01","q":"Does a signed agreement end the processor-guarantees duty?","p":"Article 28(1) permits only processors providing sufficient guarantees for GDPR-compliant measures and protection of people's rights. EDPB paragraph 99 interprets this as a continuing duty. Signing a contract or another legal act does not end it. Verify guarantees at appropriate intervals, with audits or inspections where appropriate.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 28(1)"},{"t":"Guidelines 07/2020 on the concepts of controller and processor","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2023-10/EDPB_guidelines_202007_controllerprocessor_final_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 94-99, especially paragraph 99, printed p. 31"}],"x":["A signed DPA is not conclusive proof of adequate guarantees"],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-PROCESSOR-M02","q":"Is a silently updated sub-processor list sufficient?","p":"For general written authorisation, Article 28(2) requires notice of intended additions or replacements and an opportunity to object. EDPB paragraph 128 says the processor must actively indicate or flag changes. A web list changed without a targeted notification does not meet that interpretation.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 28(2)"},{"t":"Guidelines 07/2020 on the concepts of controller and processor","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2023-10/EDPB_guidelines_202007_controllerprocessor_final_en.pdf","l":"Paragraph 128 and footnote 54, printed pp. 37-38"}],"x":["Specific authorisation needs its separate prior-authorisation process"],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{}],"gaps":["No actual vendor contracts, sub-processor lists or technical settings were examined.","Territorial applicability for an operator outside the EEA must be assessed for each processing activity.","This scoped review does not determine contract enforceability, liability caps or national contract-law issues.","No independent legal review was requested. Guidance summaries are not binding court rulings."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS","name":"GDPR: people's rights","scope":"EU","summary":"Record rights requests when received and act without undue delay, normally within one month. Stop direct marketing when the person objects. Check the conditions for erasure, retention and any refusal. An abuse refusal is exceptional and needs proof. Give intelligible access copies and explanations for relevant automated decisions. The new cross-border enforcement regulation applies from 2 April 2027 and does not change today's controller response deadline.","claims":[{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-01","q":"How should a rights request enter the workflow?","p":"Facilitate requests under Articles 15-22. Recognise the substance of the request even if it does not name the GDPR. EDPB access guidance says an access request has no prescribed form. Log receipt, identify the right or rights requested, and route the request to the controller responsible for the activity. Logging and routing are implementation steps inferred from these duties.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 12(1)-(2), 5(2), 28(3)(e)"},{"t":"Guidelines 01/2022 on data subject rights: Right of access","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2023-04/edpb_guidelines_202201_data_subject_rights_access_v2_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 47-57, section 3.1, printed pp. 21-24"}],"x":["A preferred form does not justify ignoring a clear request received through an appropriate channel"],"unk":["No intake process or staff routing was inspected"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-02","q":"How long does the controller have to respond?","p":"Respond without undue delay and within one month of receiving an Article 15-22 request. Where necessary because of complexity or number of requests, the period can extend by two further months. Explain the extension and reasons within the first month. If taking no action, explain why within one month and state the complaint and judicial-remedy routes.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 12(3)-(4)"}],"x":["One month is not a general permission to postpone action","An automatic three-month response policy does not match Article 12(3)"],"unk":["Specific national restrictions and unusual time-calculation facts remain unassessed"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-03","q":"How should the time limit be calculated?","p":"For access requests, EDPB guidance uses a calendar-month calculation, not a fixed 30-day period. If the next month lacks the corresponding date, use its last day. The guidance addresses weekends, holidays and narrow suspensions for necessary identity information or qualifying clarification under recital 63. Do not build a routine pause for every clarification request.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 01/2022 on data subject rights: Right of access","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2023-04/edpb_guidelines_202201_data_subject_rights_access_v2_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 157-162 and Examples 31-33, printed pp. 50-51"}],"x":["The controller's internal reading delay does not postpone receipt","Routine extra questions do not establish a right to pause"],"unk":["National receipt rules and a disputed suspension require specific review"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-04","q":"How much identity evidence can be required?","p":"Where there are reasonable doubts about identity, Article 12(6) allows information necessary to confirm it. Data minimisation still applies. Article 11 does not require collecting extra identifying information solely to comply when the original purposes do not require identification. If the person supplies information that enables identification, reassess the request.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 5(1)(c), 11(1)-(2), 12(2), 12(6)"}],"x":["Do not demand a passport automatically for every request","Pseudonymous data are not automatically outside the rights process"],"unk":["Identity checks must reflect the risk of wrongful disclosure or deletion"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-05","q":"What does access require?","p":"Confirm whether the person's data are processed. If they are, provide access to the data and the information required by Article 15, including purposes, categories, recipients, retention, rights, source information, relevant automated decisions and transfer safeguards. Supply a copy. For an electronic request, use a commonly used electronic form unless the person asks otherwise. Protect other people's rights when providing the copy.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 15(1)-(4)"},{"t":"Guidelines 01/2022 on data subject rights: Right of access","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2023-04/edpb_guidelines_202201_data_subject_rights_access_v2_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 17-23 and 37, printed pp. 12-13 and 18"}],"x":["A link to the privacy notice alone is not access to the person's data","Access and machine-readable portability are distinct rights"],"unk":["The scope of documents needed to make a copy intelligible depends on the case"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-06","q":"Can an access response always give only recipient categories?","p":"When a person seeks the identity of recipients, provide actual identities where their data have been or will be disclosed. The CJEU allows categories instead where identification is impossible or the controller shows a manifestly unfounded or excessive request. This access rule differs from the general notice wording in Articles 13 and 14.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"RW v Österreichische Post AG, C-154/21, ECLI:EU:C:2023:3","u":"https://juris.curia.europa.eu/juris/document/document.jsf?docid=269146&doclang=en","l":"Paragraphs 36, 43, 48-51 and operative ruling"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 15(1)(c), 12(5)"}],"x":["The operator cannot choose categories merely because they are easier to provide"],"unk":["Unknown future recipients and any refusal grounds need factual evidence"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-07","q":"When must personal data be erased?","p":"Erase without undue delay when an Article 17(1) ground applies, subject to Article 17(3). Grounds include data no longer needed, withdrawal with no other legal ground, a qualifying objection, unlawful processing, a legal erasure duty, and data collected in the Article 8(1) setting. An erasure request requires a purpose-by-purpose assessment.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 17(1)(a)-(f) and 17(3)"}],"x":["An account-deletion button cannot determine every legal retention duty"],"unk":["Applicable retention laws and case-specific exceptions are not established"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-08","q":"When can retention continue despite an erasure request?","p":"Article 17(3) permits necessary processing for expression and information, specified legal or public duties, qualifying public-health purposes, qualifying archives or research, and legal claims. Keep only data actually needed for the applicable exception. A generic possibility of future litigation does not by itself demonstrate necessity. Explain a refusal or partial refusal under Article 12(4).","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 17(3)(a)-(e), 5(1)(c) and (e), 12(4)"}],"x":["Keeping invoices under a valid duty does not authorise continued marketing","Calling all records 'legal evidence' does not establish the exception"],"unk":["Backup expiry and legal-claim retention require implementation and jurisdiction review"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-09","q":"How do correction, restriction and recipient updates work?","p":"Correct inaccurate data without undue delay and complete incomplete data where required. Article 18 permits restriction for disputed accuracy, unlawful use where the person opposes deletion, the person's legal claims, or verification of a qualifying objection. During restriction, uses beyond storage need an Article 18(2) permission. Tell the person before lifting restriction. Notify recipients of correction, erasure or restriction unless impossible or disproportionate, and identify those recipients if asked.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 16, 18(1)-(3), 19"}],"x":["Restriction is not the same operation as deletion","Disagreement about accuracy is not permission to keep using the disputed data without assessing Article 18"],"unk":["Recipient mapping and restrictions in supplier systems are untested"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-10","q":"When does portability apply?","p":"Article 20 covers personal data the person provided where processing is automated and based on consent or contract. Provide it in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable format. Transfer directly to another controller where technically feasible. Respect other people's rights. Portability does not replace the separate erasure assessment.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 20(1)-(4)"}],"x":["An activity based only on legitimate interests does not meet Article 20(1)(a)","Portability does not automatically erase the source data"],"unk":["Borderline observed versus inferred data and formats need system-specific assessment"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-11","q":"What happens after an objection to direct marketing?","p":"Article 21(2)-(3) lets a person object at any time to direct-marketing processing, including related profiling. The controller must stop that marketing use. There is no overriding-commercial-interest test for continuing it. Bring the objection right to the person's attention clearly and separately by the first communication. Assess any separate erasure request and other retained purposes separately.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 21(2)-(5), 17(1)(c) and 17(3)"}],"x":["Article 12's response window is not permission to continue marketing while waiting","An objection to marketing does not automatically invalidate necessary non-marketing contract processing"],"unk":["Systems must be checked to prevent further sends, audience uploads and marketing profiling"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-12","q":"How does a non-marketing objection differ?","p":"For Article 6(1)(e) or (f) processing, a person can object on grounds linked to their situation. Stop that processing unless the controller demonstrates overriding compelling legitimate grounds or processing for legal claims. Article 18(1)(d) gives a restriction right while the competing grounds are checked. Article 21(6) separately addresses qualifying research or statistics, with a public-interest-task exception.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 21(1), 21(6), 18(1)(d)"}],"x":["An ordinary legitimate-interest assessment does not automatically defeat a later objection","Consent withdrawal is a separate mechanism under Article 7(3)"],"unk":["No competing-interest determination was made for an actual case"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-13","q":"What limits apply to solely automated decisions?","p":"Article 22 protects a person from a decision based only on automated processing that has legal or similarly significant effects. Exceptions require contractual necessity, authorising EU or Member State law with safeguards, or explicit consent. Contract and consent exceptions require safeguards including human intervention, the person's view and a way to contest the decision. Special-category data face the additional Article 22(4) limit.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 22(1)-(4)"}],"x":["Ordinary automation without a qualifying decision or effect does not alone trigger Article 22(1)","A nominal human rubber stamp should not be assumed to remove the automated-decision issue"],"unk":["Meaningful human involvement and explanation detail require system-specific review"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-14","q":"May the controller charge, refuse or keep a suppression record?","p":"Rights handling is normally free. Article 12(5) allows a reasonable administrative fee or refusal for a demonstrably manifestly unfounded or excessive request. Article 15(3) separately permits a reasonable fee for further copies. A narrowly limited suppression record can help prevent renewed marketing after an objection. That is an implementation inference and needs its own documented basis, minimisation and retention limits.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 12(4)-(5), 15(3), 21(3), 5(1)(b)-(e), 6(1)"}],"x":["A suppression list cannot become an active marketing audience","High effort alone is not a complete refusal ground","Do not keep full marketing profiles merely to record an opt-out"],"unk":["Suppression architecture and retention must be assessed","Union or national restrictions under Article 23 need separate identification"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-M01","q":"Can a first access request exceptionally be excessive?","p":"Brillen Rottler, C-526/24, allows a first access request to be excessive only exceptionally, with the controller bearing the burden of proving abuse from all relevant circumstances. The objective and subjective abuse elements must be established. In the case described, the controller must show unequivocally an intention to manufacture compensation conditions rather than obtain access for GDPR protection. Request volume or seeking compensation alone does not establish that test. Public evidence of a repeated pattern needs other relevant supporting material.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Brillen Rottler, C-526/24","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62024CJ0526","l":"Paragraphs 24-45, especially 29-36 and 40-45, operative ruling 1"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 12(5)"}],"x":["A first request is not automatically abusive","A compensation claim alone is not sufficient"],"unk":["No actual refusal has been assessed"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-M02","q":"What explanation must an automated-decision access response give?","p":"Under Article 15(1)(h), Dun & Bradstreet Austria requires an intelligible explanation of the procedure and principles actually applied to the person's data. An algorithm or exhaustive technical steps alone are insufficient. If the controller claims trade secrets or protected third-party data, it must give the allegedly protected information to the competent supervisory authority or court for a case-specific balance. Those interests do not permit a blanket refusal.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Dun & Bradstreet Austria, C-203/22","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62022CJ0203","l":"Paragraphs 57-63 and 74-76, operative rulings 1-2"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 15(1)(h), 22(1)-(4)"}],"x":["Complexity alone does not remove the explanation duty"],"unk":["No decision system or disputed access request was supplied"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-M03","q":"Can effective access require document or database extracts?","p":"C-487/21 requires a faithful, intelligible reproduction of the personal data. This can require document extracts, whole documents or extracts from databases where essential for effective exercise of GDPR rights. Balance other people's rights and freedoms. An abstract summary is not automatically sufficient, and there is no unconditional entitlement to every whole document or database.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"CRIF, C-487/21","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62021CJ0487","l":"Paragraphs 39-45 and operative ruling 1"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 15(3)-(4)"}],"x":["The judgment does not grant a general right to complete databases"],"unk":["The necessary scope depends on the records"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-M04","q":"What follow-up applies when data made public must be erased?","p":"If a controller made personal data public and Article 17(1) obliges it to erase them, Article 17(2) requires reasonable steps, including technical measures, to inform other controllers processing those data of the person's request to erase links, copies or replications. Available technology and implementation cost must be considered.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 17(2), read with Article 17(1) and (3)"}],"x":["This does not guarantee removal of every copy"],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-RIGHTS-M05","q":"When does the new cross-border enforcement procedure apply?","p":"Regulation (EU) 2025/2518 adds procedures for GDPR cross-border enforcement. Adopted on 26 November 2025 and published on 12 December 2025, it entered into force on 1 January 2026 but applies from 2 April 2027. Article 36 applies Chapters III-IV to ex officio investigations opened after that date and complaint-based investigations where the complaint was lodged after that date. Chapters V-VI apply to cases referred to Article 65 dispute resolution or Article 66(2)-(3) urgency procedures after that date. It does not replace controllers' current Article 12 response clock or Article 33 breach deadline.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2025/2518 on additional procedural rules relating to GDPR enforcement","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2025/2518/oj/eng","l":"Publication heading and Articles 1, 36-37"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 12(3)-(4), 33(1)"}],"x":["Not an applicable deadline change on 7 November 2026"],"unk":["Future case allocation requires the actual transition facts"]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["No live rights workflow or supplier response process was tested.","National Article 23 restrictions, court procedures and sector-specific retention duties remain outside this family.","The cited EDPB clock-suspension interpretation concerns access requests. Do not turn it into an automatic pause for every right or clarification.","Backup erasure, suppression retention and recipient notifications need concrete system design and evidence.","C-526/24, C-203/22 and C-487/21 were checked for the added claims. This remains a bounded review, not an exhaustive case-law survey.","All claims are research candidates. No legal review or publication approval has occurred."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE","name":"GDPR: does it apply?","scope":"EU","summary":"Check each use of personal data separately. Processing connected to an EU establishment can be covered even when the people or processing are elsewhere. Without an EU establishment, an intentional offer to people in the EU or monitoring their behaviour there can bring related processing into scope. A website visit alone is insufficient. These scope claims use the EU rules. EEA adaptations remain unchecked.","claims":[{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-01","q":"What is the unit of the scope assessment?","p":"Assess each personal-data processing activity. The same operator can have activities inside GDPR territorial scope and other activities outside it. First check material scope, including whether the information is personal data and whether Article 2 covers the processing.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 2(1)-(2), 3 and 4(1)"},{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Version 2.1, printed p. 5, Introduction, paragraph beginning 'The EDPB underlines'"}],"x":["Truly anonymous information is not personal data","Purely personal or household activity has an Article 2(2)(c) exclusion"],"unk":["Identifiability and household exclusions need factual assessment"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-02","q":"Does an EU establishment bring processing into scope?","p":"Article 3(1) covers processing in the context of an EU establishment's activities, even if the processing occurs elsewhere. Establishment requires real and effective activity through stable arrangements. Incorporation and the server location do not settle this test. Check the link between the establishment's work and the processing.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 3(1)"},{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Version 2.1, section 1(a), printed p. 6, paragraph beginning 'While the notion of', and section 1(b), printed pp. 7-8"}],"x":["An EU establishment does not automatically bring unrelated processing into scope"],"unk":["Fact-sensitive establishment and connection assessment"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-03","q":"When does offering goods or services trigger Article 3(2)(a)?","p":"A controller or processor without an EU establishment falls within Article 3(2)(a) for processing related to an offer of goods or services to people in the EU. Payment is not required. The EDPB looks for an intended offer to people in the EU and assesses the person’s location when the offer occurs. Citizenship alone does not decide scope.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 3(2)(a)"},{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Version 2.1, section 2(a)-(b), printed pp. 14-17, paragraphs beginning 'The wording of Article 3(2)' and 'The requirement that the data subject'"}],"x":["An EU citizen outside the EU is not enough by itself","A free service is not exempt"],"unk":["Article 3(1) must still be checked separately"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-04","q":"Which facts indicate an offer aimed at the EU?","p":"Assess targeting indicators together. Examples include EU delivery, advertising aimed at an EU audience, EU country domains, and EU languages or currencies with ordering options. References to EU customers can also matter. A single indicator does not always establish targeting.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Version 2.1, section 2(b), printed pp. 17-18, indicator list and paragraph beginning 'As already mentioned'"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 3(2)(a)"}],"x":["Use of English alone by an English-speaking non-EU business is not enough","No single tick-box score replaces the combined assessment"],"unk":["Weight of indicators in the operator's actual circumstances"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-05","q":"When is website accessibility insufficient?","p":"A website being accessible from the EU does not by itself show an offer aimed at people there. An accessible email address or other contact details is also insufficient alone. An incidental EU visitor or customer does not automatically establish Article 3(2)(a). Check establishment and monitoring separately before concluding that the activity is outside scope.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Version 2.1, section 2(b), printed p. 18, paragraph beginning 'It is however important to recall'"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 3(1)-(3)"}],"x":["A domestic-only non-EU site with no EU establishment, no EU-targeted offer and no EU behavioural monitoring is not brought in merely by an EU visit"],"unk":["Incidental sales need evidence and may change into deliberate targeting over time"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-06","q":"When does monitoring trigger scope?","p":"Article 3(2)(b) covers related processing when a non-established operator monitors people who are in the EU and their behaviour occurs there. The EDPB considers the purpose of collection and subsequent behavioural analysis. Tracking for behavioural advertising, fingerprinting and individual profiling can qualify. Not every online collection or analysis automatically amounts to monitoring.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 3(2)(b)"},{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Version 2.1, section 2(c), printed pp. 19-20, paragraphs beginning 'For Article 3(2)(b)' and 'However, the use of the word', and monitoring list"}],"x":["A bare assertion that analytics are anonymous does not establish an exclusion","Collection without monitoring does not itself trigger Article 3(2)(b), but other routes may apply"],"unk":["Actual analytics and logging configurations must be inspected"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-07","q":"Does using an EU processor make a foreign customer subject to the GDPR?","p":"Using an EU-established processor does not alone bring a non-EU controller into territorial scope. The processor's own EU activities can still be covered. Conversely, an overseas processor's work related to its customer's EU-targeted offer or monitoring can fall within Article 3(2). Assess each actor's processing separately.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Version 2.1, section 1(d), printed pp. 10-12, especially Example 7 on p. 12, and section 2(d), printed pp. 20-21"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 3(1)-(2), 28(1) and 28(3)"}],"x":["EU hosting alone is not an automatic GDPR scope rule for its overseas customer"],"unk":["Processor contract duties and international transfers need separate assessment"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-08","q":"What other territorial route and follow-up requirement matter?","p":"Article 3(3) also covers a controller in a place where a Member State's law applies through public international law. If Article 3(2) applies, Article 27 normally requires a written appointment of an EU representative. Its exception for occasional processing requires all listed conditions, including the limits on sensitive data and likely risk. A small business is not automatically exempt.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 3(3), 27(1)-(3)"}],"x":["Public authorities or bodies have an Article 27(2)(b) exception","Article 27 does not create a general small-business exemption"],"unk":["Representative location and the full risk assessment remain operator-specific"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-09","q":"Can the product turn scope into a single worldwide yes/no label?","p":"Use an activity record for contact enquiries, orders, newsletters and analytics. Record establishment, audience location, targeting and monitoring facts for each activity. An out-of-scope conclusion for one activity must not be copied to another. This is a proposed research-to-product method inferred from Article 3 and the EDPB activity-specific approach.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 3(1)-(3)"},{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Version 2.1, Introduction, printed p. 5, paragraph beginning 'The EDPB underlines'"}],"x":["An 'EU customer' country field alone cannot settle all activities"],"unk":["No site configuration or operator evidence was supplied"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-M01","q":"Can the EU-establishment route cover people outside the EU?","p":"Article 3(1) can cover processing about people outside the EU when it occurs in the context of an EU establishment's activities. EDPB Example 4 applies it to a French company's service for customers in Morocco, Algeria and Tunisia. The Article 3(2) requirement that the person be in the EU must not be imported into Article 3(1).","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 3(1)"},{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Section 1(c), printed pp. 8-9, Example 4"}],"x":["This does not make all unrelated processing by the organisation covered"],"unk":["The factual establishment link remains operator-specific"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-SCOPE-M02","q":"Does a language used in both the EU and the operator's country establish targeting?","p":"A language generally used in the operator's own third country is insufficient by itself to establish an intended offer to people in the EU. The rule is not limited to English. French used by a Canadian operator is an illustration of the same condition. Assess other targeting indicators together.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Section 2(b), printed p. 17, paragraph beginning 'The recital further specifies'"}],"x":["The French-in-Canada example illustrates the general wording and is not a quoted EDPB example"],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["These claims address the EU wording of Article 3. EEA incorporation and national adaptations for Iceland, Liechtenstein and Norway were not verified in this task. Do not equate Europe with the EU.","No operator-specific establishment, targeting or analytics evidence was supplied.","EDPB scope PDF has no numbered paragraphs. Exact locators use its printed pages, section headings and paragraph openings.","National sector rules, international transfer safeguards and representative arrangements require separate work.","Current-version check is bounded to the retrieved EUR-Lex consolidation and procedure register on 2026-11-07. Recheck before publication."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER","name":"GDPR: transfers outside the EEA","scope":"EU","summary":"Map where personal data goes and who can access it. Decision 2023/1795 provides the DPF route for covered EEA-origin transfers to actively certified US organisations. Verify the recipient and data scope. DPF remains subject to litigation and continuing monitoring, including the EDPB's July 2026 assessment request. That request does not invalidate the decision. Other transfers need their own applicable route and assessment.","claims":[{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-01","q":"What makes a data flow an international transfer?","p":"EDPB requires three elements. The exporter is subject to GDPR for the processing, makes personal data available to another entity, and the importer is in a third country or is an international organisation.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 05/2021 on Article 3 and Chapter V","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2023-02/edpb_guidelines_05-2021_interplay_between_the_application_of_art3-chapter_v_of_the_gdpr_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 9-13"}],"x":["The importer's own GDPR coverage does not remove Chapter V.","A person's direct disclosure to a controller is not automatically an exporter-to-importer transfer."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-02","q":"What is the starting legal requirement?","p":"Chapter V applies to covered transfers and onward transfers. Other GDPR requirements still apply. Adequacy, appropriate safeguards and specific derogations are distinct routes.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 44, 45(1), 46(1) and 49(1)"}],"x":["A lawful basis for processing does not itself supply a transfer mechanism."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-03","q":"What is the correct DPF decision?","p":"The DPF adequacy decision is Commission Implementing Decision 2023/1795. Regulation 2023/1543 concerns electronic evidence in criminal proceedings.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2023/1795 on the EU-US Data Privacy Framework","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2023/1795/oj/eng","l":"Title and Article 1"},{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2023/1543 on electronic evidence","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32023R1543","l":"Title"}],"x":["The supplied 2023/1543 citation must not be used for DPF adequacy."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-04","q":"What does DPF adequacy cover?","p":"Article 1 covers personal data transferred from the Union to US organisations on the Department of Commerce DPF List. It does not declare all US recipients adequate.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2023/1795 on the EU-US Data Privacy Framework","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2023/1795/oj/eng","l":"Article 1 and Annex I, Section I.3"}],"x":["A US business address or an expired Privacy Shield entry is insufficient."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-05","q":"Can a non-EEA operator assume DPF covers its transfer?","p":"EDPB's July 2023 note excludes transfers by entities outside the EU subject to Article 3(2) from this adequacy decision's scope. Its 2026 FAQ addresses EEA exporters.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Information note on transfers to the US after the adequacy decision","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2023-07/edpb_informationnoteadequacydecisionus_en.pdf","l":"Question 1, footnotes 2 and 5"},{"t":"EU-US Data Privacy Framework FAQ for European businesses","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2026-04/edpb_dpf_faq-for-businesses_v2_en.pdf","l":"Page 1, footnote 1, and Question 3"}],"x":["An operator serving EU users from another country must not receive an automatic DPF green light."],"unk":["Apply the geographic limit to the actual arrangement with qualified advice if facts are mixed."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-06","q":"What did Latombe decide?","p":"The General Court dismissed the DPF annulment action on 3 September 2025. Its assessment concerned legality at adoption, not a permanent guarantee about later US developments.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Latombe v Commission, T-553/23","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/PDF/?uri=CELEX:62023TJ0553","l":"Paragraphs 15, 22, 58 and 204, operative part point 1"}],"x":["This was a General Court judgment, not a CJEU appeal judgment."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-07","q":"What is the appeal's checked status?","p":"The official CURIA case list displays C-703/25 P as 'Case in progress' on 7 November 2026. The appeal was lodged on 31 October 2025. The order of 4 June 2026 admitted Microsoft as an intervener supporting the Commission. It did not decide the appeal's merits.","k":"inference","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Latombe v Commission, C-703/25 P, case register","u":"https://juris.curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-703/25&language=en","l":"Case C-703/25 P, displayed status 'Case in progress', checked 2026-11-07"},{"t":"Order of the President in C-703/25 P","u":"https://juris.curia.europa.eu/juris/document/document.jsf?cid=441301&dir=&docid=312430&doclang=FR&mode=req&occ=first&pageIndex=0&part=1&text=","l":"Paragraphs 1-2 and operative part points 1-3"}],"x":["Do not describe the June order as an annulment or final merits endorsement."],"unk":["The register is a checked snapshot. Later court events can change it."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-08","q":"Is DPF currently available?","p":"EUR-Lex marks Decision 2023/1795 in force, and the Commission adequacy directory still lists the DPF. The checked appeal register shows C-703/25 P in progress. This supports a dated inference that DPF remains available within its scope. The Commission's first periodic review of 9 October 2024 envisaged the next review after three years alongside continuing monitoring. The EDPB's letter of 31 July 2026 asks the Commission to assess the effects of Trump v. Slaughter. Neither that request nor a pending appeal itself suspends or annuls the decision. Continuing monitoring is separate from judicial review of legality at adoption.","k":"inference","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2023/1795 on the EU-US Data Privacy Framework","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2023/1795/oj/eng","l":"EUR-Lex document status, Article 1 and Article 3(1), (4)-(5)"},{"t":"Latombe v Commission, C-703/25 P, case register","u":"https://juris.curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-703/25&language=en","l":"Case C-703/25 P, displayed status 'Case in progress', checked 2026-11-07"},{"t":"First periodic review of the EU-US Data Privacy Framework, COM(2024) 451 final","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:52024DC0451","l":"Section 5"},{"t":"Letter to Commissioner McGrath on Trump v. Slaughter","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2026-08/edpb_letter_20260731_us_supremecourt_judgment_trump_v_slaughter_en.pdf","l":"Page 2 final substantive paragraph"},{"t":"Adequacy decisions","u":"https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/international-dimension-data-protection/adequacy-decisions_en","l":"DPF and first periodic review entries"}],"x":["This is not a forecast that DPF will survive all future challenges."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-09","q":"How must a US vendor's certification be checked?","p":"EDPB says to check the official DPF List for active certification, its scope and covered subsidiaries. Certifications renew annually. Inactive participants cannot receive new transfers on this basis.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"EU-US Data Privacy Framework FAQ for European businesses","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2026-04/edpb_dpf_faq-for-businesses_v2_en.pdf","l":"Question 3, page 4, and Question 3.1, page 5"}],"x":["A vendor badge or parent company's name is not enough."],"unk":["The live DPF register returned a JavaScript shell in this retrieval. No named vendor was certified by this research."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-10","q":"What if employment data is transferred?","p":"Before an EEA exporter transfers HR data under the DPF, the US recipient must meet either of two alternatives. It must hold active certification covering that information as HR data. Alternatively, it must hold active certification covering the information as another personal-data type and commit in its privacy policy to cooperate and comply with EU DPA advice for that data. In either case, the exporter must tell the importer that HR data is included.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"EU-US Data Privacy Framework FAQ for European businesses","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2026-04/edpb_dpf_faq-for-businesses_v2_en.pdf","l":"Question 3, page 4, first paragraph and footnotes 10-11"}],"x":["HR means human resources here, not Croatia."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-11","q":"What does DPF not replace?","p":"DPF-covered transfers need no Article 46 tool or supplementary transfer measures. Article 28 processor terms and the other GDPR obligations remain.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Information note on transfers to the US after the adequacy decision","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2023-07/edpb_informationnoteadequacydecisionus_en.pdf","l":"Question 1, including text on page 2"},{"t":"EU-US Data Privacy Framework FAQ for European businesses","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2026-04/edpb_dpf_faq-for-businesses_v2_en.pdf","l":"Question 3, final paragraph, and Question 3.3"}],"x":["Certification does not establish a lawful processing purpose, valid cookie consent or suitable security."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-12","q":"What if no adequacy route covers the transfer?","p":"Article 46 requires safeguards, enforceable rights and effective remedies. Article 47 governs approved binding corporate rules. A generic vendor privacy promise is insufficient.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 46(1)-(3) and Article 47(1)-(2)"}],"x":["Binding corporate rules are not a blanket route for unrelated vendors."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-13","q":"Which SCC module and scope apply?","p":"Decision 2021/914 offers controller-to-controller, controller-to-processor, processor-to-processor and processor-to-controller modules. Its Article 1(1) covers importers whose relevant processing is outside GDPR scope.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Commission Implementing Decision 2021/914","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2021/914/oj/eng","l":"Article 1(1)-(2), recital 7 and Annex, Clauses 1 and 8 module headings"}],"x":["Do not use these clauses automatically for an importer already subject to GDPR for the same processing."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-14","q":"What must be completed in SCCs?","p":"The Commission says parties must bind themselves, complete the annexes and select the correct module. Describe actual transfers and security measures. Conflicting terms cannot undermine SCC protections.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"New Standard Contractual Clauses, questions and answers","u":"https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/international-dimension-data-protection/new-standard-contractual-clauses-questions-and-answers-overview_en","l":"Questions 6-7, 35 and 39"}],"x":["A link to blank Commission clauses is not a completed transfer contract."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-15","q":"What assessment accompanies the SCCs?","p":"Clause 14 requires a documented assessment of relevant destination laws, practices and transfer circumstances. Clause 16 requires suspension when the importer breaches or cannot comply.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Commission Implementing Decision 2021/914","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2021/914/oj/eng","l":"Annex, Clause 14(a)-(f), Clause 15 and Clause 16(a)-(c)"}],"x":["Clause 14 applies to Module 4 in the combination scenario specified in its heading."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-16","q":"How should supplementary measures be assessed?","p":"EDPB gives six steps. Map transfers, choose a tool, assess laws and practices, add effective measures, complete formalities and reassess. Stop transfers if sufficient protection cannot be achieved.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Recommendations 01/2020 on supplementary transfer measures","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2021-06/edpb_recommendations_202001vo.2.0_supplementarymeasurestransferstools_en.pdf","l":"Sections 2.1-2.6, paragraphs 69-73, and Annex 2, Use Case 6, paragraphs 94-95"}],"x":["Encryption in transit or at rest may not solve access risk where the recipient needs plaintext.","An adequacy-covered transfer follows the adequacy route instead."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-17","q":"Can a cookie banner supply the transfer derogation?","p":"Article 49(1)(a) requires explicit consent to the proposed transfer after risk information. A general cookie choice does not establish those conditions.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 49(1)(a), opening paragraph, and Article 7"}],"x":["Article 49 contains exceptional grounds, not a routine substitute for an available durable mechanism."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-18","q":"Can routine vendor use rely on a contract derogation?","p":"EDPB says Article 49 derogations must be interpreted restrictively. The operator must meet the strict conditions of the selected ground. Routine outsourcing cannot be presumed to qualify.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Recommendations 01/2020 on supplementary transfer measures","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2021-06/edpb_recommendations_202001vo.2.0_supplementarymeasurestransferstools_en.pdf","l":"Section 2.2, paragraphs 24-26"}],"x":["Commercial convenience alone does not establish necessity."],"unk":["Any proposed Article 49 route needs a dedicated ground-specific review."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-19","q":"Does a foreign authority's demand authorise disclosure?","p":"Article 48 addresses recognition or enforcement of foreign demands through international agreements, without excluding other Chapter V grounds. A demand alone does not settle transfer lawfulness.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 48"}],"x":["Refer actual compelled-disclosure cases for specialist assessment."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-20","q":"What should the website ask before choosing a mechanism?","p":"Collect the exporter and importer identities, locations, roles, data categories, storage and remote-access routes. Match each flow to a covered mechanism and record its limits.","k":"inference","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 05/2021 on Article 3 and Chapter V","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2023-02/edpb_guidelines_05-2021_interplay_between_the_application_of_art3-chapter_v_of_the_gdpr_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 9-13 and Examples 7 and 8.1"},{"t":"Recommendations 01/2020 on supplementary transfer measures","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2021-06/edpb_recommendations_202001vo.2.0_supplementarymeasurestransferstools_en.pdf","l":"Section 2.1, paragraphs 8-14"}],"x":["EU hosting alone does not rule out third-country support or onward transfers."],"unk":["No actual data-flow map or vendor documentation was provided."]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-M01","q":"What did the first periodic DPF review establish?","p":"The Commission published the first periodic DPF review on 9 October 2024 as COM(2024) 451 final. Section 5 envisages the next periodic review after three years, alongside ongoing monitoring and consultation on review frequency. That timetable is not a guarantee that adequacy will remain unchanged.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"First periodic review of the EU-US Data Privacy Framework, COM(2024) 451 final","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:52024DC0451","l":"Section 5, concluding paragraphs"},{"t":"Adequacy decisions","u":"https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/international-dimension-data-protection/adequacy-decisions_en","l":"Review entry dated 9 October 2024"}],"x":["Periodic review timing does not prevent earlier monitoring or action"],"unk":["No later completed periodic report was established by these targeted checks"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-M02","q":"What does the EDPB's July 2026 letter mean for DPF?","p":"On 31 July 2026, the EDPB asked the Commission to assess whether Trump v. Slaughter affects the functioning of Decision 2023/1795. It highlighted the role of independent supervisory authorities and requested relevant action and information sharing. The letter is a monitoring request. It does not suspend or annul the adequacy decision.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"Letter to Commissioner McGrath on Trump v. Slaughter","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2026-08/edpb_letter_20260731_us_supremecourt_judgment_trump_v_slaughter_en.pdf","l":"Page 1, date and independence discussion, page 2 final substantive paragraph"}],"x":["The request is not a completed Commission reassessment"],"unk":["A later formal Commission response was not established by these targeted checks"]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-M03","q":"What must the public notice say about transfers?","p":"Where applicable, Articles 13(1)(f) and 14(1)(f) require disclosure of the intended third-country or international-organisation transfer and the existence or absence of a Commission adequacy decision. For the specified safeguard routes, identify the safeguards and how to obtain a copy or where they are available. An internal transfer map alone does not provide this information to people.","k":"law","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 13(1)(f), 14(1)(f)"},{"t":"EU-US Data Privacy Framework FAQ for European businesses","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2026-04/edpb_dpf_faq-for-businesses_v2_en.pdf","l":"Question 3.2, printed p. 5"}],"x":["A disclosure does not itself make a transfer lawful"],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GDPR-TRANSFER-M04","q":"What happens to data already received by an inactive DPF participant?","p":"The EDPB FAQ says a company removed from the DPF List must continue to apply the DPF Principles to personal data received during participation for as long as it retains those data. New transfers cannot rely on an inactive certification and require another applicable Chapter V ground.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU/EEA","US"],"src":[{"t":"EU-US Data Privacy Framework FAQ for European businesses","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2026-04/edpb_dpf_faq-for-businesses_v2_en.pdf","l":"Question 3, printed p. 4, paragraphs on inactive participants"}],"x":["No recipient's live status was verified"],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["The supplied decision number 2023/1543 was incorrect for DPF. This output uses 2023/1795 and records the correction.","The DPF List and Department of Commerce verification page returned JavaScript-only content. Certification-check instructions are supported by the EDPB's 2026 FAQ, but no vendor-specific active record was verified.","EUR-Lex consolidated views 02023D1795-20230920 and 02021D0914-20210607 were blocked by a JavaScript robot check. OJ texts, displayed consolidation dates, in-force indicators and official guidance were inspected. Full comparison against those consolidated views remains open.","The Latombe appeal was pending in the checked official register. Refresh the register and decision status before publication or reliance after 7 November 2026.","The 2026 procedural order was available in French. Its English summary is an unverified translation.","No transfer-impact assessment or country-law review for a specific vendor was performed.","The mechanism for an importer whose same processing is already subject to GDPR needs separate current-instrument research. Do not automatically apply 2021/914.","No independent legal review was requested.","Targeted checks established the 2024 periodic report and July 2026 EDPB letter. No later completed periodic DPF report or formal Commission response was established. This does not prove that none exists."]},{"id":"F-GPSR","name":"Product safety (GPSR)","scope":"EU","summary":"Check whether a consumer product is supplied through commercial activity, including free supply, and identify the operator's role. Pure services and unresolved standalone-software cases need separate classification. Online offers, physical or accompanying information, safety complaints, dangerous-product reporting and recalls have distinct duties. A QR code is not a universal replacement for mandatory information. Marketplace duties need their own assessment.","claims":[{"id":"F-GPSR-01","q":"What products trigger the GPSR?","p":"The GPSR covers products intended for consumers or reasonably likely to be used by them, including some professional products. Market supply must occur in the course of commercial activity, whether paid or free and including products supplied as part of a service. New, used, repaired and reconditioned products can be covered, subject to Article 2 exclusions and specific EU product legislation. An occasional private gift or sale is not covered solely because a consumer could use the item.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Arts. 1(2), 2(1)-(3), 3(1), (6)"}],"x":["A purely professional product with no reasonably foreseeable consumer use","Article 2(2) excluded categories"],"unk":["Product-specific legislation and safety risks."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-02","q":"Are digital-only services outside the GPSR?","p":"A digital-only SaaS service, consultancy service or information website that supplies no consumer product is outside the GPSR on those facts. Recital 17 excludes services themselves. Products supplied or made available during a service can remain covered. Connected goods and safety-relevant software must not be dismissed merely because software is involved.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR original Official Journal text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/oj/eng","l":"Recital 17"},{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Arts. 3(1), 6(1)(b)-(c), (g)-(h)"}],"x":["Pure digital-only services without covered products"],"unk":["Standalone downloadable software and mixed digital products need separate classification. This claim is not a blanket exemption for all software."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-03","q":"What is excluded or governed mainly by other product law?","p":"Article 2(2) excludes categories including medicines, food, feed, specified living organisms, plant-protection products and antiques. Goods clearly marked for repair or reconditioning before use have the Article 2(3) exclusion. Specific EU safety requirements take priority for covered risks. Article 2(1) also disapplies named GPSR chapters for harmonised products, so the duties must be checked provision by provision.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Art. 2(1)-(3)"}],"x":["Do not describe excluded food or medicines as unregulated"],"unk":["Exact overlap for toys, electrical goods, medical devices and other regulated products."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-04","q":"When is a non-EU online offer treated as EU market supply?","p":"Under Article 4, online or other distance-sale offers count as making products available on the EU market where they target EU consumers. Directing activities to one or more Member States is the test. Mere website accessibility does not alone establish targeting according to recital 21. Delivery options, language, currency and marketing can be relevant evidence.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Art. 4"},{"t":"GPSR original Official Journal text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/oj/eng","l":"Recital 21"}],"x":["A website merely accessible in the EU, without targeting evidence"],"unk":["Actual sales configuration and target-country facts."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-05","q":"Is the operator a seller, importer, manufacturer or marketplace?","p":"GPSR duties depend on the operator's role for each product. Manufacturers, importers and distributors have different duties. Selling under an own name or trademark, or making a qualifying substantial modification, can produce manufacturer obligations under Article 13. A marketplace intermediates sales by traders and can also hold an economic-operator role for products it supplies itself.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Art. 3(8)-(14), Arts. 9-13"},{"t":"GPSR original Official Journal text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/oj/eng","l":"Recital 46"}],"x":["A marketplace label does not remove own-sales duties"],"unk":["Dropshipping and fulfilment arrangements need contract and logistics review."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-06","q":"What must an online product offer display?","p":"Article 19 requires the online offer to show the manufacturer's name or trade name and postal and electronic address. For a non-EU manufacturer it must also identify the relevant EU responsible person and their postal and electronic address. It must identify the product with a picture, type and other identifier, and show required warnings or safety information in the language determined by the target Member State.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Art. 19(a)-(d)"}],"x":["A generic footer or contact form alone does not establish compliance with product-specific information"],"unk":["Language and warning requirements for each product and target country."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-07","q":"Does an overseas product need an EU responsible operator?","p":"Article 16 requires an EU-established responsible economic operator before a product covered by that provision is placed on the market. The person and contact information must accompany the product in one of the permitted ways. Article 19 separately requires the applicable responsible person's details in distance-sale offers where the manufacturer is outside the EU. Harmonised products require the relevant Regulation 2019/1020 and sector-law analysis.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Arts. 2(1), 16(1)-(3), 19(b)"}],"x":["Do not assume a non-EU seller's address satisfies the EU-established-operator requirement"],"unk":["Which eligible operator actually accepts and performs the role."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-08","q":"What safety work sits behind an ordinary product listing?","p":"Article 5 requires safe products. Where Chapter III Section 1 applies, Article 9 requires manufacturer risk analysis and technical documentation, including a ten-year retention period. Articles 11-12 give importers and distributors verification, corrective-action and cooperation duties. Article 20 covers notification of qualifying product accidents. A complete product-safety programme cannot be replaced by a website disclaimer.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Arts. 2(1), 5, 9(1)-(3), 11-12, 20"}],"x":["Chapter-scope limits for harmonised products"],"unk":["Technical safety assessment, testing and applicable standards are not completed here."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-09","q":"What independent duties apply to online marketplaces?","p":"Article 22 requires product-safety contact points, registration with the Safety Gate Portal and internal product-safety processes. Marketplaces must act on specified authority orders without undue delay and within two working days. They must process Article 16 DSA product-safety notices without undue delay and within three working days. Article 22(12) requires cooperation, direct recall notifications where applicable and publication of recall information.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Art. 22(1)-(4), (8), (12)"}],"x":["The three-day rule is a notice-processing duty, not a universal automatic takedown rule"],"unk":["Operational workflow and national authority channels."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-10","q":"Do small marketplaces inherit a GPSR size exemption from the DSA?","p":"The GPSR has no general micro or small-business exclusion matching DSA Articles 19 and 29. Its independent marketplace duties therefore require a separate check. Some Article 22 provisions expressly operate for compliance with particular DSA duties, so their interaction with DSA exemptions needs careful provision-level assessment. It is unsafe to infer that all GPSR duties disappear.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Art. 22, especially (1)-(4), (8)-(12)"},{"t":"Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU) 2022/2065","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 19, 29, 2(4)(f)"}],"x":["No transfer of a DSA exemption to every GPSR duty"],"unk":["Specialist interpretation of Article 22(7), (9) and (11) for DSA-exempt providers."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-11","q":"What must happen after a safety recall?","p":"Articles 35-36 require direct consumer notification where affected consumers can be identified and prescribe recall-notice information. Under Article 37, the economic operator responsible for a recall must offer an effective, free and timely remedy. Normally consumers choose between at least two of repair, replacement and refund, subject to the provision's feasibility and proportionality exceptions.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Arts. 35-37"}],"x":["A marketplace's cooperation duty is not automatically the seller's remedy duty"],"unk":["Product-specific recall measures and remedy exceptions."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-12","q":"When did the GPSR apply and what changed in 2026?","p":"The GPSR applies from 13 December 2024. Article 51 preserves sale of products that complied with Directive 2001/95/EC and were placed on the market before that date. The retrieved current consolidation is dated 29 May 2026 and incorporates Regulation (EU) 2024/2748, including internal-market-emergency provisions. It does not replace the main December 2024 application date.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Amendment table, Art. 2(1)(b), Chapter IIa, Arts. 51-52"}],"x":["Manufacture date alone is not first EU market placement"],"unk":["Emergency activation and any product-specific measures were not surveyed."]},{"id":"F-GPSR-13","q":"Can a QR code or online listing replace mandatory product information?","p":"Article 21 permits additional digital information, not a universal substitute for the required product, packaging or accompanying-document information. Check product identification, manufacturer contact, instructions and safety information under Article 9(5)-(7), and responsible-person details under Article 16(3). Statutory location alternatives and language/accessibility conditions still apply.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Articles 9(5)-(7), 16(3), 21"}],"x":["Specific harmonised-product legislation can change the applicable route."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GPSR-14","q":"Which dangerous-product notifications need the Safety Business Gateway?","p":"When a manufacturer considers or has reason to believe its marketed product is dangerous, Article 9(8) requires immediate corrective measures, consumer information and notification through the Safety Business Gateway to authorities where the product was made available. Importers, distributors and marketplaces have their own related triggers and duties. Safety Gate registration, Gateway notifications and consumer recalls are distinct actions.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Articles 9(8), 11(8), 12(4), 22(12)(d)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-GPSR-15","q":"What manufacturer complaint channels and records are required?","p":"Manufacturers must make communication channels publicly available for consumer complaints, accidents and safety issues, taking disability accessibility needs into account. Investigate complaints and accident information about products alleged to be dangerous. Keep an internal register of those complaints, recalls and corrective measures. Store only personal data needed for the investigation, for no longer than necessary and never more than five years after entry.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GPSR consolidated 29 May 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/2026-05-29/eng","l":"Article 9(11)-(13)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{"s":"A subscription-only writing service delivers no goods.","r":"The service itself is outside GPSR. Data, consumer and AI rules may still apply."},{"s":"A small US seller ships own-brand lamps to France.","r":"EU targeting and manufacturer or importer arrangements require GPSR and electrical-product checks."},{"s":"A coaching service supplies a connected exercise device.","r":"The service exclusion does not exclude the device."},{"s":"A small marketplace claims DSA Article 29 relief.","r":"Its independent GPSR contact, notice and recall duties still need assessment."}],"gaps":["No product-specific conformity, testing, recall-template or standards audit.","No national warning-language, penalties or market-surveillance authority census.","Standalone software and mixed digital-product classification remain a specialist gap.","The interaction of DSA-dependent GPSR paragraphs with small-platform exemptions needs legal review.","EEA incorporation and adaptations were not checked."]},{"id":"F-NIS2","name":"NIS2 cybersecurity","scope":"EU","summary":"NIS2 covers specified sectors and entity types, with size rules and important exceptions. A website alone does not trigger it. Check national scope, group size, registration and update filings, incident reporting and recipient communications. Some digital providers also need an EU representative and the binding 2024/2690 requirements. Equivalent sector-specific rules can replace corresponding duties. This is not a verified national compliance result.","claims":[{"id":"F-NIS2-01","q":"What is the basic NIS2 scope rule?","p":"Article 2(1) covers public or private entities of a type in Annex I or II that meet the medium-enterprise category or exceed its ceilings and provide services or carry out activities in the EU. Sector and entity type must be established as well as size. Having a website, using cloud hosting or handling customer data is not itself the scope test.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Art. 2(1), Annexes I and II"}],"x":["No listed entity type and no special scope trigger"],"unk":["National implementation can provide a higher level of cybersecurity under Article 5."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-02","q":"Why are most microbusinesses outside the ordinary size rule?","p":"An autonomous enterprise with fewer than 10 staff and turnover or balance-sheet total no more than EUR 2 million is micro under the incorporated Recommendation. A small enterprise has fewer than 50 staff and turnover or balance-sheet total no more than EUR 10 million. Such enterprises generally fall below Article 2(1), but only after checking linked or partner enterprises and every size-independent trigger.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 2(1)-(4), 3"},{"t":"Commission Recommendation 2003/361/EC","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reco/2003/361/oj/eng","l":"Annex Arts. 2-6"}],"x":["Being below 50 staff is not sufficient where financial or group facts change the classification"],"unk":["Accounting periods and group calculation need source records."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-03","q":"Which sectors and website-related services are listed?","p":"Annex I covers specified entities in energy, transport, banking, financial-market infrastructure, health, drinking water, waste water, digital infrastructure, business-to-business ICT service management, public administration and space. Annex II adds postal and courier services, waste management, chemicals, food, listed manufacturing, digital providers and research. Website-relevant categories include cloud providers, data centres, CDNs, managed service providers, marketplaces, search engines and social-network platforms.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Annex I points 1-11, Annex II points 1-7"}],"x":["Sector labels alone are insufficient without the defined entity activity"],"unk":["Borderline managed-service and digital-provider classifications."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-04","q":"Can SaaS be a cloud computing service?","p":"NIS2 defines cloud computing by on-demand administration and broad remote access to scalable, elastic and shareable computing resources. Recital 33 includes SaaS among cloud-service models. A SaaS operator must test its actual service against that definition, then size and exceptions. Simply buying cloud hosting for an ordinary shop does not make the shop a cloud-service provider.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Art. 6(30), recital 33, Annex I point 8"}],"x":["A customer of a cloud provider is not thereby the provider"],"unk":["Specific SaaS architecture and service model."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-05","q":"Which providers are covered regardless of size?","p":"Article 2(2)(a) covers public electronic communications networks and publicly available electronic communications services, trust service providers, TLD registries and DNS service providers regardless of size. Article 3 classifies qualified trust service providers, TLD registries and DNS providers as essential regardless of size. Non-qualified trust services are also in scope, but not automatically essential on the same basis.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 2(2)(a)(i)-(iii), 3(1)(b)-(c), 3(2), 6(24)-(27), Annex I point 8"}],"x":["Owning a domain or using a third-party certificate is not the same as providing these services"],"unk":["Whether an activity meets the legal service definition."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-06","q":"Can other small entities be designated?","p":"Article 2(2)(b)-(e) includes listed-sector entities that are sole critical providers, whose disruption has specified public-safety or systemic effects, or that have specific national or regional importance. Article 2(3) covers entities identified as critical under Directive 2022/2557 regardless of size. Public-administration rules and national options also require separate checks. A generic small-business exemption cannot override these triggers.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Art. 2(2)(b)-(f), (3), (5), Art. 3(1)(d)-(g)"}],"x":["Do not infer criticality simply because a customer values the service"],"unk":["National designated-entity lists and notices."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-07","q":"Are domain registration services and domain owners treated alike?","p":"Article 2(4) includes entities providing domain-name registration services regardless of size. Article 28 requires registration-data collection, accuracy procedures and lawful access. A registrar or reseller must be distinguished from an ordinary registrant who only owns a domain. Registration-service status alone should not be assumed to impose every essential-entity risk-management duty without checking Articles 2-3 and national law.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 2(4), 3, 6(22), 27-28"}],"x":["Ordinary website domain ownership alone"],"unk":["National implementation and whether another role triggers essential or important entity status."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-08","q":"How can an overseas digital provider be reached?","p":"For the listed digital providers in Article 26(1)(b), jurisdiction normally follows the main EU establishment. A covered provider without an EU establishment that offers services in the EU must appoint an EU representative. Article 26(2) supplies the hierarchy for identifying the main establishment. This rule does not turn every overseas merchant with an EU customer into a NIS2 entity.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Art. 26(1)(b), (2)-(4)"}],"x":["Scope must be established before applying the representative rule"],"unk":["Responsible Member State and national registration route."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-09","q":"What duties follow if the entity is covered?","p":"Articles 20-21 require Member States to impose management oversight and proportionate cybersecurity risk management on essential and important entities. The measures cover incident handling, continuity, supply chains, secure development, vulnerability handling, security evaluation, training, cryptography, access controls and authentication. Certain digital-provider measures are further specified in Implementing Regulation 2024/2690. ENISA's 2025 guidance supports implementation but does not itself decide scope.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 20, 21(1)-(5)"},{"t":"ENISA NIS2 technical implementation guidance","u":"https://www.enisa.europa.eu/publications/nis2-technical-implementation-guidance","l":"Publication overview, 26 June 2025"}],"x":["Not a universal security checklist binding every small website through NIS2"],"unk":["Detailed national and implementing-regulation requirements for the particular entity."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-10","q":"What are the incident-reporting triggers and times?","p":"Article 23 concerns incidents with a significant impact on the service, with significance defined in paragraph 3 and further rules where applicable. The usual sequence is an early warning within 24 hours, notification within 72 hours and a final report within one month after notification, with qualifications for ongoing incidents. Trust service providers have a 24-hour incident-notification rule for significant impacts on trust services.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Art. 23(1), (3)-(4)"}],"x":["Not every bug, failed login or service issue meets the significant-incident threshold"],"unk":["Sector-specific significance thresholds, national process and notification contacts."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-11","q":"Does a customer contract make a small supplier directly subject to NIS2?","p":"Article 21(2)(d) requires covered entities to address supply-chain security. A covered customer's security terms can therefore reach a small supplier contractually. Those terms alone do not prove that the supplier is directly an essential or important entity. Direct statutory scope and contractual duties must be reported separately.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 2-3, 21(2)(d), 21(3)"}],"x":["Do not label a supplier directly regulated solely because a customer requests NIS2 assurances"],"unk":["Actual contractual obligations."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-12","q":"What national dates and implementation must be checked?","p":"Member States had to transpose NIS2 by 17 October 2024 and apply their measures from 18 October 2024. National scope, designation and implementation remain necessary checks. Article 5 permits national provisions ensuring a higher level of cybersecurity, consistent with EU law.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 5 and 41(1)"}],"x":["The EU transposition deadline does not prove every country implemented on time"],"unk":["All country-pack national measures and present enforcement position."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-13","q":"Have the targeted NIS2 amendments or general Omnibus changed scope?","p":"The retrieved EUR-Lex records for the targeted NIS2 amendments, COM(2026) 13, and the separate general Digital Omnibus, COM(2025) 837, both show ongoing procedures. They are proposals, not current exemptions or postponements. This differs from the separate AI Omnibus, which was enacted.","k":"proposal","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Procedure 2026/0012/COD, targeted NIS2 amendments","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/procedure/EN/2026_12","l":"Procedure 2026/0012/COD, status Ongoing"},{"t":"Procedure 2025/0360/COD, general Digital Omnibus","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/procedure/EN/2025_360","l":"Procedure 2025/0360/COD, status Ongoing"}],"x":["No statutory relief inferred from proposals"],"unk":["Monitor for final adoption, OJ publication and commencement."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-14","q":"Are registration and updates separate from incident reporting?","p":"Article 3(3)-(4) requires Member States to list essential/important entities and domain-registration providers and obtain identity, contact, IP-range, sector and service-country information, with changes reported without delay and within two weeks. Article 27 separately specifies a registry for listed digital providers, initial national submissions by 17 January 2025, establishment/representative details and changes without delay and within three months. These are distinct provisions, not a choice of deadline. Check applicable national filings, scope and update rules.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Articles 3(3)-(4), 27(1)-(5)"}],"x":[],"unk":["No national filing portal, implementation or operator registration was verified."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-15","q":"Which numerical size ceilings and ownership rules apply?","p":"The SME upper ceilings are fewer than 250 persons and annual turnover at most EUR 50 million or balance-sheet total at most EUR 43 million. A small enterprise has fewer than 50 persons and turnover or balance-sheet total at most EUR 10 million. Apply partner/linked-enterprise aggregation and the Recommendation's accounting rules. NIS2 Article 2(1) covers qualifying listed medium enterprises or entities exceeding the medium ceilings and disapplies Annex Article 3(4)'s public-ownership exclusion. Headcount alone is insufficient, and size-independent triggers remain.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Commission Recommendation 2003/361/EC","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reco/2003/361/oj/eng","l":"Annex Articles 2-6, especially 2(1)-(2), 3(4)"},{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Article 2(1)-(2)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-NIS2-16","q":"Do equivalent sector-specific rules replace relevant NIS2 duties?","p":"Under Article 4, equivalent sector-specific EU cybersecurity or incident-reporting obligations displace the corresponding NIS2 provisions, including related supervision/enforcement, for the entities covered. Risk measures must be at least equivalent in effect. Reporting equivalence includes the specified authority-access requirements. Financial entities need a DORA assessment rather than assuming identical controls and reports stack unchanged.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Article 4(1)-(3), recital 28"}],"x":[],"unk":["A complete DORA perimeter and obligation comparison was not performed."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-17","q":"What binding instrument adds digital-provider technical and incident rules?","p":"Implementing Regulation 2024/2690 sets technical and methodological risk-management requirements and specifies significant-incident cases for its listed DNS, TLD, cloud, data-centre, CDN, managed-service, managed-security, marketplace, search, social-platform and trust-service providers. Article 2 points to its Annex. It is binding law, distinct from ENISA explanatory guidance. This packet establishes the instrument's scope, not a completed audit of its controls or thresholds.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2024/2690","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg_impl/2024/2690/oj/eng","l":"Articles 1-3 and Annex"}],"x":[],"unk":["Full control catalogue, sector thresholds and operational implementation are not audited."]},{"id":"F-NIS2-18","q":"When must service recipients be informed?","p":"Where appropriate, essential and important entities must notify recipients without undue delay of significant incidents likely to adversely affect their service. Where applicable, tell recipients potentially affected by a significant cyber threat without undue delay what measures or remedies they can take. Inform them of the threat itself where appropriate. These communications are separate from authority reporting and require national implementation checks.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"NIS2 Directive (EU) 2022/2555","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj/eng","l":"Article 23(1)-(2)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{"s":"An independent two-person shop uses Cloudflare DNS for its own site.","r":"Domain ownership and buying DNS do not themselves trigger DNS-provider status."},{"s":"A two-person business provides authoritative DNS services to customers.","r":"The size-independent DNS trigger requires assessment."},{"s":"A micro SaaS is part of a large corporate group.","r":"Its own headcount may not establish a small-enterprise exclusion."},{"s":"A small designer signs a covered bank's security terms.","r":"Contractual obligations can apply without direct NIS2 scope."}],"gaps":["National transposition and scope have not been checked for any of the 30 country packs.","No enterprise-group calculation or critical-entity designation check completed.","No fact-specific DNS, trust-service, cloud or managed-service classification.","Proposals require monitoring. Neither ongoing procedure was treated as enacted relief.","No EEA incorporation assessment.","Technical implementation guidance was checked at overview level, not audited as a complete control catalogue."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR","name":"Sensitive data, children, sectors","scope":"EU","summary":"Escalate children's data, sensitive data, criminal data and significant solely automated decisions before producing a compliance result. The DPO test depends on the actual core activities and scale, including regular systematic monitoring. National child-consent ages require country-specific current evidence. The French wording is sourced but its statutory currency remains unresolved. Medical, financial and legal services need separate perimeter checks. MiCA's maximum Article 143(3) transition has ended. A disclaimer does not resolve these questions.","claims":[{"id":"F-SECTOR-01","q":"When does GDPR Article 8 require parental consent?","p":"Article 8 applies where an information society service is offered directly to a child and the processing relies on Article 6(1)(a) consent. The EU default age for the child's own consent is 16, but national law may lower it to no less than 13. Below the relevant age, consent must be given or authorised by a holder of parental responsibility. Controllers must make reasonable verification efforts.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Art. 8(1)-(2)"},{"t":"EDPB Guidelines 05/2020 on consent","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_202005_consent_en.pdf","l":"Section 7.1, paragraphs 125-127"}],"x":["Article 8 is not a universal ban on processing under-16 data","Other lawful bases still require their own valid conditions"],"unk":["Applicable national law for cross-border services."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-02","q":"Can one age threshold be used as a full cross-border answer?","p":"EDPB consent guidance warns that a cross-border controller cannot always rely only on its main-establishment country's age rule. The relevant national rules may use different connecting factors. The public helper should ask which countries the service reaches and return unresolved national-law checks rather than claim that one EU-wide age resolves every case.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"EDPB Guidelines 05/2020 on consent","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_202005_consent_en.pdf","l":"Section 7.1.3, paragraph 131, printed page 27 / PDF page 27 (index 26)"}],"x":["Not a general minimum account age or contractual-capacity rule"],"unk":["Conflict-of-laws result and national implementation for every target country."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-03","q":"What does the French rule say?","p":"CNIL's regulator-hosted text of French Data Protection Act Article 45 states age 15 for a child's own consent in the Article 8 direct information-society-service setting, with joint consent of the child and parental-authority holder(s) below 15. The page is dated 15 November 2024. This is a sourced wording finding, not verified statutory currency on 7 November 2026 or a choice-of-law result.","k":"law","j":["FR"],"src":[{"t":"CNIL, French Data Protection Act","u":"https://www.cnil.fr/fr/le-cadre-national/la-loi-informatique-et-libertes","l":"Article 45, first two paragraphs"}],"x":["Do not apply to every processing operation involving a child"],"unk":["Current authoritative statutory version on 2026-11-07 remains unverified. Do not return an approved French age result."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-04","q":"What age do Ireland and Austria use in official guidance?","p":"The Irish DPC states that Ireland's digital-consent age is 16. Austria's DSB states that parental authorisation is required below 14 in the Article 8 and section 4(4) DSG setting. These are regulator-guidance findings. They are not a completed national-statute audit or a list of all EU and EEA ages.","k":"official_guidance","j":["IE","AT"],"src":[{"t":"Irish DPC, What is the age of digital consent?","u":"https://www.dataprotection.ie/sites/default/files/uploads/2023-04/DPC_ChildrensData_ParentalConsent.pdf","l":"PDF page 2 (index 1), What is the age of digital consent?"},{"t":"Austrian DSB, Teens and kids","u":"https://dsb.gv.at/ueber-die-datenschutzbehoerde/teens-kids","l":"Teens and kids, paragraph on Art. 8 GDPR and section 4(4) DSG"}],"x":["No inference about unresearched countries"],"unk":["Current statutory texts and national amendments still require cross-checking."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-05","q":"Do children still need protection when Article 8 does not apply?","p":"Article 8(3) preserves national rules on children's contractual capacity. GDPR Articles 5, 6 and 12 still govern lawful, fair and understandable processing outside the specific consent setting. Recital 38 calls for particular protection of children. Replacing consent with an invalid contract or legitimate-interest assertion is not a lawful workaround.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 5, 6, 8(3), 12(1), recital 38"}],"x":["Valid non-consent processing is not automatically prohibited by Article 8"],"unk":["National capacity, children's service and age-assurance rules."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-06","q":"What is special-category data?","p":"Article 9(1) covers data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, trade-union membership, genetic data, biometrics used for unique identification, health data, and sex-life or sexual-orientation data. Processing is prohibited unless an Article 9(2) condition applies. An Article 6 basis is also required. A small operator has no general exemption.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 6, 9(1)-(2), recital 51"}],"x":["Ordinary financial data is not automatically Article 9 data","A photograph is not automatically biometric data used for identification"],"unk":["Whether free text, inferences or model outputs reveal protected attributes."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-07","q":"Is explicit consent always enough for health or sensitive data?","p":"Article 9(2)(a) permits explicit consent subject to its conditions, but EU or national law may prevent lifting the prohibition by consent. Health-care grounds in Article 9(2)(h) depend on the listed purposes and Article 9(3) professional-secrecy conditions. Article 9(4) allows extra national conditions for genetic, biometric and health data. A wellness or medical label does not itself provide a legal basis.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Art. 9(2)(a), (h)-(i), (3)-(4)"}],"x":["A consent checkbox alone does not prove valid explicit consent or lawful processing"],"unk":["National health-data and professional-secrecy requirements."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-08","q":"When should sensitive-data processing trigger a DPIA or DPO review?","p":"Article 35 requires a DPIA where processing is likely to create high risk, including listed large-scale sensitive-data processing and certain automated evaluations. Article 37(1)(b) requires a DPO where core activities require regular and systematic large-scale monitoring. Paragraph (c) covers core activities consisting of large-scale Article 9 data or criminal-conviction/offence data under Article 10. Public-authority and additional national-law triggers also apply. Neither duty follows from headcount alone. Recital 91's individual physician/lawyer example does not remove other high-risk triggers.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 35(1)-(4), 36(1), 37(1)(a)-(c), (4), recital 91"}],"x":["Not every small sensitive-data use automatically requires a DPO","Absence of a DPO trigger does not remove the DPIA test"],"unk":["National mandatory-DPIA lists and risk assessment."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-09","q":"When does a health website or SaaS become a medical-device issue?","p":"Software intended by its manufacturer for specified medical purposes, including diagnosis, prevention, monitoring, prediction, prognosis or treatment of disease, can be a medical device under Article 2(1) MDR. Article 6 reaches devices offered online to people established in the EU. Product claims and intended purpose require review. General information or an administrative booking function alone does not establish the same medical purpose.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Medical Devices Regulation consolidated 19 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02017R0745-20260719","l":"Arts. 2(1), 2(12), 6(1)-(2), Annex VIII Rule 11"},{"t":"MDCG 2019-11 rev.1, software qualification and classification","u":"https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/b45335c5-1679-4c71-a91c-fc7a4d37f12b_en?filename=mdcg_2019_11_en.pdf","l":"Sections 1 and 3, June 2025 revision"}],"x":["Being used in a clinic does not by itself establish medical-device intended purpose"],"unk":["Device classification, evidence, conformity route, professional licensing and transitions."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-10","q":"What financial-service functions require escalation?","p":"Personal recommendations about transactions in financial instruments can be investment advice under MiFID II Article 4(1)(4). Professional investment services generally require prior authorisation under Article 5, subject to scope and exemptions. Automated advice, trade execution and portfolio management therefore need a specialist perimeter review. General educational material is not automatically a personal recommendation, but its actual content and commercial context matter.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"MiFID II consolidated 6 June 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02014L0065-20260606","l":"Arts. 2-5, especially 4(1)(4) and 5(1), Annex I Section A"}],"x":["No claim that every finance blog is an investment firm"],"unk":["National licensing, third-country access, promotions, credit, insurance and DORA analysis."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-11","q":"What payment and crypto functions require escalation?","p":"Providing payment services is distinct from accepting payment as an ordinary merchant. PSD2 Articles 4 and 11 and Annex I require a perimeter check for payment execution, remittance, initiation or account-information services, with specified exemptions and registration routes. MiCA Article 59 similarly restricts providing crypto-asset services to authorised or otherwise entitled persons. Custody, exchange, execution and crypto advice need a dedicated review.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"PSD2 consolidated 17 January 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02015L2366-20250117","l":"Arts. 3, 4(3), 11(1), 32-33, Annex I"},{"t":"MiCA consolidated 9 January 2024","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02023R1114-20240109","l":"Art. 3(1)(16), Arts. 59-61"}],"x":["A checkout using a third-party payment provider does not alone make the merchant a payment institution"],"unk":["PSD3/PSR reform status, MiCA exceptions and transitions, AML duties and national licences require separate review."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-12","q":"When should legal services trigger a national review?","p":"Personalised legal advice, representation, reserved drafting and use of a protected professional title should trigger a country-specific professional-rules review. The e-Commerce Directive preserves professional rules and requires specified professional information online. These provisions do not establish a universal licence to provide legal services, and a disclaimer does not resolve whether the actual activity is reserved.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"e-Commerce Directive 2000/31/EC","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/oj/eng","l":"Arts. 5(1)(e)-(f), 8(1), 8(4), recital 33"},{"t":"Your Europe, regulated professions","u":"https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/work/professional-qualifications/regulated-professions/index_en.htm","l":"What is a regulated profession, national variation warning"}],"x":["A general legal-information article is not automatically representation or a reserved professional service"],"unk":["Reserved activities, bar rules, privilege and professional insurance in each country. Lawyers Directive consolidation retrieval was blocked."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-13","q":"How should the public helper handle other regulated professions?","p":"Official EU guidance explains that regulated professions differ by country and may require qualifications, examinations or professional-body registration. The helper should escalate claimed regulated services and protected titles to the relevant national authority. For covered online professional services, e-Commerce Directive Article 5 requires professional-body, title, issuing-country and applicable-rules information. Recognition of a qualification is not proof that every service or business arrangement is authorised.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Your Europe, regulated professions","u":"https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/work/professional-qualifications/regulated-professions/index_en.htm","l":"What is a regulated profession, national-authority and database guidance"},{"t":"e-Commerce Directive 2000/31/EC","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2000/31/oj/eng","l":"Art. 5(1)(e)-(f), Art. 8"}],"x":["Do not infer licensing solely from a generic word such as consultant"],"unk":["National profession registers, licensing, advertising and insurance duties."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-14","q":"Does a general EU-customer label resolve all sector scope?","p":"Each regime has its own territorial and activity tests. GDPR Article 3 covers EU establishment or specified targeting or monitoring of people in the EU, not EU citizenship alone. The medical-device online-offer rule and investment-service rules use different tests. Triage should collect these facts separately and treat unresolved professional or sector questions as gaps rather than issue a compliant result.","k":"inference","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Art. 3(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Medical Devices Regulation consolidated 19 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02017R0745-20260719","l":"Art. 6"},{"t":"MiFID II consolidated 6 June 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02014L0065-20260606","l":"Arts. 1 and 39-42"}],"x":["EU nationality alone is not the general GDPR targeting test"],"unk":["Cross-border sector-law and conflict-of-laws assessment."]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-15","q":"What separate screen applies to criminal-conviction and offence data?","p":"Article 10 permits Article 6(1)-based processing of criminal convictions, offences and related security measures only under official-authority control or authorisation by EU or Member State law with appropriate safeguards. A comprehensive criminal-conviction register may be kept only under official-authority control. Article 9 explicit consent is not a substitute for this authorisation test.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 6(1), 10"}],"x":["No national authorisation is established for an individual operator."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-16","q":"Does a solely automated significant decision need escalation?","p":"Article 22 concerns decisions based solely on automated processing, including profiling, that produce legal or similarly significant effects. Exceptions require contract necessity, authorisation by EU/national law with safeguards, or explicit consent. Contract and consent exceptions require at least human intervention, an opportunity to express a view and to contest the decision. Special-category decisions additionally require Article 9(2)(a) or (g) and safeguards. An algorithm without a solely automated significant decision does not alone trigger this provision.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 22(1)-(4)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-17","q":"Which separate safeguards govern AI bias processing?","p":"AI Act Article 4a permits exceptional special-category processing only as strictly necessary for the specified bias detection and correction. High-risk providers use paragraph 1. Other-system/model providers and deployers and high-risk deployers use paragraph 2's health, safety, fundamental-rights or unlawful-discrimination conditions. Other or synthetic/anonymised data must be insufficient. Apply technical reuse limits, security and pseudonymisation, strict documented access and confidentiality, no access by other parties, deletion when bias is corrected or retention ends, and records explaining necessity and why alternatives fail. Applicable data-protection rules remain. Paragraph 2 creates no duty to perform bias testing.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"AI Act, consolidated 27 July 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02024R1689-20260727","l":"Article 4a(1)-(2), Article 2(7)"},{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, Digital Omnibus on AI","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng","l":"Article 1(6)"}],"x":["No general training-data or health-data exemption."],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-SECTOR-18","q":"Has MiCA's maximum service-provider grandfathering period ended?","p":"MiCA Article 143(3) permitted eligible providers lawfully operating before 30 December 2024 to continue only until 1 July 2026 or earlier authorisation/refusal. Member States could shorten or disapply that transition. Its maximum period has expired by 7 November 2026. A service cannot rely on that paragraph to operate indefinitely while awaiting authorisation. Check current authorisation or another valid entitlement separately.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"MiCA consolidated 9 January 2024","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02023R1114-20240109","l":"Article 143(3), read with Articles 59 and 63"}],"x":[],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{"s":"A site records a child's data under a valid non-consent legal basis.","r":"Article 8 parental-consent conditions do not automatically apply, but other child-protection duties do."},{"s":"A contact form stores ordinary names and business email addresses.","r":"Article 9 is not triggered on those facts alone. Free-text health disclosures can change the analysis."},{"s":"A general budgeting article gives no personal recommendation or payment service.","r":"Those investment and payment functions are not established simply by the topic."},{"s":"A symptom app claims to diagnose a disease.","r":"Medical-device and health-data review is required despite a wellness disclaimer."},{"s":"A legal blog offers general explanations and no individual case service.","r":"A reserved-service conclusion is not automatic. Adding personalised case advice requires a new review."}],"gaps":["National Article 8 examples cover only FR, IE and AT. The other 27 input country packs remain unresearched for this issue, including IS, LI and NO.","FR statutory wording was read on CNIL. IE and AT findings rely on regulator guidance and need current-statute confirmation.","All national licensing, reserved-activity, insurance and professional-advertising checks remain open.","The Lawyers Directive consolidated-text retrieval returned a JavaScript anti-bot page. No operative claim was extracted from it.","No comprehensive finance-law review. MiCA Article 143(3)'s maximum transition is now extracted, but payment reform, credit, insurance, AML, individual authorisation and DORA still need specialist review.","Medical-device software and professional health-care rules require case-specific classification. MDR and IVDR simplification procedure 2025/0404/COD was still ongoing in the checked record.","No EEA incorporation or cross-border conflict-of-laws census.","No qualified legal reviewer has approved any public-facing text.","F-SECTOR-03: France's Article 45 wording is available on the working CNIL mirror, but the current statutory version remains unverified after Legifrance access failures. Age 15 and joint consent are not refuted, but a definitive current-law age output remains blocked.","F-SECTOR missingClaims[2]: National age outputs remain blocked unless supported by a current country-specific statutory check and the applicable connecting-factor analysis. FR wording and IE/AT guidance examples do not establish a 30-country lookup. The other 27 country packs, including IS/LI/NO, are unverified. Working sources are listed in SECTOR-01 to SECTOR-04."]},{"id":"F-TAX","name":"VAT and invoicing","scope":"EU","summary":"Classify the supply, customer and establishment before deciding VAT treatment. The EUR 10,000 rule combines qualifying cross-border TBE services and intra-EU distance goods sales, excluding VAT. Crossing it changes treatment immediately. OSS reporting and the cross-border SME exemption are separate routes. Check customer-location evidence, Croatia's exemption cessation and invoice rules, and the applicable eRačun phase. This remains an accountant intake framework.","claims":[{"id":"F-TAX-01","q":"Which facts must an accountant establish first?","p":"Classify each supply, customer capacity and establishment before assigning VAT treatment. The general service rules distinguish B2B customer location from B2C supplier location, with exceptions.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"VAT Directive 2006/112/EC, consolidated 14 April 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02006L0112-20250414","l":"Articles 43-45"},{"t":"European Commission, Place of taxation","u":"https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/taxation/vat/vat-directive/place-taxation_en","l":"Supply of services section"}],"x":["No country inferred from website language, hosting or domain"],"unk":["Fixed-establishment analysis and exemptions"]},{"id":"F-TAX-02","q":"Is the offering an electronically supplied service?","p":"An electronic service is essentially automated, needs minimal human intervention and depends on information technology. Email contact alone is insufficient. Individually supplied professional advice and teacher-led online teaching require separate classification.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Implementing Regulation (EU) 282/2011, consolidated 14 April 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011R0282-20250414","l":"Article 7(1), (3)(i)-(j), Annex I"},{"t":"VAT Directive 2006/112/EC, consolidated 14 April 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02006L0112-20250414","l":"Article 58(1), final sentence"}],"x":["Do not treat all online sales as digital services"],"unk":["Mixed packages and live services"]},{"id":"F-TAX-03","q":"Where are B2C electronic services taxed?","p":"Article 58 generally places B2C telecommunications, broadcasting and electronic services at the customer's location. Article 59c's exception requires the supplier to be established, or otherwise permanently addressed or usually resident, in only one Member State. The VAT-exclusive total of qualifying cross-border services plus intra-Community distance goods sales must not exceed EUR 10,000 in either the current or preceding calendar year. Destination treatment applies from the time the threshold is exceeded. Opting into destination treatment binds for at least two calendar years.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"VAT Directive 2006/112/EC, consolidated 14 April 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02006L0112-20250414","l":"Articles 58(1), 59c(1)-(3)"}],"x":["The EUR 10,000 rule is not a worldwide VAT-free allowance","No automatic availability to a non-EU supplier"],"unk":["Customer-location evidence and exact classification"]},{"id":"F-TAX-04","q":"Which OSS scheme, if any, should the accountant consider?","p":"OSS is optional VAT reporting through one Member State. The non-Union scheme covers qualifying services from suppliers with no EU establishment. The Union scheme covers eligible intra-EU distance goods sales and certain B2C services. OSS does not remove domestic return obligations.","k":"official_guidance","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"European Commission, The One Stop Shop","u":"https://vat-one-stop-shop.ec.europa.eu/one-stop-shop_en","l":"Background paragraphs on optional schemes and domestic returns"},{"t":"European Commission, Register to OSS","u":"https://vat-one-stop-shop.ec.europa.eu/one-stop-shop/register-oss_en","l":"Who can register and table of eligible supplies"},{"t":"VAT Directive 2006/112/EC, consolidated 14 April 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02006L0112-20250414","l":"Articles 358a-359, 369a-369b"}],"x":["OSS is not a tax exemption","A payment processor is not automatically the taxable supplier"],"unk":["Applicable scheme, registration date and domestic registrations"]},{"id":"F-TAX-05","q":"Which invoicing and reverse-charge questions remain?","p":"Ask which country's invoice rules apply under Article 219a and whether Article 196 shifts VAT liability to the business customer. Check invoice contents, exemption wording, deadlines and retention under the applicable rules.","k":"proposal","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"VAT Directive 2006/112/EC, consolidated 14 April 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02006L0112-20250414","l":"Articles 196, 219a, 220 and 226"}],"x":["No universal invoice template or blanket reverse charge"],"unk":["Country-specific invoice duties and self-billing arrangements"]},{"id":"F-TAX-06","q":"Which ViDA dates belong in the accountant's review?","p":"ViDA is phased. Conditional domestic e-invoicing options started on 14 April 2025. OSS changes start on 1 January 2027. Major registration and platform changes start on 1 July 2028, with the specified platform rule deferrable to 1 January 2030.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Council Directive (EU) 2025/516","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2025/516/oj/eng","l":"Articles 1-3, 6(1)-(3), 7"},{"t":"European Commission, VAT in the Digital Age","u":"https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/taxation/vat/vat-digital-age-vida_en?prefLang=et","l":"Timeline points 1-3"}],"x":["No claim that all ViDA duties already apply in 2026"],"unk":["National choices and later amendments"]},{"id":"F-TAX-07","q":"What later ViDA dates must remain separate?","p":"Article 4 changes apply on 1 July 2029. Cross-border digital reporting under Article 5 starts on 1 July 2030. The 1 January 2035 alignment deadline is a derogation for qualifying pre-existing domestic systems, not a general business postponement.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Council Directive (EU) 2025/516","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2025/516/oj/eng","l":"Articles 4-5 and 6(4)-(5)"},{"t":"European Commission, VAT in the Digital Age","u":"https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/taxation/vat/vat-digital-age-vida_en?prefLang=et","l":"Timeline points 4-5"}],"x":["No automatic 2035 deadline for each operator"],"unk":["Future implementation details"]},{"id":"F-TAX-08","q":"Which Croatian transactions enter the eRačun regime?","p":"The regime concerns defined domestic transactions between specified issuers and recipients. Article 2(1)(34) requires both parties to have a Croatian seat or residence and a supply within Croatian VAT scope, including exempt supplies. A PDF alone is not a structured eRačun.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o fiskalizaciji, NN 89/2025","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2025_06_89_1233.html","l":"Articles 2(1)(24),(34), 38(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Porezna uprava, eRačun","u":"https://porezna.gov.hr/fiskalizacija/bezgotovinski-racuni/eracun","l":"Opening eRačun definition"}],"x":["No extension to every invoice from a foreign website"],"unk":["Specific special-law exceptions"]},{"id":"F-TAX-09","q":"What applies in Croatia in 2026 and 2027?","p":"From 1 January 2026, covered VAT-registered issuers issue and receive eRačuni. Covered non-VAT recipients receive and fiscalise incoming eRačuni in 2026. Their issuing duty under Article 38(1)(2)-(3) starts on 1 January 2027.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o fiskalizaciji, NN 89/2025","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2025_06_89_1233.html","l":"Articles 2(1)(24), 38(1), 41, 47, 80"},{"t":"Porezna uprava, eRačun","u":"https://porezna.gov.hr/fiskalizacija/bezgotovinski-racuni/eracun","l":"Opening phase paragraphs"}],"x":["Not every non-VAT individual is a covered recipient"],"unk":["The operator's category must be checked"]},{"id":"F-TAX-10","q":"Do payment method and B2C sales require separate checks?","p":"Cash or card transactions may use Article 39's exception if already invoiced and fiscalised under Article 15. Consumer fiscalisation separately includes transaction-account payments from 1 January 2026, subject to statutory exclusions.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o fiskalizaciji, NN 89/2025","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2025_06_89_1233.html","l":"Articles 2(1)(21),(26), 3-4, 39, 80"}],"x":["Bank transfer is not a general fiscalisation escape","Do not duplicate fiscalisation where Article 39 applies"],"unk":["Implementing-rule and software configuration review"]},{"id":"F-TAX-11","q":"What Croatian small-taxpayer VAT threshold should an accountant check?","p":"Article 90 sets a EUR 60,000 domestic annual-turnover threshold for the Croatian small-taxpayer exemption. Other-EU established applicants also face EUR 100,000 Union turnover and notification conditions. Turnover composition, cessation and invoice effects are governed by Articles 90 and 90f-90h.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"VAT Act amendments, NN 152/2024","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2024_12_152_2508.html","l":"Amendment Articles 16-18 and 28, VAT Act Articles 89a-89b, 90, 90f-90h"},{"t":"VAT Act amendment, NN 52/2025","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2025_03_52_673.html","l":"Article 1"},{"t":"VAT Act amendments, NN 151/2025","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2025_12_151_2262.html","l":"Articles 1-12"},{"t":"VAT Act amendment, NN 32/2026","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_03_32_377.html","l":"Article 1"},{"t":"VAT Act supplement, NN 48/2026","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2026_05_48_578.html","l":"Article 1"}],"x":["Not a general exemption from registration, invoicing or reverse-charge duties","Do not equate turnover with profit or money received"],"unk":["Accountant must calculate turnover and dates for actual facts"]},{"id":"F-TAX-12","q":"Does an obrt label answer the tax questions?","p":"It does not establish VAT status or the applicable fiscalisation phase. Ask the accountant to distinguish the person's legal form, income or profit-tax treatment, any lump-sum regime, VAT identification and domestic VAT registration.","k":"proposal","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o obrtu, NN 143/2013, item 3065","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2013_12_143_3065.html","l":"Articles 2 and 4"},{"t":"Zakon o fiskalizaciji, NN 89/2025","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2025_06_89_1233.html","l":"Articles 2(1)(21),(24), 38(1), 80"},{"t":"VAT Act amendments, NN 152/2024","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2024_12_152_2508.html","l":"Amendment Article 17, VAT Act Article 90"}],"x":["No conclusion about any named person's tax liability"],"unk":["Lump-sum income-tax eligibility, contributions and business registration are not researched here"]},{"id":"F-TAX-13","q":"Can live virtual activities use destination taxation?","p":"For non-taxable customers, Article 54(1) places qualifying cultural, artistic, sporting, scientific, educational, entertainment or similar activities that are streamed or otherwise made virtually available at the customer's establishment, permanent address or usual residence. This can apply even where teacher-delivered online courses are excluded from electronic services under Implementing Regulation Article 7(3)(j). Do not automatically classify every individual tuition arrangement as such an activity.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"VAT Directive 2006/112/EC, consolidated 14 April 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02006L0112-20250414","l":"Articles 53 and 54(1), second subparagraph"},{"t":"Implementing Regulation (EU) 282/2011, consolidated 14 April 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011R0282-20250414","l":"Article 7(3)(j)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-TAX-14","q":"Is the cross-border SME exemption different from OSS?","p":"An EU-established taxable person may use another Member State's small-enterprise exemption if Union annual turnover does not exceed EUR 100,000 and supplies remain within that state's applicable threshold. Prior notification to the establishment state and an individual EX identification number are required. The exemption starts on notification of the number or confirmation of an update. Article 288a also tests the preceding year and ends the cross-border exemption when the Union ceiling is exceeded. Quarterly reports and change notifications apply. Exemption eligibility is distinct from OSS reporting.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"VAT Directive 2006/112/EC, consolidated 14 April 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02006L0112-20250414","l":"Articles 284(2)-(5), 284a-284b, 288a"}],"x":[],"unk":["Country-specific eligibility and transaction treatment need checking."]},{"id":"F-TAX-15","q":"What happens when Croatia's small-taxpayer threshold is crossed?","p":"Croatia's Article 90f ends the domestic exemption from the time its EUR 60,000 threshold is exceeded. Exceeding the domestic threshold in the preceding calendar year prevents exemption for the following calendar year. The cross-border route also tests the EUR 100,000 Union ceiling. Exempt taxpayers may not show VAT on invoices or deduct input VAT. A voluntary election into ordinary taxation binds for the current and following calendar year.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"VAT Act amendments, NN 152/2024","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2024_12_152_2508.html","l":"Article 18, inserted Articles 90f(1)-(4), 90g and 90h"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-TAX-16","q":"How do deposits and unavailable e-invoice addresses affect eRačun?","p":"The Fiscalisation Act's domestic-transaction definition includes advance payments. Where an eRačun cannot be issued because the recipient identifier is unavailable in AMS, Article 40 permits a paper invoice and requires the Article 51 e-reporting route. This is a specific identifier-unavailability fallback, not a general exemption for failed delivery.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o fiskalizaciji, NN 89/2025","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2025_06_89_1233.html","l":"Articles 2(1)(34), 40(1)-(2), 51"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"F-TAX-17","q":"Which enacted OSS implementation phases must be tracked?","p":"Implementing Regulation 2026/1869 amends OSS administrative exchange rules. Its Article 2 applies from 1 January 2027 until 30 June 2028. Articles 1 and 3 apply from 1 July 2028. These implementation phases do not replace the separate ViDA Directive timetable.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2026/1869","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg_impl/2026/1869/oj","l":"Articles 1-4"}],"x":[],"unk":["Detailed administrative message implementation is outside this packet."]},{"id":"F-TAX-18","q":"What evidence supports the customer's location?","p":"Under Article 24b(d), the residual location presumption normally needs two non-contradictory items from Article 24f. A supplier with qualifying supplies from an EU business or fixed establishment not exceeding EUR 100,000 excluding VAT in the current and preceding years may use one listed item supplied by an independent participant in the supply. Specific landline, mobile-network and decoder presumptions apply first. Article 24d permits defined rebuttal and authority challenge. These evidence rules are separate from the EUR 10,000 place-of-supply threshold.","k":"law","j":["EU"],"src":[{"t":"Implementing Regulation (EU) 282/2011, consolidated 14 April 2025","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011R0282-20250414","l":"Articles 24b, 24d, 24f"}],"x":["The EUR 100,000 evidence simplification is not a VAT exemption."],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["This is an accountant intake framework. No operator's residence, legal form, turnover, tax registration or liability has been determined.","EU/EEA status is not an EU VAT-territory test. Non-EU domestic taxes, treaties, customs and permanent-establishment issues are not resolved.","Croatian VAT Article 90 and its post-2024 amendment chain were checked. The complete VAT Act, all rulebooks and all taxes were not audited.","Fiscalization Act scope and phase dates were checked. Detailed technical rules, including NN 11/2026 eRačun rules and later consumer-fiscalisation rule changes, need a separate implementation review.","Croatian English descriptions are unverified researcher translations. The primary Croatian text controls.","Cross-border SME exemption eligibility, customer-location proof, rate selection, platform deemed-supplier rules and goods/import regimes need transaction-specific follow-up.","National ViDA transposition and future amendments must be refreshed before the relevant start date.","Publication approval and qualified legal or tax review have not occurred."]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH","name":"AZOP and breach routes","scope":"HR","summary":"AZOP is Croatia's personal-data protection authority. Use its official contact page for current details and the route matching your enquiry. Rights complaints, DPO appointment notifications and breach reports use distinct processes. Check authority competence for cross-border processing. The new EU cross-border enforcement procedure applies from 2 April 2027. Today's qualifying controller breach notifications still use the GDPR's 72-hour rule.","claims":[{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-01","q":"Which authority supervises privacy in Croatia?","p":"Article 4 of Croatia's GDPR implementing act names Agencija za zaštitu osobnih podataka, known as AZOP, as the Article 51 supervisory authority. It is an independent state body based in Zagreb. AZOP's current official homepage confirms that it supervises GDPR implementation. English rendering of the Croatian sources is an unverified researcher translation.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o provedbi Opće uredbe o zaštiti podataka, NN 42/2018-805","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2018_05_42_805.html","l":"Article 4(1)-(3)"},{"t":"AZOP official homepage","u":"https://azop.hr/","l":"Homepage introduction beneath 'Agencija za zaštitu osobnih podataka'"}],"x":["AZOP is not automatically the authority for every EU website issue"],"unk":["Case-specific competence remains to be assessed"]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-02","q":"What is the Croatian GDPR implementing act?","p":"The act is Zakon o provedbi Opće uredbe o zaštiti podataka, NN 42/2018-805, published on 9 May 2018. Article 1 provides for implementation of Regulation (EU) 2016/679. Article 57 sets entry into force on 25 May 2018. Article 1(2) excludes the listed competent-authority criminal-law processing and national security and defence. This identifies the original act and its commencement, not a certified current consolidation.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o provedbi Opće uredbe o zaštiti podataka, NN 42/2018-805","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2018_05_42_805.html","l":"Publication heading and Articles 1(1)-(2), 57"}],"x":["Do not substitute the repealed pre-GDPR personal-data act","The original publication alone does not prove the absence of later amendments"],"unk":["Full amendment-chain verification is unresolved"]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-03","q":"Where can an operator find current AZOP contact details?","p":"Use AZOP's official contact page at https://azop.hr/kontakt/. On the checked date it lists Ulica Metela Ožegovića 16, HR-10000 Zagreb, azop@azop.hr and telephone +385 1 4609-000. It states that in-person attendance requires a prior appointment. Its helpdesk section is the general GDPR-enquiry route. The agency's own DPO address is not a general external legal-advice service.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Kontakt","u":"https://azop.hr/kontakt/","l":"Sections 'Kontakt', 'Postupak prijema građana / stranaka', 'HELP - DESK' and 'Službenik za zaštitu podataka'"}],"x":["Do not reuse an older postal address from a third-party privacy notice","Do not direct all external questions to AZOP's internal DPO"],"unk":["Contact details may change after 2026-11-07"]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-04","q":"What is the complaint entry point?","p":"Article 34 allows a person who considers a protected right infringed to ask AZOP to determine the infringement. The current contact page links to https://azop.hr/zahtjev-za-utvrdivanje-povrede-prava/. The link was verified, but repeated retrieval of its detailed page timed out. The precise form instructions and attachment requirements therefore remain unverified. The statute provides for an administrative decision and an administrative-court challenge, rather than an administrative appeal.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o provedbi Opće uredbe o zaštiti podataka, NN 42/2018-805","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2018_05_42_805.html","l":"Article 34(1)-(4)"},{"t":"Kontakt","u":"https://azop.hr/kontakt/","l":"Link labelled 'ZAHTJEV ZA UTVRĐIVANJE POVREDE PRAVA'"}],"x":["A data subject complaint is different from a controller's personal-data breach notification"],"unk":["Detailed complaint-page body repeatedly timed out","Do not infer that the complaint route is unavailable","Court filing time limits and procedure were not researched"]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-05","q":"How does AZOP receive DPO appointment notifications?","p":"AZOP's appointment page instructs organisations to submit DPO contact details and appointment decisions only through its web form. It says submissions by post or to azop@azop.hr or registar@azop.hr will not be considered. Changes require completion of the full form again. It separately directs questions about appointment to registar@azop.hr. This is a current administrative instruction, not a claim that every small website must appoint a DPO.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Obrazac za imenovanje službenika za zaštitu podataka","u":"https://azop.hr/obrazac-za-imenovanje-sluzbenika-za-zastitu-podataka/","l":"Notices beginning 'NAPOMENA! Molimo da kontakt podatke' and 'VAŽNO!', before 'OBRAZAC ZA IMENOVANJE SLUŽBENIKA ZA ZAŠTITU PODATAKA'"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 37(1), (4) and (7)"}],"x":["A general email enquiry is not a submitted DPO appointment","An Article 27 representative is a separate role"],"unk":["The form was read but not submitted or functionally tested"]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-06","q":"Where is the controller's breach-reporting entry point?","p":"AZOP's official entry point is https://azop.hr/izvjescivanje-o-povredi-osobnih-podataka/. Its opening guidance describes reporting without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of awareness, unless the breach is unlikely to risk people's rights and freedoms. Article 33(1) requires the competent supervisory authority, so the operator must first establish whether AZOP is the appropriate recipient.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Izvješćivanje o povredi osobnih podataka","u":"https://azop.hr/izvjescivanje-o-povredi-osobnih-podataka/","l":"Heading 'Izvješćivanje o povredi osobnih podataka' and first substantive paragraph"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 33(1)"}],"x":["Not every security incident is a personal-data breach","Do not use a person's rights-complaint form as the controller breach-notification route"],"unk":["Only the entry page and opening guidance were checked","Submission method, form attachments and any subsequent notification updates were not tested"]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-07","q":"Does every site serving a Croatian customer answer only to AZOP?","p":"Do not assume that AZOP is the sole authority because a customer is in Croatia. GDPR Articles 55 and 56 allocate supervisory competence and the lead-authority role for qualifying cross-border processing. Article 77 allows complaints in particular where the person habitually resides, works or says an infringement occurred. EDPB scope guidance says an operator with no EU establishment cannot use the Article 56 one-stop-shop mechanism.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 55(1), 56(1)-(3), 77(1)"},{"t":"Guidelines 3/2018 on the territorial scope of the GDPR","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_3_2018_territorial_scope_after_public_consultation_en_1.pdf","l":"Version 2.1, section 2 introduction, printed p. 13, paragraph beginning 'In this context, the EDPB confirms'"}],"x":["An EU representative alone does not establish a lead-authority entitlement","A Croatian-language notice does not itself allocate exclusive supervisory competence"],"unk":["Lead-authority selection requires a separate factual assessment"]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-M01","q":"Does the new EU procedure change today's Croatian deadlines?","p":"Regulation (EU) 2025/2518 adds procedures for GDPR cross-border enforcement. Adopted on 26 November 2025 and published on 12 December 2025, it entered into force on 1 January 2026 but applies from 2 April 2027. Article 36 applies Chapters III-IV to ex officio investigations opened after that date and complaint-based investigations where the complaint was lodged after that date. Chapters V-VI apply to cases referred to Article 65 dispute resolution or Article 66(2)-(3) urgency procedures after that date. It does not replace controllers' current Article 12 response clock or Article 33 breach deadline.","k":"law","j":["HR","EU"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2025/2518 on additional procedural rules relating to GDPR enforcement","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2025/2518/oj/eng","l":"Publication heading and Articles 1, 36-37"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 12(3)-(4), 33(1)"}],"x":["Do not apply this as a change to today's domestic procedure"],"unk":["Croatian procedural interactions need case-specific assessment"]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-M02","q":"Did P.Z. 421 become an amendment?","p":"Legislative-procedure fact, not operative law: the Croatian Parliament's P.Z. 421 record states that the proposer withdrew this GDPR implementing-act amendment proposal on 15 December 2022. That proposal is not an enacted amendment. Its withdrawal does not establish the complete amendment history of NN 42/2018.","k":"inference","j":["HR","EU"],"src":[{"t":"P.Z. 421, GDPR implementing-act amendment proposal, withdrawal record","u":"https://sabor.hr/hr/sjednice-sabora/prijedlog-zakona-o-izmjeni-zakona-o-provedbi-opce-uredbe-o-zastiti-podataka-s","l":"'Datum povlačenja iz procedure', 15.12.2022, and paragraph beginning 'Dana 15. prosinca 2022'"}],"x":["A legislative-procedure fact, not a new operative duty"],"unk":["The complete Croatian amendment chain remains unresolved"]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-M03","q":"What breach-report delivery steps does AZOP's page currently give?","p":"AZOP's breach page directs use of its linked RTF report and completion of fields marked with an asterisk. It asks for the responsible person's signature and the controller's stamp where applicable, delivery to Ulica Metela Ožegovića 16, 10000 Zagreb, and a scanned copy to incidenti@azop.hr. These are the checked page's administrative instructions.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR","EU"],"src":[{"t":"Izvješćivanje o povredi osobnih podataka","u":"https://azop.hr/izvjescivanje-o-povredi-osobnih-podataka/","l":"Heading 'Dostava izvješća Agenciji', two following paragraphs"}],"x":["No form was submitted or delivery receipt obtained"],"unk":["Linked RTF contents and submission functionality were not inspected"]},{"id":"N-HR-AUTH-M04","q":"What if breach information is incomplete or affected people face high risk?","p":"Article 33 permits information in phases without undue further delay where it cannot be supplied at once. A notification made after 72 hours needs reasons for the delay. Document every personal-data breach, including its facts, effects and remedial action. Article 34 also requires communication to affected people without undue delay when high risk is likely, subject to its exceptions.","k":"law","j":["HR","EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 33(1), 33(4)-(5), 34(1)-(3)"}],"x":["A low-risk notification exception does not remove breach documentation"],"unk":["No actual incident has been assessed"]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["Full Croatian amendment-chain verification is unresolved. NN 42/2018 is the retrieved original act. Do not label it a verified current consolidated text.","The complaint URL was verified on AZOP's contact page, but detailed-page retrieval repeatedly returned a timeout. Form requirements remain a gap, not evidence that filing is impossible.","AZOP's detailed competence page also timed out on re-fetch. Authority identity is supported independently by Article 4 and the current homepage.","All Croatian-to-English explanations are unverified researcher translations. No official English translation was relied upon.","No forms were submitted. No filing, acknowledgement or authority-response process was tested.","National consumer, telecommunications, cookie and marketing enforcement allocation was not fully assessed in this authority task.","The breach-report RTF's contents and submission functionality remain unchecked. Claim M03 describes the opened page instructions only."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO","name":"Consumer info before sale","scope":"HR","summary":"Use Croatia's current Article 60 list before a covered consumer agrees, in Croatian and Latin script. Apply checkout and durable-confirmation duties. Publish the separate complaints notice and operate acknowledgement, 15-day response and one-year record retention. The new website price-list duty starts on 17 November 2026. Its implementing method and the limited-space exception remain unresolved.","claims":[{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO-01","q":"Which Croatian law and amendments were verified?","p":"NN 19/2022 is the Consumer Protection Act. NN 59/2023 repeals its Arts. 114-130. NN 59/2026 replaces Art. 60 and introduces further consumer-law changes.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Heading and Art. 60"},{"t":"Zakon o predstavničkim tužbama za zaštitu kolektivnih interesa i prava potrošača, NN 59/2023, item 998","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2023_06_59_998.html","l":"Arts. 37-38"},{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Arts. 1, 24, 66"}],"x":[],"unk":["This is a provision-level review, not a complete Croatian consumer-law audit."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO-02","q":"What commencement phases matter on 7 November 2026?","p":"NN 59/2026 generally commenced on 17 June 2026. Its listed phases are 19 June, 31 July, 27 September and 17 November. Article 60's withdrawal-function information began on 19 June.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 66, read with publication heading and Arts. 24, 28"}],"x":["The future 17 November phase concerns amended Art. 7(1)-(9), not a general postponement of all consumer duties."],"unk":["Historic transactions need the version applicable on their own date."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO-03","q":"What basic information precedes a distance contract?","p":"Current Art. 60 requires clear pre-contract information about the product, trader, contacts, representative, complaints address, price, payment, delivery and applicable withdrawal rights.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 24 replacing Art. 60(1)(1)-(13)"}],"x":["Distance financial services are excluded from Art. 60 by amended Art. 44(3)."],"unk":["Other Art. 44 exclusions and sector rules need case-specific checks."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO-04","q":"What information is specific to subscriptions and digital supply?","p":"Art. 60 covers subscription costs, duration, cancellation conditions, minimum commitment, digital conformity, functionality and compatibility. Provider-supplied minimum software-update periods became relevant on 27 September 2026.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 24, new Art. 60(1)(15), (18), (21)-(25), 60(3)-(5), Art. 66"}],"x":["Do not invent a provider's promised update period."],"unk":["The actual offer and supplier information must be collected."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO-05","q":"Which goods information changed in September 2026?","p":"Current Art. 60 adds goods conformity and conditional durability, repair and update information. Harmonised notices and labels apply under the specified conditions.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 24, new Art. 60(1)(8), (14), (16)-(19), (27), 60(7)-(8), Art. 66"},{"t":"Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2025/1960","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg_impl/2025/1960/oj","l":"Arts. 1-3 and Annexes I-II"}],"x":["Goods-specific notice rules are not a substitute for digital-service conformity information."],"unk":["Exact Croatian notice artwork and any corrigenda require review before use."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO-06","q":"Which language and format must the information use?","p":"Art. 60(9) requires Croatian in Latin script and permits additional languages. Art. 72(1) requires a format suited to the distance medium and simple, understandable language.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 24, new Art. 60(9)"},{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Art. 72(1), Art. 64"}],"x":["A foreign establishment does not alone resolve applicable language duties."],"unk":["Cross-border language application needs a conflict-of-law check."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO-07","q":"What must happen at ordering and after agreement?","p":"The checkout must clearly communicate the payment obligation. Current Art. 74(1) specifies prominent pre-order fields. Art. 76 requires timely durable contract confirmation.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Art. 74(2)-(4), Arts. 75-76"},{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 27 replacing Art. 74(1), Art. 66"}],"x":["A link to editable website terms alone does not establish delivery of durable confirmation."],"unk":["The actual checkout and confirmation need inspection."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO-08","q":"Do financial services use this general information checklist?","p":"Since 19 June 2026, amended Art. 44(3) excludes distance financial services from specified general provisions, including Art. 60. The dedicated financial-services chapter must be used.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Arts. 19-20 amending Arts. 43-44, Arts. 30-44, Art. 66"}],"x":["Do not apply the general Article 60 checklist to every financial-service contract."],"unk":["The dedicated financial-services information regime is outside this task's detailed extraction."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO-09","q":"What written-complaint notice and handling process is required?","p":"Article 10 requires written-complaint channels at business premises, by post and by email. Optional online channels must preserve communication time and content on a durable medium. Acknowledge receipt in writing without delay. Display a clear, visible and legible complaints notice at the premises and on an established website. Reply in writing within 15 days and state whether the complaint is accepted as justified. Retain complaint records on a durable medium for one year from receipt.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Article 10(1)-(4), (6)-(7)"},{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Article 6"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-INFO-10","q":"When does the new website price-list duty start?","p":"New Consumer Protection Act Article 7(9) requires traders to publish current product price lists on their websites. NN 59/2026 Article 66 starts paragraphs 1-9 on 17 November 2026, so this duty is scheduled rather than operative on 7 November. Article 7(12) delegates the publication method to a ministerial rule. Its exact implementing requirements remain unverified.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Article 4, new Article 7(9),(12), and Article 66"}],"x":[],"unk":["The implementing rule under Article 7(12) was not located in the targeted official search."]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["English descriptions are research translations from Croatian. They are marked unverified_translation and need qualified language/legal review.","The official limited-space rule in amended Art. 72(2) contains specific cross-references. Its consistency with the CRD has not been resolved and is not translated into a simplified exception.","Full sector exclusions, cross-border applicability and national interpretation remain outside this bounded review.","No website, terms, checkout or contract evidence was supplied, so no operator compliance conclusion is possible.","PRICE-LIST-IMPLEMENTATION: The new Article 7(9) start date is confirmed, but the Article 7(12) implementing rule was not established by official searches for cjenici and način objave. Do not invent format, update frequency or exemptions, or treat a search miss as absence of a rule.","M-HR-LIMITED-MEDIUM: Keep amended Article 72(2)'s cross-reference issue blocked for reduced-disclosure templates until compared with current Article 60 and CRD Article 8(4). General disclosure duties remain available."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW","name":"14-day withdrawal","scope":"HR","summary":"A covered consumer can usually withdraw within 14 days. Covered online contracts also need the June 2026 withdrawal function. Starting a service does not alone remove withdrawal, but validly requested partial performance can create a proportionate charge if the information conditions are met. Paid digital content has a separate test. The official digital-content charge instruction and Croatian no-money branch remain public-template blockers.","claims":[{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-01","q":"What is the ordinary Croatian distance-contract withdrawal period?","p":"Art. 79 provides ordinary withdrawal without reasons within 14 days. Services and non-tangible digital content run from conclusion. Goods normally run from receipt, with special rules for split and regular deliveries.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Art. 79(1)-(5)"},{"t":"Withdrawal information and form regulation, NN 105/2026, item 1272","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2026_09_105_1272.html","l":"Annex I, part I.1"}],"x":["Art. 86 exceptions","Financial services use their dedicated regime."],"unk":["An actual deadline requires date and calendar-rule checks."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-02","q":"What happens if withdrawal information is missing?","p":"Art. 80 extends withdrawal until one year after the ordinary period ends. If the information arrives during that year, the consumer has 14 days from receipt.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Art. 80(1)-(2)"}],"x":["An unconditional exception must be checked before assuming a withdrawal right exists."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-03","q":"How does the consumer communicate withdrawal?","p":"Art. 81 permits the form or another unequivocal statement sent before expiry. The consumer bears the proof burden. The online function is an additional route.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Art. 81(1)-(5)"},{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 28 inserting Art. 81a"}],"x":["The trader cannot make the model form the only permitted route."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-04","q":"What online function is required from 19 June 2026?","p":"Art. 81a requires a prominent, accessible withdrawal function during the withdrawal period, followed by confirmation. The trader must promptly acknowledge the content and submission date and time on a durable medium.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 28, inserted Art. 81a(1)-(7), Art. 66"},{"t":"Directive (EU) 2023/2673","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2023/2673/oj/eng","l":"Art. 1(3), CRD Art. 11a"}],"x":["This does not establish ordinary cancellation rights after withdrawal expires."],"unk":["UI implementation needs a separate accessibility and legal wording review."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-05","q":"What are the Croatian function labels?","p":"Art. 81a uses 'raskid ugovora' and 'potvrditi raskid ugovora', or corresponding unambiguous wording. Research translations are 'withdraw from contract' and 'confirm withdrawal'.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 28, inserted Art. 81a(2), (5)"}],"x":[],"unk":["The English translations are not statutory labels or approved UI copy."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-06","q":"How do refund and return mechanics work?","p":"Normally, the trader refunds within 14 days after notice. Goods refunds can await return or evidence of dispatch. The consumer normally sends goods back within 14 days after notice.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Art. 83(1)-(4), Art. 84(1)-(3)"},{"t":"Withdrawal information and form regulation, NN 105/2026, item 1272","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2026_09_105_1272.html","l":"Annex I, part I.2-I.4"}],"x":["Premium delivery above the cheapest offered standard delivery need not be refunded.","Alternative refund method requires express consent and no extra cost.","Return-cost and diminished-value conditions need separate assessment."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-07","q":"Which exception can apply to a paid digital service?","p":"For a paid service, Art. 86(1)(1) requires complete performance, prior express consent and acknowledgement of withdrawal loss on completion. Merely activating a subscription does not establish completion.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Art. 77(1)-(2), Art. 86(1)(1)"},{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Art. 2(6), Art. 16(a)"}],"x":["Special off-premises protection in Art. 86(2)","Digital content requires a different test."],"unk":["Whether performance is complete depends on the contract. Data-only digital-service exceptions need separate Croatian analysis."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-08","q":"How does the paid digital-content exception differ?","p":"Art. 86(1)(13) concerns paid digital content without a physical medium. Performance must begin with prior express consent and acknowledged withdrawal loss, and the trader must provide contract confirmation.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Art. 86(1)(13), Art. 76(2)"},{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 29 replacing Art. 84(10), Art. 66"}],"x":["This is not a general exception for digital services."],"unk":["The data-only content route is not resolved by this paid-content extraction."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-09","q":"Which official Croatian withdrawal instructions are current?","p":"NN 105/2026 item 1272 prescribes the withdrawal instructions and example form. It commenced on 26 September 2026 and includes an online-function location instruction.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Withdrawal information and form regulation, NN 105/2026, item 1272","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2026_09_105_1272.html","l":"Articles 1, 3-5. Annex I part II, penultimate paragraph (mandatory function location) and final paragraph (additional electronic-form option)"}],"x":["Do not copy every option into one undifferentiated form."],"unk":["Part I.6's digital-content wording needs reconciliation before template use."]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-10","q":"Can an early-start service create a proportionate charge after withdrawal?","p":"Under Article 84(8), a consumer who expressly requested early service performance under Articles 68 or 77 must pay proportionately for performance before withdrawal notice. Use the contract price, or market value if excessive. Paragraph 9 excludes the charge if the required withdrawal and cost information was not supplied or an express early-start request was absent. A paid distance-service request must also acknowledge loss after full performance under Article 77(2). Partial performance does not itself remove withdrawal.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Articles 77(1)-(2), 84(8)-(9), 86(1)(1)"},{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Article 24, current Article 60(1)(9)-(12)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-CONSUMER-WITHDRAW-11","q":"Can Croatia's paid-content exception decide no-money contracts?","p":"Croatian Article 86(1)(13) expressly describes non-tangible digital content for which the consumer undertakes to pay a price. CRD Article 16(m) states a separate opening performance condition and attaches further safeguards to paid contracts. These texts must not be treated as interchangeable for a no-money digital-content contract without national interpretation.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Article 86(1)(13)"},{"t":"Directive 2011/83/EU, consolidation of 27 September 2026","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02011L0083-20260927","l":"Article 16(m)"}],"x":[],"unk":["No definitive Croatian no-money withdrawal outcome is supplied."]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["Croatian-to-English descriptions are unverified research translations.","No contract, consent log or confirmation evidence was tested.","Data-only digital contracts, mixed supply, holiday calculation and sector-specific exceptions require further research.","The Croatian Digital Content Directive transposition and its defect-remedy regime are not fully researched in this task.","M-HR-TEMPLATE-CONFLICT: The opened NN 105/2026 Annex I part I.6 includes non-tangible digital content in a proportionate-charge instruction. Its relationship with amended Article 84(10), Article 86(1)(13) and CRD Article 14(4)(b) remains unresolved. Do not generate a general digital-content charge clause."]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT","name":"Cookie consent","scope":"HR","summary":"Where Croatian device-access law applies, obtain consent before non-essential storage or access. The two statutory exceptions concern communication transmission and a service expressly requested by the user. AZOP says non-essential cookies must stay off until consent. Necessary-only sites do not need an acceptance banner but must explain cookie use. Check actual tools, separate purposes and proportionate consent evidence. Territorial application remains a separate question.","claims":[{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-01","q":"What is Croatia's device-access rule?","p":"Article 43(4) requires consent after clear and complete information, particularly about purposes, before network-based storage or access to information on a user's terminal equipment.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Electronic Communications Act NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 43(4), first sentence"},{"t":"ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2002/58","l":"Article 5(3), first sentence"}],"x":["Do not limit the provision to named cookie formats or identifiable data."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-02","q":"What are the exemptions?","p":"Article 43(4) preserves access solely for communication transmission and necessary access to provide an information society service on the user's express request.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Electronic Communications Act NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 43(4), second sentence"},{"t":"ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2002/58","l":"Article 5(3), second sentence"}],"x":["Small-business size and a vendor's essential label do not establish an exemption."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-03","q":"Which amendments were checked?","p":"The checked chain is NN 76/2022, NN 14/2024 and NN 45/2026. The 2024 law repealed Article 41. Article 43(4) remains unchanged by those changes.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Electronic Communications Act NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 43(4) and Article 187"},{"t":"Zakon o kibernetičkoj sigurnosti, NN 14/2024","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2024_02_14_254.html","l":"Article 115(1), third indent, and Article 116"},{"t":"Electronic Communications Act amendment NN 45/2026","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_04_45_523.html","l":"Articles 1-38, especially Articles 10-11 and 38"},{"t":"Zakoni, legislation directory","u":"https://www.hakom.hr/hr/zakoni-2038/2038","l":"Heading Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, entries NN 76/22 i 14/24 and NN 45/26"}],"x":["HAKOM's unofficial 2024 consolidation alone does not include the 2026 law."],"unk":["No later amendment appeared in the targeted official checks. This does not guarantee an exhaustive national corpus."]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-04","q":"When did the checked provisions take effect?","p":"The base Act took effect on 12 July 2022, with separate euro-related provisions delayed. The relevant 2024 repeal took effect on 15 February 2024. The 2026 amendment took effect on 7 May 2026.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Electronic Communications Act NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 187 and publication header, 4 July 2022"},{"t":"Zakon o kibernetičkoj sigurnosti, NN 14/2024","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2024_02_14_254.html","l":"Article 116 and publication header, 7 February 2024"},{"t":"Electronic Communications Act amendment NN 45/2026","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_04_45_523.html","l":"Article 38 and publication header, 29 April 2026"},{"t":"Zakoni, legislation directory","u":"https://www.hakom.hr/hr/zakoni-2038/2038","l":"NN 45/26 entry, parenthetical commencement date"}],"x":["These dates do not imply Article 43(4) was rewritten in 2024 or 2026."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-05","q":"What does AZOP's dated 2026 guidance say?","p":"AZOP says non-essential analytics and marketing cookies must remain inactive until valid consent. Preselected choices, continued browsing, silence and inactivity do not supply consent.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Published 14.8.2026, paragraph beginning Ako se, međutim, koriste analitički and paragraph beginning Nužni kolačići"},{"t":"Planet49, C-673/17","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62017CJ0673","l":"Operative part point 1"}],"x":["A statement in a policy is not proof that the listed tools are installed."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-06","q":"Does a necessary-only site need an acceptance banner?","p":"AZOP's 2026 guidance says a site using only technically necessary cookies does not need a banner asking users to accept cookies. It still requires clear information about their use.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Paragraph beginning Ako internetska stranica koristi isključivo tehnički nužne kolačiće"}],"x":["A necessary-only cookie inventory does not excuse unexamined pixels or other tracking."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-07","q":"How should necessity be judged?","p":"WP29 Opinion 04/2012 assesses strict necessity from the user's requested function. It gives conditional examples including user input, session authentication, user-centred security, requested media playback, load balancing and selected interface settings. Each example has purpose and duration limits. For Croatia, the applicable legal test remains Article 43(4).","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Opinion 04/2012 on Cookie Consent Exemption, WP194","u":"https://ec.europa.eu/justice/article-29/documentation/opinion-recommendation/files/2012/wp194_en.pdf","l":"Sections 2, 3.1-3.7 and 5"},{"t":"Electronic Communications Act NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 43(4), second sentence"}],"x":["Advertising and unrelated tracking do not become necessary through a security or operational label."],"unk":["The full AZOP 2024 exemption-example section could not be retrieved in this revision. No claim is made that AZOP's list was independently verified."]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-08","q":"What inventory does AZOP recommend?","p":"AZOP recommends a technical inventory of installed cookies and tracking tools. Policies must match actual behaviour. The owner retains responsibility when an external designer builds the site.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Paragraphs beginning Ističemo da činjenica, Stoga je prije svega potrebno and Sama tvrdnja, plus numbered recommendations 1-4"}],"x":["No site was inspected in this task."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-09","q":"Does advertising always require a website pixel?","p":"AZOP distinguishes buying Facebook or Meta advertising from adding tracking to a site. An installed Meta Pixel for tracking or conversion purposes generally needs prior consent.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Paragraph beginning Ako u budućnosti budete koristili oglašavanje putem Facebooka and following paragraph"}],"x":["This guidance does not establish any named vendor's GDPR compliance."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-M01","q":"What evidence of cookie consent should be retained?","p":"The controller must be able to demonstrate valid consent. EDPB guidance supports proportionate records linking the person's consent to when and how it was given and the purposes and information presented. Evidence can include the interface and workflow version. Do not collect excessive extra identifying data merely to prove consent.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR","EU"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 7(1)"},{"t":"Guidelines 05/2020 on consent","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_202005_consent_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 104-108, especially 106-108, printed pp. 22-23"}],"x":["A working banner alone is not evidence that this person consented"],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-M02","q":"Should different cookie purposes have separate choices?","p":"EDPB guidance requires separate consent choices for separate processing purposes. AZOP's 2026 page discusses separate category controls and requires policies to match installed tools. Explain the actual controller, purposes, personal data, recipients, storage duration and withdrawal route before activating processing that requires consent.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR","EU"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 05/2020 on consent","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_202005_consent_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 43-45 and 64-65"},{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Paragraphs beginning 'Sukladno AZOP-ovoj Preporuci', 'Sama tvrdnja' and 'Ako se, međutim'"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Articles 7(3), 13(1)-(2)"}],"x":["No detailed attribution to the inaccessible 2024 AZOP information list is made"],"unk":["The 2024 AZOP page could not be fully retrieved"]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-CONSENT-M03","q":"Do first-party or cookieless labels create a Croatian analytics exemption?","p":"No general Croatian analytics exemption was established by the checked sources. Apply Article 43(4)'s storage, access and necessity tests to actual operations. WP29 does not regard first-party analytics as strictly necessary merely because operators value them. A cookieless label does not establish that device access is absent.","k":"inference","j":["HR","EU"],"src":[{"t":"Electronic Communications Act NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 43(4)"},{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Paragraph beginning 'Ako se, međutim'"},{"t":"Opinion 04/2012 on Cookie Consent Exemption, WP194","u":"https://ec.europa.eu/justice/article-29/documentation/opinion-recommendation/files/2012/wp194_en.pdf","l":"Section 4.3, printed pp. 10-11"},{"t":"Guidelines 2/2023 on Technical Scope of Art. 5(3) of ePrivacy Directive","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/2024-10/edpb_guidelines_202302_technical_scope_art_53_eprivacydirective_v2_en_0.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 44 and 52-56"}],"x":["No other country's analytics exception is imported"],"unk":["This is a bounded research conclusion, not an exhaustive Croatian-law audit"]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["All English summaries of Croatian sources are unverified translations. Croatian text controls.","The territorial reach of Article 43(4) for every non-Croatian operator was not established. A Croatian visitor alone is not treated as a completed conflict-of-laws analysis.","The amendment check is limited to this provision. No later amendment was found in official searches and the HAKOM directory, but no full Croatian-law corpus audit was performed.","The base Narodne novine page intermittently timed out. Its ELI route and full-text variant eventually supplied Article 43(4), corroborated by HAKOM's 2024 unofficial text.","HAKOM's available unofficial consolidation stops at NN 14/2024. NN 45/2026 was inspected separately.","No general Croatian audience-measurement exemption beyond the two statutory tests was established. Do not import another country's exception.","No actual site was tested and no independent legal review was requested.","The AZOP 2024 exemption-example section remains inaccessible in this revision. Claim 07 now relies on the opened WP29 Opinion 04/2012 and Croatian Article 43(4), without attributing the example list to AZOP.","Sol-confirmed 2024 AZOP banner and withdrawal material is retained from its review. This revision's direct retry timed out, so it does not supply a fresh full-page receipt."]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW","name":"Reject and withdraw","scope":"HR","summary":"Where Croatian rules apply, let visitors reject non-essential cookies as easily as they accept them. AZOP places both choices on the first banner layer. Keep settings available for later withdrawal and stop processing that relied on the withdrawn consent. Test the actual controls. These findings do not establish Croatian jurisdiction for every foreign operator with a Croatian visitor.","claims":[{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-01","q":"What is the legal basis for withdrawal?","p":"GDPR Article 7(3) gives a right to withdraw consent at any time, as easily as giving it. Inform the person of this right before consent.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 7(3)"},{"t":"Electronic Communications Act NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 43(4) and Article 5(2), as amended by NN 45/2026 Article 3"},{"t":"Electronic Communications Act amendment NN 45/2026","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_04_45_523.html","l":"Article 3, replacement text of Article 5(2)"}],"x":["Withdrawal does not retrospectively make prior lawful consent-based processing unlawful."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-02","q":"Must rejection be as easy as acceptance?","p":"AZOP's 2026 guidance says visitors must be able to accept or reject non-essential cookies equally easily on the first banner layer.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Published 14.8.2026, paragraph beginning Banner koji sadrži samo gumb"},{"t":"Obrada osobnih podataka putem kolačića i pružanje informacija ispitanicima vezano za obradu osobnih podataka","u":"https://azop.hr/obrada-osobnih-podataka-kolacici/","l":"Paragraph beginning Agencija upućuje voditelje obrade and first-layer correct-banner example"}],"x":["Article 7(3) expressly addresses withdrawal. The first-layer rejection instruction is stated here as AZOP guidance."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-03","q":"What does AZOP say about button design?","p":"AZOP says accept and reject options should be displayed and designed equally. Avoid misleading design that steers the user towards acceptance.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Paragraph beginning Sukladno AZOP-ovoj Preporuci"},{"t":"Obrada osobnih podataka putem kolačića i pružanje informacija ispitanicima vezano za obradu osobnih podataka","u":"https://azop.hr/obrada-osobnih-podataka-kolacici/","l":"Paragraph containing Opcije Odbijam kolačiće i Prihvaćam kolačiće, and incorrect-banner examples"}],"x":["The sources do not prescribe an exact universal pixel size or colour code."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-04","q":"What happens if a visitor closes the banner?","p":"AZOP's 2024 recommendation says a close control should let the visitor continue without tracking. The 2026 guidance requires non-essential categories to stay inactive until consent.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Obrada osobnih podataka putem kolačića i pružanje informacija ispitanicima vezano za obradu osobnih podataka","u":"https://azop.hr/obrada-osobnih-podataka-kolacici/","l":"Paragraphs beginning Također, u prvom sloju and U tom slučaju, plus first-layer correct-banner example"},{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Paragraphs beginning Ako se, međutim and Nužni kolačići"}],"x":["Closing is not an affirmative consent choice.","Necessary exempt operations need their own assessment."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-05","q":"How should later withdrawal be made available?","p":"AZOP's recommendation calls for a cookie-settings link in every page footer. Users must be able to withdraw easily at any time. Its 2026 guidance repeats the ease requirement.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Obrada osobnih podataka putem kolačića i pružanje informacija ispitanicima vezano za obradu osobnih podataka","u":"https://azop.hr/obrada-osobnih-podataka-kolacici/","l":"Paragraph beginning Od ključne je važnosti da ispitanik ima u svakom trenutku mogućnost povući svoju privolu"},{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Paragraph beginning Nužni kolačići, final sentence"}],"x":["A browser-settings instruction alone is not the in-site mechanism described by AZOP."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-06","q":"Is a floating icon mandatory?","p":"The EDPB taskforce accepts an easily accessible icon or a visible, consistently placed link. It does not prescribe one specific withdrawal design.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Report of the work undertaken by the Cookie Banner Taskforce","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2023-01/edpb_20230118_report_cookie_banner_taskforce_en.pdf","l":"Section 9, paragraphs 31-35"}],"x":["Read this alongside AZOP's specific footer-link recommendation.","The EU report does not certify every footer link as sufficient."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-07","q":"Can withdrawal require a phone call or email?","p":"EDPB says consent obtained through a service-specific electronic interface should be withdrawable through that interface. Its example rejects a business-hours telephone step after online consent.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 05/2020 on consent","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_202005_consent_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 113-116 and Example 22"}],"x":["The same physical action is not always required, but withdrawal must be equally easy.","Do not impose a new account or contact channel solely to withdraw."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-08","q":"What must happen after withdrawal?","p":"EDPB says to stop the processing that relied on consent. If no other lawful basis justifies keeping the data, delete it. Prior lawful processing remains lawful.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Guidelines 05/2020 on consent","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_202005_consent_en.pdf","l":"Paragraphs 117-120"},{"t":"GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng","l":"Article 7(3) and Article 17(1)(b), subject to Article 17(3)"}],"x":["Withdrawal is not an automatic command to erase every business record.","Legitimate interests cannot simply replace withdrawn device-access consent."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-09","q":"How should the mechanism be tested?","p":"After withdrawal, prevent future non-essential reads, writes and tracking requests for the withdrawn purposes. Coordinate relevant vendors. Test the change instead of only hiding the banner.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Paragraphs beginning Nužni kolačići and Slijedom navedenoga, recommendations 2 and 5"},{"t":"Guidelines 05/2020 on consent","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_202005_consent_en.pdf","l":"Paragraph 117"}],"x":["Browser restrictions may prevent a site directly deleting third-party storage. Future access and processing still need to stop.","A backend flag alone is not proof that client-side collection stopped."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-10","q":"Must rejection or withdrawal block basic content?","p":"AZOP's 2026 guidance says rejecting cookies must not prevent access to the site's basic content and functions. EDPB says withdrawal should occur without detriment.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Paragraph beginning Nužni kolačići, second sentence"},{"t":"Guidelines 05/2020 on consent","u":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/documents/files/file1/edpb_guidelines_202005_consent_en.pdf","l":"Paragraph 114"}],"x":["This is not a full assessment of paid alternatives, large-platform consent-or-pay models or every optional embed."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-COOKIE-WITHDRAW-11","q":"What is the Croatian version check behind this guidance?","p":"The provision-level check found Article 43(4) unchanged across the 2024 and 2026 changes. The 2026 AZOP page links the recommendation updated on 6 August 2024.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Electronic Communications Act NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 43(4) and Article 187"},{"t":"Zakon o kibernetičkoj sigurnosti, NN 14/2024","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2024_02_14_254.html","l":"Article 115(1), third indent, and Article 116"},{"t":"Electronic Communications Act amendment NN 45/2026","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_04_45_523.html","l":"Articles 1-38, especially Articles 10-11 and 38"},{"t":"Zakoni, legislation directory","u":"https://www.hakom.hr/hr/zakoni-2038/2038","l":"Heading Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, entries NN 76/22 i 14/24 and NN 45/26"},{"t":"AZOP cookie guidance","u":"https://azop.hr/kolacici-gdpr/","l":"Publication date 14.8.2026 and hyperlink to the AZOP recommendation"},{"t":"Obrada osobnih podataka putem kolačića i pružanje informacija ispitanicima vezano za obradu osobnih podataka","u":"https://azop.hr/obrada-osobnih-podataka-kolacici/","l":"Page date Ažurirano: 6.kolovoza 2024."}],"x":["This is a scoped provision and guidance check, not a complete national-law review."],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["All English summaries of Croatian materials are unverified translations. Croatian text controls.","The territorial reach of Croatian rules to every operator outside Croatia remains unassessed.","No banner, consent platform, vendor withdrawal API or live website was tested.","The exact cookie deletion and vendor notification steps depend on the implementation. Browser limitations do not establish that continued tracking is lawful.","AZOP's 2024 page includes descriptive wording about browsing-based consent in its discussion of existing banner practices. Do not lift that wording into an affirmative consent rule. Its detailed instructions and the 2026 page require an affirmative choice.","No fixed universal renewal period, button dimensions or mandatory floating-icon rule was established.","The version check is provision-specific. A full Croatian consolidation and any later developments need checking before a later publication.","No independent legal review was requested.","Sol confirmed the 2024 AZOP withdrawal paragraphs. This revision retained those findings, but its direct retrieval of that page timed out. No fresh full-page retrieval is claimed."]},{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE","name":"Accessibility act (NN 89/2025)","scope":"HR","summary":"Croatia's NN 89/2025 accessibility law applies from 28 June 2025 and its October 2026 amendment is in force. Consumer e-commerce can be covered, with a qualifying service microenterprise exemption. The contract transition concerns contracts concluded before 28 June 2025. Covered providers need accessible compliance information and ongoing correction and notification. State Inspectorate market inspectors supervise e-commerce services.","claims":[{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE-01","q":"Is NN 89/2025 the Croatian EAA transposition?","p":"Yes. NN 89/2025 item 1231 is the Accessibility Requirements Act. Article 2 transposes Directive 2019/882. Article 38 sets commencement at 28 June 2025.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zahtjevima za pristupačnost proizvoda i usluga, NN 89/2025, item 1231","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2025_06_89_1231.html","l":"Publication heading, Arts. 2 and 38"},{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Art. 31(2)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE-02","q":"Does NN 114/2026 exist and what does it amend?","p":"NN 114/2026 item 1371 amends NN 89/2025. It commenced on 15 October 2026. It renumbers definitions and changes compliance and enforcement provisions, without replacing the e-commerce scope or service exemption.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Accessibility Requirements Act amendment, NN 114/2026, item 1371","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_10_114_1371.html","l":"Heading, Arts. 1-13, especially Arts. 1 and 13"},{"t":"Zakon o zahtjevima za pristupačnost proizvoda i usluga, NN 89/2025, item 1231","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2025_06_89_1231.html","l":"Arts. 3(2), 4(2)"}],"x":[],"unk":["No exhaustive review of subordinate measures is claimed."]},{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE-03","q":"Which Croatian services include e-commerce?","p":"Art. 3(2) covers listed consumer services, including e-commerce. The e-commerce definition concerns remote website or mobile services requested by a consumer to conclude a consumer contract.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zahtjevima za pristupačnost proizvoda i usluga, NN 89/2025, item 1231","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2025_06_89_1231.html","l":"Art. 3(2), Art. 5 point 25 in original text"},{"t":"Accessibility Requirements Act amendment, NN 114/2026, item 1371","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_10_114_1371.html","l":"Art. 1, final sentence"}],"x":["B2B-only transactions do not meet this consumer-contract definition."],"unk":["Enquiry and booking boundaries need facts and Croatian interpretation."]},{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE-04","q":"Which Croatian microenterprise service exemption applies?","p":"Art. 4(2) exempts microenterprises providing services. Current Art. 5 point 22 requires average annual employment below 10 and the statutory prior-year revenue or asset threshold up to EUR 2 million.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zahtjevima za pristupačnost proizvoda i usluga, NN 89/2025, item 1231","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2025_06_89_1231.html","l":"Art. 4(2), original Art. 5 point 18"},{"t":"Accessibility Requirements Act amendment, NN 114/2026, item 1371","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_10_114_1371.html","l":"Art. 1, final sentence"}],"x":["Product obligations are not automatically exempt.","Exactly 10 average workers does not satisfy below 10."],"unk":["The statute distinguishes total assets for profit-tax payers from long-term assets for income-tax payers. Counting and related-enterprise treatment need review."]},{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE-05","q":"What transitional provisions did Croatia adopt?","p":"Article 37 allows limited continued product use until 28 June 2030 and unchanged service contracts concluded before 28 June 2025 until expiry, capped at five years from that date. Qualifying existing self-service terminals have a separate useful-life limit capped at 20 years after entry into use.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zahtjevima za pristupačnost proizvoda i usluga, NN 89/2025, item 1231","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2025_06_89_1231.html","l":"Art. 37(1)-(3)"},{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Art. 32(1)-(2)"}],"x":["No blanket exemption for an older active website."],"unk":["Whether a particular renewal changes an old contract needs review."]},{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE-06","q":"Which content exclusions can affect a Croatian site?","p":"Art. 4(1) excludes specified older media and office files, qualifying maps, uncontrolled third-party content and qualifying archives. Each exclusion has its own conditions.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zahtjevima za pristupačnost proizvoda i usluga, NN 89/2025, item 1231","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2025_06_89_1231.html","l":"Art. 4(1), five indents"}],"x":["Outsourcing alone does not prove the third-party-content exclusion."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE-07","q":"What duties remain for a covered non-exempt service?","p":"The Act requires accessible service delivery and compliance information. Annex I covers websites, support and e-commerce identification, security and payment. Art. 17 separately governs fundamental alteration and disproportionate burden.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zahtjevima za pristupačnost proizvoda i usluga, NN 89/2025, item 1231","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2025_06_89_1231.html","l":"Arts. 6, 15, 17, Annex I section III and IV(g), Annex V"},{"t":"Accessibility Requirements Act amendment, NN 114/2026, item 1371","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_10_114_1371.html","l":"Art. 12(1) correcting Annex I III(d)"}],"x":["An accessibility widget or statement alone does not prove compliance."],"unk":["The actual service has not been assessed."]},{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE-08","q":"Does Croatian law directly mandate one WCAG edition?","p":"Art. 18 links presumption of conformity to harmonised standards or technical specifications. A specific WCAG edition cannot be treated as the entire Croatian statutory test.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zahtjevima za pristupačnost proizvoda i usluga, NN 89/2025, item 1231","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2025_06_89_1231.html","l":"Art. 18(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Directive (EU) 2019/882","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng","l":"Art. 15(1)"}],"x":["Do not transfer a public-sector WAD citation to EAA without checking it."],"unk":["Current OJ citation status remains unresolved in F-EAA."]},{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE-09","q":"What ongoing accessibility information and correction duties apply?","p":"A covered provider must prepare Annex V compliance information and make it publicly available in written and oral forms accessible to disabled people. Keep it throughout service operation. Maintain conformity procedures that account for service, legal, standard and specification changes. Correct non-conformity and immediately notify competent authorities in each Member State where the service is provided, with details of non-compliance and measures taken.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zahtjevima za pristupačnost proizvoda i usluga, NN 89/2025, item 1231","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2025_06_89_1231.html","l":"Article 15(2)-(5), Annex V"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-EAA-SCOPE-10","q":"Who supervises Croatian e-commerce accessibility?","p":"Article 25(9) assigns e-commerce service inspection to the State Inspectorate's market inspectors. The October 2026 amendment requires accessible information about the authorities and permits specialist assistance when the inspector lacks expertise or equipment. Under paragraph 12, the Inspectorate pays examination costs if the service complies, and the operator pays if it does not.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zahtjevima za pristupačnost proizvoda i usluga, NN 89/2025, item 1231","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2025_06_89_1231.html","l":"Article 25(9)"},{"t":"Accessibility Requirements Act amendment, NN 114/2026, item 1371","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_10_114_1371.html","l":"Article 4, inserting Article 25(10)-(12), Article 13"}],"x":[],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["English descriptions are unverified translations of Croatian legislation.","Subordinate standards, enterprise-counting guidance, group structures and Croatian enforcement interpretation have not been fully reviewed.","Current EN 301 549 EAA citation status is unresolved. See F-EAA.","The review does not cover all Croatian disability, equality, public-sector or procurement requirements.","No real enterprise figures or website were assessed."]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-B2B","name":"Email to companies and obrt","scope":"HR","summary":"Classify the recipient before relying on Croatia's legal-person carve-out. A sole trader remains a natural person. Article 50(3) identity, electronic-commerce, linked-site and free cessation safeguards survive the carve-out. Named employee addresses can involve personal data and related notice duties. Their recipient classification needs Croatian legal review. The 2019 HAKOM example is historical.","claims":[{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-B2B-01","q":"What does the legal-person carve-out cover?","p":"Article 50(4) excludes communications to legal persons from paragraphs 1 and 2. Paragraph 3 still governs sender identity and a free cessation address. B2B is therefore not a sufficient recipient classification.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(3)-(4)"}],"x":["No exemption from all marketing or data-protection rules"],"unk":["Named employee mailboxes require classification"]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-B2B-02","q":"Is an obrtnik a legal person merely because the message is professional?","p":"Article 4 of the Obrt Act defines an obrtnik as a natural person. A professional purpose does not by itself create legal personality. Article 2(2) separately recognises a legal person carrying on craft activity. Check the recipient's registered form.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o obrtu, NN 143/2013, item 3065","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2013_12_143_3065.html","l":"Articles 2(1)-(2), 4"},{"t":"Obrt amendments, NN 127/2019","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2019_12_127_2560.html","l":"Articles 1-3"},{"t":"Correction to obrt amendments, NN 41/2020","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2020_04_41_880.html","l":"Sole correction"},{"t":"Obrt Act amendment, NN 114/2026, item 1370","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2026_10_114_1370.html","l":"Articles 1, 44"}],"x":["No assumption that every craft activity is operated by a natural person"],"unk":["Specific foreign sole-trader forms require local verification"]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-B2B-03","q":"Does listing a personal address in a business directory make it a corporate recipient?","p":"HAKOM rejected that reasoning in its 4 March 2019 decision about a personal address in a freelance directory. This is a historical enforcement example under former Article 107, not a ruling on every employee mailbox under current Article 50.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"HAKOM inspector decision, 4 March 2019, UP/I-344-07/19-01/03","u":"https://www.hakom.hr/UserDocsImages/2019/odluke_rjesenja_presude/Rje%C5%A1enje%20inspektora%20za%20D.N.%20d.o.o%20oko%20ne%C5%BEeljene%20komunikacije.pdf","l":"Pages 2-3, reasoning on freelance-directory listing"}],"x":["Not a general judicial precedent","Not a current-law employee-mailbox ruling"],"unk":["Final appeal outcome not checked"]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-B2B-04","q":"How should named employee addresses be classified?","p":"A named employee address can be personal data even when used for work. Identify separately whether the message is addressed to the company or the individual. Neither a corporate domain nor a personal name alone settles Article 50(4).","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Article 4(1), Articles 6 and 21(2)-(3)"},{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(1),(4)"}],"x":["No blanket rule that all named corporate mailboxes require consent","No blanket rule that all company-domain mailboxes are exempt"],"unk":["No current, decisive HAKOM/AZOP source resolving these mailbox variants was retrieved"]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-B2B-05","q":"Which information and objection notices are needed?","p":"Where GDPR applies, provide Article 13 information when collecting data directly. For indirectly obtained data, Article 14 normally requires information within one month, or by an earlier first communication or disclosure. Apply the statutory exceptions, including information already held. Draw attention to the Article 21 marketing-objection right clearly and separately by the first communication. Assess Article 95 for the processing concerned.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Articles 13(1)-(4), 14(1)-(5), 21(2)-(4), 95"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-B2B-06","q":"Which safeguards survive a sending exception?","p":"Article 50(3) prohibits misleading or concealed sender identity, electronic-commerce non-compliance, messages without a valid free cessation email address or number, and messages encouraging visits to websites contrary to electronic-commerce rules. Article 50(4) excludes legal-person communications only from paragraphs 1 and 2, so paragraph 3 remains relevant.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(3)-(4)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-B2B-07","q":"What can the historical directory decision establish?","p":"The 4 March 2019 HAKOM inspector decision rejected the assumption that listing a personal address in a freelance directory made it a legal-person recipient. It is an individual administrative decision under former Article 107, not current general guidance deciding all employee mailboxes. The appeal outcome was not checked.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"HAKOM inspector decision, 4 March 2019, UP/I-344-07/19-01/03","u":"https://www.hakom.hr/UserDocsImages/2019/odluke_rjesenja_presude/Rje%C5%A1enje%20inspektora%20za%20D.N.%20d.o.o%20oko%20ne%C5%BEeljene%20komunikacije.pdf","l":"Heading and printed pages 2-3, especially inspector's conclusion on page 3"}],"x":["No general current employee-mailbox classification."],"unk":["Appeal outcome."]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["Current employee-mailbox classification needs Croatian legal review. The historical HAKOM decision does not settle it.","The legal-person carve-out is a Croatia-specific implementation, not a rule for all EU countries.","The October 2026 Obrt amendment is enacted but future-dated to 1 January 2027.","Croatian source translations are unverified. No person-specific recipient classification is offered."]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-CUSTOMER","name":"The customer exception","scope":"HR","summary":"The customer exception requires lawful acquisition, a qualifying sale context and the sender's own similar offerings. Give a free, easy objection at collection and in every email, and respect an initial refusal. Message safeguards and applicable privacy notices remain relevant. Croatia's consumer wording and the scope of AZOP's recommendation alongside Inteligo remain interpretation or source-reconciliation gaps.","claims":[{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-CUSTOMER-01","q":"What must the sender establish for the customer exception?","p":"Article 50(2) permits the trader to use addresses obtained from its own consumers for a sale, only for its own similar offerings. Consumers need a clear, easy, free objection at collection and in every message, with no prior refusal.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(2)"},{"t":"Directive 2002/58/EC, consolidated 19 December 2009","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02002L0058-20091219","l":"Article 13(2)"}],"x":["Purchased lists","Third-party offers"],"unk":["Similarity and the relationship must be established"]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-CUSTOMER-02","q":"Can the Croatian wording be expanded to every business customer?","p":"The Croatian text uses potrošači, meaning consumers, rather than a general business-contact category. Its consumer definition excludes business, craft and professional purposes. Do not automatically apply this exception to every professional individual.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(2), Article 5(1)(54) in base text"},{"t":"Telecom amendments, NN 45/2026","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_04_45_523.html","l":"Article 3, renumbering former points 50-75 to 52-77"},{"t":"Directive 2002/58/EC, consolidated 19 December 2009","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02002L0058-20091219","l":"Article 13(2)"}],"x":["No blanket professional-customer exception"],"unk":["Reconciliation of national consumer wording with the Directive needs Croatian legal review"]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-CUSTOMER-03","q":"What does Inteligo change in this assessment?","p":"Inteligo interprets the EU exception to include its specific free publishing account. It also excludes an additional Article 6(1) assessment for processing covered by a compliant Article 13(2) communication. Application to another Croatian service needs a factual comparison.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"CJEU, Inteligo Media, C-654/23, 13 November 2025, ECLI:EU:C:2025:871","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62023CJ0654","l":"Paragraphs 53-69 and operative points 1-2"},{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(2)"}],"x":["No blanket permission for free downloads or all GDPR processing"],"unk":["No post-Inteligo Croatian authority guidance settling the specific edge cases was found"]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-CUSTOMER-04","q":"Which information and objection notices are needed?","p":"Where GDPR applies, provide Article 13 information when collecting data directly. For indirectly obtained data, Article 14 normally requires information within one month, or by an earlier first communication or disclosure. Apply the statutory exceptions, including information already held. Draw attention to the Article 21 marketing-objection right clearly and separately by the first communication. Assess Article 95 for the processing concerned.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Articles 13(1)-(4), 14(1)-(5), 21(2)-(4), 95"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-CUSTOMER-05","q":"Which safeguards survive a sending exception?","p":"Article 50(3) prohibits misleading or concealed sender identity, electronic-commerce non-compliance, messages without a valid free cessation email address or number, and messages encouraging visits to websites contrary to electronic-commerce rules. Article 50(4) excludes legal-person communications only from paragraphs 1 and 2, so paragraph 3 remains relevant.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(3)-(4)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-CUSTOMER-06","q":"Must acquisition itself be lawful?","p":"The EU customer exception requires the contact details to have been obtained lawfully under applicable data-protection law. Inteligo separates that acquisition from the covered sending operation. It does not retrospectively cure unlawful collection.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Directive 2002/58/EC, consolidated 19 December 2009","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02002L0058-20091219","l":"Article 13(2)"},{"t":"CJEU, Inteligo Media, C-654/23, 13 November 2025, ECLI:EU:C:2025:871","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:62023CJ0654","l":"Paragraphs 38-39, 48, 64-69"}],"x":[],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["Croatian Article 50(2) uses consumer wording. Treatment of professional natural-person customers needs legal interpretation.","No retrieved AZOP or HAKOM guidance applies Inteligo to Croatian free-account examples.","No universal time limit for an existing-customer relationship was established. Avoid inventing a six-month or two-year rule.","Croatian passages are unverified researcher translations. The shared current-law chain is recorded in every claim.","HR-CUSTOMER-M1: AZOP's recommendation landing page opened, but attempts to read its Izravni marketing passage timed out. Reconcile its exact legitimate-interest wording with Inteligo paragraphs 68-69 before claiming the authority positions align. The CJEU holding does not authorise unrelated processing. Official URL: https://azop.hr/preporuka-za-provedbu-testa-legitimnog-interesa-prema-clanku-6-stavku-1-tocki-f-opce-uredbe-o-zastiti-podataka/."]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-NATURAL","name":"Email to individuals","scope":"HR","summary":"Natural-person marketing recipients generally need prior consent, subject to the conditional customer exception. Publishing an address alone does not establish that consent. Check identity, electronic-commerce and linked-site safeguards, a free cessation channel and applicable GDPR information and objection notices.","claims":[{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-NATURAL-01","q":"What is the starting rule for an individual recipient?","p":"Article 50(1) requires prior consent for email direct promotion and sales. It includes SMS and MMS. The customer exception and legal-person carve-out must be assessed separately.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(1),(2),(4)"}],"x":["Article 50(2) qualifying customer emails","Article 50(4) legal-person recipients"],"unk":["Cross-border applicability"]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-NATURAL-02","q":"Does HAKOM confirm the consumer-facing rule?","p":"HAKOM's consumer guidance states that direct promotion by email requires prior consent and a free way to stop further messages. Read that short guidance alongside Article 50's exceptions.","k":"official_guidance","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"HAKOM, Zlonamjerne i neželjene EK","u":"https://www.hakom.hr/hr/zlonamjerne-i-nezeljene-ek/11398","l":"Neželjene elektroničke komunikacije, first two paragraphs"},{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(2)-(4)"}],"x":["Guidance is not a complete statutory statement"],"unk":["Page publication date"]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-NATURAL-03","q":"Which amendment chain supports Article 50?","p":"The checked 2024 and 2026 amendments leave Article 50 unchanged. NN 14/2024 repeals Article 41. NN 45/2026 renumbers definitions, including the consumer definition from Article 5(1)(54) to 5(1)(56).","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(1)-(4)"},{"t":"Zakon o kibernetičkoj sigurnosti, NN 14/2024","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2024_02_14_254.html","l":"Articles 115(1), 116"},{"t":"Telecom amendments, NN 45/2026","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_04_45_523.html","l":"Articles 3, 10-13, 38"},{"t":"HAKOM laws register","u":"https://www.hakom.hr/hr/zakoni-2038/2038","l":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama list"}],"x":["Historical Article 107 is not the current locator"],"unk":["No later amendment found is a bounded search result, not a completeness guarantee"]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-NATURAL-04","q":"Which information and objection notices are needed?","p":"Where GDPR applies, provide Article 13 information when collecting data directly. For indirectly obtained data, Article 14 normally requires information within one month, or by an earlier first communication or disclosure. Apply the statutory exceptions, including information already held. Draw attention to the Article 21 marketing-objection right clearly and separately by the first communication. Assess Article 95 for the processing concerned.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Articles 13(1)-(4), 14(1)-(5), 21(2)-(4), 95"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-NATURAL-05","q":"Which safeguards survive a sending exception?","p":"Article 50(3) prohibits misleading or concealed sender identity, electronic-commerce non-compliance, messages without a valid free cessation email address or number, and messages encouraging visits to websites contrary to electronic-commerce rules. Article 50(4) excludes legal-person communications only from paragraphs 1 and 2, so paragraph 3 remains relevant.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkim komunikacijama, NN 76/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_07_76_1116.html","l":"Article 50(3)-(4)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-EMAIL-NATURAL-06","q":"Does publishing an email address establish consent?","p":"A publicly listed address alone does not demonstrate the freely given, specific, informed and unambiguous affirmative consent required by GDPR Article 4(11). Check actual permission and any applicable statutory exception.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679, consolidated text","u":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:02016R0679-20160504","l":"Article 4(11), Article 7(1)"},{"t":"HAKOM inspector decision, 4 March 2019, UP/I-344-07/19-01/03","u":"https://www.hakom.hr/UserDocsImages/2019/odluke_rjesenja_presude/Rje%C5%A1enje%20inspektora%20za%20D.N.%20d.o.o%20oko%20ne%C5%BEeljene%20komunikacije.pdf","l":"Printed page 3, freelance-directory reasoning"}],"x":["This is not a binding classification of every public professional mailbox."],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["Cross-border choice-of-law analysis is not completed.","All English descriptions of Croatian sources are researcher translations, not verified legal translations.","Consent evidence, message content and actual recipient status remain unknown for every operator.","Later-amendment search was bounded to the official sources and research date. Qualified legal review remains unrequested."]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO","name":"Trader info on your site","scope":"HR","summary":"Start with legal form and establishment. Croatian online providers have Electronic Commerce Act identity and order-flow duties. Consumer sales add information and a separate complaints notice. The d.o.o. disclosure fields now have a bounded official amendment-chain check. The Obrt incorporation text is checked, but its exact website effect remains unresolved. The new price-list duty starts on 17 November 2026, with implementing details still open.","claims":[{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-01","q":"Which identification disclosures does the Electronic Commerce Act require?","p":"Art. 6 requires directly and permanently accessible provider identity, obrt or company seat, effective contact including email, and applicable registration, supervisory, professional and VAT details.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkoj trgovini, NN 173/2003, item 2504","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2003_10_173_2504.html","l":"Art. 6(1), seven disclosure groups"}],"x":["The VAT-number limb is conditional.","This provision is not limited to consumer sales."],"unk":["Territorial application must be resolved for foreign providers."]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-02","q":"What must displayed prices explain?","p":"Art. 6(2) requires clear prices and disclosure of whether delivery, handling, tax and other price-affecting costs are included.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkoj trgovini, NN 173/2003, item 2504","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2003_10_173_2504.html","l":"Art. 6(2)"},{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 24, new Art. 60(1)(5), 60(3)-(6)"}],"x":["This does not replace tax or invoice rules."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-03","q":"Did Croatia's 2025 DSA law remove these disclosures?","p":"NN 67/2025 repealed Electronic Commerce Act Arts. 16, 17, 18 and 21. It did not repeal Art. 6.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Croatian Digital Services Act implementation law, NN 67/2025, item 857","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2025_04_67_857.html","l":"Arts. 25(1), 26"},{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkoj trgovini, NN 173/2003, item 2504","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2003_10_173_2504.html","l":"Art. 6"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-04","q":"What does the Obrt Act expressly require?","p":"Art. 19 defines the obrt business name, including the proprietor's name and seat, and requires display at the specified business locations. Paragraph 6 applies company-name provisions correspondingly.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o obrtu, NN 143/2013, item 3065","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2013_12_143_3065.html","l":"Art. 19(1)-(6)"},{"t":"Obrt amendments, NN 127/2019","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2019_12_127_2560.html","l":"Amending provisions"},{"t":"Correction to obrt amendments, NN 41/2020","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2020_04_41_880.html","l":"Sole correction to Article 88a(2)"},{"t":"Obrt Act amendment, NN 114/2026, item 1370","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2026_10_114_1370.html","l":"Articles 1-44, particularly 44"}],"x":["The express physical-display clause is not, by itself, a complete website checklist."],"unk":["The scope of corresponding Companies Act application to an obrt website needs qualified review."]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-05","q":"Does the 2026 obrt amendment itself impose a new website identity list?","p":"NN 114/2026 item 1370 schedules new registration data in Art. 15 from 1 January 2027. Those prospective fields do not themselves establish a website-display list.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Obrt Act amendment, NN 114/2026, item 1370","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2026_10_114_1370.html","l":"Arts. 4 and 44"},{"t":"Zakon o obrtu, NN 143/2013, item 3065","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2013_12_143_3065.html","l":"Art. 19"}],"x":[],"unk":["Any website duty must be traced to an applicable disclosure provision."]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-06","q":"What does the official Companies Act text require on a d.o.o. website?","p":"For a d.o.o., Companies Act Article 21(4)-(5) requires its website to state the company name and seat, registry court and registration number, account-holding institutions' names and seats and account numbers, share capital and whether fully paid, any unpaid portion, and each management-board member's surname and at least one given name. The checked post-2011 amendment chain preserves this provision.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o trgovačkim društvima, official consolidated text, NN 152/2011, item 3144","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/full/2011_12_152_3144.html","l":"Article 21(4), (5)(1) and (3)"},{"t":"Justice Ministry Companies Act register","u":"https://mpudt.gov.hr/zakon-o-trgovackim-drustvima/6631?impaired=0","l":"Companies Act amendment list through NN 136/2024"},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 111/2012","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2012_10_111_2392.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 68/2013","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2013_06_68_1348.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 110/2015","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2015_10_110_2127.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 40/2019","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2019_04_40_816.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 34/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_03_34_398.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 114/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_10_114_1712.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 18/2023","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2023_02_18_305.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 130/2023","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2023_11_130_1794.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 136/2024","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2024_11_136_2248.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."}],"x":["The issued-share field in Art. 21(5)(2) concerns a d.d., not a d.o.o."],"unk":["Other company forms and case-specific website compliance remain outside this d.o.o. finding."]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-07","q":"Which rule supplies the additional consumer phone and complaints details?","p":"For covered consumer distance contracts, current Consumer Protection Act Art. 60 adds telephone, email, representative and complaints-address information. Electronic Commerce Act disclosures do not replace it.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Art. 24, new Art. 60(1)(2)-(4)"},{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkoj trgovini, NN 173/2003, item 2504","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2003_10_173_2504.html","l":"Art. 6(1)"}],"x":["Financial-service and other scope exclusions need separate routing."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-08","q":"Can Croatian trader rules simply be applied to every overseas seller?","p":"The Electronic Commerce Act distinguishes Croatian establishment from providers established in another Member State, with exceptions. Foreign-provider scope and consumer contractual obligations must be assessed separately.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkoj trgovini, NN 173/2003, item 2504","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2003_10_173_2504.html","l":"Arts. 3-4"},{"t":"Electronic Commerce Act amendment, NN 36/2009, item 796","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2009_03_36_796.html","l":"Art. 1 replacing Art. 4(2)"},{"t":"Electronic Commerce Act amendment, NN 67/2008, item 2228","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2008_06_67_2228.html","l":"Art. 3 inserting Art. 5a"}],"x":["No blanket all-world Croatian disclosure conclusion."],"unk":["Third-country scope, Rome I and enforcement require a separate legal analysis."]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-09","q":"What written-complaint notice and handling process is required?","p":"Article 10 requires written-complaint channels at business premises, by post and by email. Optional online channels must preserve communication time and content on a durable medium. Acknowledge receipt in writing without delay. Display a clear, visible and legible complaints notice at the premises and on an established website. Reply in writing within 15 days and state whether the complaint is accepted as justified. Retain complaint records on a durable medium for one year from receipt.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o zaštiti potrošača, NN 19/2022, item 203","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_02_19_203.html","l":"Article 10(1)-(4), (6)-(7)"},{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Article 6"}],"x":[],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-10","q":"When does the new website price-list duty start?","p":"New Consumer Protection Act Article 7(9) requires traders to publish current product price lists on their websites. NN 59/2026 Article 66 starts paragraphs 1-9 on 17 November 2026, so this duty is scheduled rather than operative on 7 November. Article 7(12) delegates the publication method to a ministerial rule. Its exact implementing requirements remain unverified.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o izmjenama i dopunama Zakona o zaštiti potrošača, NN 59/2026, item 728","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2026_06_59_728.html","l":"Article 4, new Article 7(9),(12), and Article 66"}],"x":[],"unk":["The implementing rule under Article 7(12) was not located in the targeted official search."]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-11","q":"What supports the current d.o.o. disclosure chain?","p":"The Ministry register lists the Companies Act amendments through NN 136/2024. The opened nine post-2011 instruments do not change principal Act Article 21(4)-(5). References in NN 18/2023 and 130/2023 to Article 21 concern the separate NN 114/2022 capital-conversion transition. The resulting disclosure finding is bounded to the cited fields and research date.","k":"inference","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Justice Ministry Companies Act register","u":"https://mpudt.gov.hr/zakon-o-trgovackim-drustvima/6631?impaired=0","l":"Companies Act amendment list"},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 111/2012","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2012_10_111_2392.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 68/2013","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2013_06_68_1348.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 110/2015","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2015_10_110_2127.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 40/2019","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2019_04_40_816.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 34/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_03_34_398.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 114/2022","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2022_10_114_1712.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 18/2023","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2023_02_18_305.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 130/2023","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2023_11_130_1794.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."},{"t":"Companies Act amendment NN 136/2024","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2024_11_136_2248.html","l":"Amending provisions affecting the principal Act. For NN 18/2023 and 130/2023, distinguish amendments to NN 114/2022 Article 21."}],"x":["No claim of an exhaustive whole-act or case-law audit."],"unk":[]},{"id":"N-HR-TRADER-INFO-12","q":"What must the Croatian electronic-order flow provide?","p":"Electronic Commerce Act Article 12 requires clear pre-contract steps, terms, languages and codes information and input-error correction. Article 13 requires storable and reproducible terms. Article 14 requires electronic acknowledgement without delay of a message containing an offer or acceptance. Non-consumer parties can agree derogations from Article 12(1)-(2) and Article 14(1), not from Article 13. For contracts concluded only by individual email or equivalent communication, Article 12(4) excludes Article 12(1)-(3) and Article 14(3) excludes Article 14(1)-(2). The Article 13 duty to provide storable and reproducible terms still applies. The 2008 amendment removed the word 'expressly' from those B2B derogations.","k":"law","j":["HR"],"src":[{"t":"Zakon o elektroničkoj trgovini, NN 173/2003, item 2504","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2003_10_173_2504.html","l":"Articles 12(1)-(4), 13 and 14(1)-(3)"},{"t":"Electronic Commerce Act amendment, NN 67/2008, item 2228","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2008_06_67_2228.html","l":"Articles 6-7"},{"t":"Croatian Digital Services Act implementation law, NN 67/2025, item 857","u":"https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2025_04_67_857.html","l":"Article 25(1)"}],"x":[],"unk":[]}],"notApply":[{},{},{},{},{},{}],"gaps":["English translations are unverified research translations.","Tax identifiers, fiscalisation, invoices, sector licences and beneficial ownership were not mapped as website duties.","No operator identity or actual website was supplied for verification.","Cross-border applicability for providers outside Croatia remains conditional.","PRICE-LIST-IMPLEMENTATION: The new Article 7(9) start date is confirmed, but the Article 7(12) implementing rule was not established by official searches for cjenici and način objave. Do not invent format, update frequency or exemptions, or treat a search miss as absence of a rule.","The Obrt amendment chain is checked, but Article 19(6)'s exact corresponding application to an obrt website remains a legal-interpretation gap. Do not transplant d.o.o. capital and management disclosures to a sole trader."]}];
